For more help, type 'help <topic>' for any command, skill, or spell.
Type 'help KEYWORD' to see a list of all of the available topics.
Also help on: DAMAGE DYING EXPERIENCE NEWS STORY TICK WIZLIST GUILDS
If you are new here, we recommend that you read BEGINNERS.

The COMMANDS topic shows only the commands that your current trust level
allows you to use.  Mortals and heroes will see only mortal commands here.
Immortal-only commands are documented under WIZHELP and will not appear to
non-immortals.

See also: COMMANDS, WIZHELP
~

0 MAP MAPPER AUTOMAP AUTOMAPPER MAPPING~
Times of Chaos has an automatic client-side map in its official Mudlet
interface.  There is no MAP game command: the mapper is a window in Mudlet
that follows your character as you move.

The server supplies a complete world atlas, so the surrounding rooms are
already drawn before you take a step.  Rooms you visit refresh themselves from
GMCP Room.Info data: name, area, terrain, and visible directional exits.  If a
visible exit is removed or points somewhere new after a world update, the
saved map corrects it when that room is visited again.

The atlas draws rooms and their visible exits, and nothing more.
Secret exits are not sent until the game reveals them.  Portals, ENTER
routes, teleports, scripted movement, and other non-directional travel may
not draw a normal map line.  Deleted rooms that can no longer be visited
may remain in Mudlet's saved map.  Room descriptions, EXITS, and SEARCH
remain authoritative.

WHAT THE MAP MARKS
Each room can carry one symbol saying what it is for:

  $  a shop             ?  a questmaster      +  a healer
  P  a practice guild   T  a trainer          &  a pet shop
  #  a jail             X  a death trap

A room is marked when you visit it, from what the game reports about the
room you are standing in.  Nothing is marked in advance, and a room with no
service carries no symbol - most rooms do not.  A death trap is therefore
only ever marked after it has already killed you, which is the point of
marking it.

Doors are drawn as the game reports them: open, closed, or locked.

Not every way through is a direction.  A portal you enter, a rope you climb,
the arcade cabinet that takes you to Hyrule - the map knows those too, and
clicking a room on the far side of one walks you through it.  They are
loaded from the published route list, which the interface fetches for you a
few seconds after you connect.

Symbols and doors are drawn inside the room boxes, so they appear only when
the map is zoomed in far enough to draw those boxes.  Zoom in with the mouse
wheel over the map if you see coloured rooms and no symbols.

The map draws rooms, exits, and doors.  It does not show who or what is
standing in a room - use LOOK for that.

WHERE CAN I GO?
WALKTO lists every area you can walk to and how many rooms out it is.
ROUTES explains how to follow one.

The map belongs to your local Mudlet profile, not your character file.  The
browser client and plain Telnet do not currently show this automatic map.

See also: MUDLET, WALK, GMCP, EXITS, SEARCH, WEBCLIENT
~

0 MUDLET TOCGUI~
Mudlet is the recommended desktop client for Times of Chaos.  Create a profile
for toc.jeremybean.com on port 9000.  In Mudlet's profile settings, leave
"Enable GMCP" and "Allow server to install script packages" enabled.  The
server will offer the official interface and the world atlas automatically.

WHAT THE INTERFACE SHOWS
  Gauges    HP, mana, movement, and experience, across the top.
  Identity  Your name, level, race, and class.
  Affects   What is currently on you, shortest duration first, so the one
            about to lapse is the one you see.  Five ticks or less turns
            orange.  Shadowmeld shows no number because it does not run
            out: it ends when you leave the room, strike, or go visible.
  Target    Who you are fighting and how badly they are hurt, and
            under it your damage per round: the fight you are in,
            kept up to date every round, then the last fight once it
            is over.  Every fight counts, not only the training dummy,
            and it works with damage numbers off.
  Quest     Your quest, what it wants, and the timer counting down,
            and under it the room and region the quest master said
            the target was last seen in.
            An EMERGENCY contract says so, and the last minute turns
            red.
  Online    Who else is playing - the players you could see.  A wizi,
            cloaked, stealthed, shadowmelded, hidden or invisible
            player is left off unless you could see them anyway.
  Map       The whole world, following you as you move.
  Exits     A button for each way out of the room.  Click one to go.
  Tabs      Below the map, four of them:
              Chat   every channel you can hear
              Tells  just the tells, so they are not lost in gossip
              Here   who and what is in the room, aggressives marked
              Items  what you are wearing and what you carry
            The chat shows what you actually heard - a yell from
            another area, or a channel you have switched off, never
            reaches it - in the colours you chose with COLOR, so it
            matches the main window.
  Awards    An achievement is shown over the map for a few seconds
            when you earn it.

Package updates are offered automatically when the server advertises a new
version.

COMMANDS
These are local Mudlet aliases, not game commands.  They do nothing over
plain Telnet or in the browser client.

  tocgui status  show the package version and GMCP state, which
                 bare TOCGUI also does
  tocgui atlas   download the world map into this profile
  tocgui off     hide the interface
  tocgui on      restore the interface

  walk           report on a walk in progress
  walk <vnum>    walk to a room by number
  walk <name>    walk to a published destination, such as WALK THALOS
  walk routes    fetch the published route list
  walk stop      break off a walk

Clicking a room on the map walks you there.  See WALK.

Mapping continues while the interface is hidden.  Your map is saved in the
Mudlet profile.  A profile that was mapped before the world atlas shipped
keeps its old map; tocgui atlas replaces it with the full world.

If the interface says GMCP is waiting, enable GMCP and server packages in
the profile, then reconnect.  Manual package files and source are at:
  github.com/jeremydbean/toc2026/tree/main/mudlet

See also: MAP, WALK, GMCP, WEB, WEBCLIENT
~

0 WALK CLICKTOWALK~
WALK is a Mudlet alias, not a game command.  It does nothing over plain
Telnet or in the browser client.  For the game's own movement shortcut, see
RUN.

Click any room on the map to walk there, or:

  walk <vnum>    walk to a room by number
  walk <name>    walk to a published destination, such as WALK THALOS
  walk routes    fetch the published route list
  walk           report on a walk in progress
  walk stop      break off

HOW IT WALKS
One room at a time.  The next step is sent only once the game confirms you
arrived in the room the route expected, so the walk keeps pace with the
server, respects movement lag, and stops the moment something diverts you -
a mobile in the way, a recall, a portal, being dragged.  It never fires a
whole path at the server and hopes.

A door the map already knows is shut is opened before the step rather than
after it.  For a door nothing warned about, the walk waits a few seconds,
tries OPEN once, and gives up if that does not work.

NAMED DESTINATIONS
WALK ROUTES loads the published route list, the same one the web dashboard
shows.  WALK <name> then matches an area or room name: WALK THALOS, WALK
WYVERN, WALK OZ.

A named walk needs the destination on your own map.  If it is not there yet
the published route is printed instead of walked, because those routes are
written from the Oak Tree Square and are wrong from anywhere else.

The same list carries every way through that is not a compass direction, so
a walk can route through a portal, a rope, or the Hyrule cabinet without you
naming it.  The walk sends the command the same way you would type it.

See also: MUDLET, MAP, RUN, RECALL
~

0 ROUTES DESTINATIONS TRAVEL~
The realms publish a walking route to almost every area: 89 of the 92 areas
that have rooms.  Each route is written from the Oak Tree Square, gives the
commands to get there, and says how many rooms out it is.

WHERE TO SEE THEM
  In game        WALKTO lists every area and how far out it is.
  Mudlet         WALK ROUTES loads the list, then WALK <name> follows one,
                 such as WALK THALOS or WALK WYVERN.  See WALK.
  Browser client The Routes panel lists every destination.
  Web dashboard  The Directions view, with the same data.

A route is a walk, not a shortcut.  It uses ordinary movement, opens the
doors along the way, and passes through whatever lives between here and
there - a route to a distant area is not a safe route for a low level
character.

Routes count a room you have to wait in, such as a river or a teleport, as
worth several rooms of walking, because you cannot steer while it carries
you.  A published route may therefore walk you past a faster-looking ride.

THREE AREAS HAVE NO ROUTE
Dresden has none because it contains the Oak Tree Square, which is where
every route starts.  The Quest Zone and Temple Despair have none because
nothing in the world links to them.

Secret doors, and anything the game has not shown you, are not in a route.
EXITS and SEARCH remain authoritative.

See also: WALKTO, MAP, WALK, MUDLET, RUN, RECALL
~

0 DUMMY TRAINING YARD~
Syntax: dummy                  the menu, and what it is set to
        dummy <level>          what level it should pretend to be
        dummy shape <name>     how it should defend
        dummy hits <type>      what it should hit you with
        dummy magic <kind>     what it should cast at you, or none
        dummy haste            an extra attack a round, on or off
        dummy rounds <n>       n rounds, then the bell
        dummy endless          no bell; DUMMY REPORT ends the run
        dummy defends yes|no   whether it dodges, parries and blocks
        dummy fights yes|no    whether it hits you back
        dummy reset            back to the standard, ranked fight
        dummy report           stop early and read the numbers
        dummy leaderboard      the benchmark leaderboard

A training dummy you can hit as hard as you like.

WHERE IT IS
  In the Training Yard, beneath the great oak in the centre of the Oak
  Tree Square.  From the centre of the square: down, south.  Go down the
  staircase in the tree to the Entrance to the Hall of Heroes, and the
  yard is through the archway to the south.  North walks you back out.

  In Mudlet, WALK DUMMY walks you there.  Anybody can get there, whatever
  their class or guild.

ONE AT A TIME
  The yard takes one fighter at a time.  While somebody is in there you
  are told they are training and to wait for them to come out -
  immortals excepted - so a run is never shared and never interrupted.

  Walk out in the middle of a run, or recall, and the run is abandoned:
  you are put back the way you were when it began.  DUMMY REPORT first
  if you want the numbers.

THE RULES OF THE YARD
  Neither of you can die there.  The dummy soaks up everything, and its
  blows take you down to one hit point and no further.

  When the run ends you are put back the way you were when it began -
  hit points, mana and moves - not healed to full.  Walk in hurt and you
  walk out hurt; the yard is for measuring, not for resting.  Leave in
  the middle of a run, by recall or anything else, and the run is
  abandoned and you are put back on the way out.

  Nothing is learned there.  Your skills and spells do not improve
  while you are in the yard, whatever you do.

THE STANDARD FIGHT - THE ONLY ONE THAT IS RANKED
  By default every run is the standard fight: walk in, KILL DUMMY, and
  that is the ranked run - twenty-five rounds, then a bell, against a dummy at
  your own level that fights normally.  It dodges,
  parries and blocks, and it hits you back.  It is worth what a mobile
  of your level really averages, measured across every mobile in the
  world, so the time-to-kill in the report means something.

  The leaderboard ranks the standard fight and nothing else.  A run
  counts only if every one of these holds, from the first blow to the
  bell:

    - it is twenty-five rounds and reaches the bell
    - the dummy is your own level
    - it defends itself and it fights back
    - its shape, damage type, spell and haste are as DUMMY RESET
      leaves them

  Change any of them - a longer or shorter fight, an endless one, a
  dummy that will not hit you or will not defend itself, anything at
  all - and the run is not ranked.  Stopping early with DUMMY REPORT is
  not ranked either, and neither is a run during which somebody
  changed the dummy.  Immortals are never ranked; trusted players are.

  Whatever you change lasts while you are in the yard.  When the yard
  empties the dummy goes back to the standard fight, so whoever comes in
  next and simply attacks it gets the ranked run, not your settings.

  You are never left guessing: the menu has a Ranked line, every change
  you make tells you straight away whether runs will still count, and
  an unranked run says exactly why at the bell.  DUMMY RESET puts
  everything back to the standard fight.  DUMMY LEADERBOARD shows the
  board.

CHANGING IT
  Shapes        soft, armored, evasive, brutal
  Damage types  slash, pierce, bash, fire, cold, lightning, acid,
                energy, holy
  Spells        none, force, fire, cold, lightning, acid, light, harm,
                negative

  A damage school delivered by a weapon tests a resistance.  The same
  school delivered by a spell tests a saving throw as well, which is a
  different question about the same suit of armour.  DUMMY HASTE gives
  it a second attack a round, for what your armour is worth against
  something fast.  It casts once a pulse, always the same spell: a
  reading you cannot reproduce is not a measurement.

  To measure your damage alone, take the dummy's own part out of the
  fight.  DUMMY DEFENDS NO makes it stand still: it stops dodging,
  parrying, blocking and ducking, so every blow you throw is one it
  takes.  DUMMY FIGHTS NO makes it stop hitting you, spells included.
  Your own defences still work either way - that is your armour.

  DUMMY ROUNDS <n> makes the fight n rounds long instead of twenty-five, and
  DUMMY ENDLESS takes the bell away so the run lasts until DUMMY REPORT.

  None of these is ranked.  They are for finding things out; the
  standard fight is for comparing yourself with everybody else.

  None of this can be changed in the middle of a run: a reading blended
  from two opponents is worse than none.  DUMMY RESET is the exception -
  it throws the run away and starts over, and works mid-fight.

READING THE REPORT
  The clock starts at the first blow in either direction, so taking a
  moment to choose a weapon costs nothing.

  WHAT YOU DID is your total, your damage per second, per swing and per
  round - per round is the figure to compare two runs on, since it does
  not move with how long the rounds happened to take.  Then how many
  swings landed, your best and average hit, what missed and what it
  turned aside, and whether the damage came from your weapon, your
  spells or your skills.

  Then attack by attack: every spell you cast, every special attack and
  your weapon, each on its own line, biggest first, with its damage, its
  share, how many of its attempts landed, its average and its best.
  Second and third attack are not separable from the weapon itself - the
  game swings them as the same attack - so they are in its line.

  WHAT IT DID TO YOU is the same from the other side: how often it
  tried, how many you ducked, parried, dodged or blocked, what got
  through, your worst hit, and how long it would take you from full.

  After a ranked run, the board: this run, your personal best, where you
  stand among everybody who has run it, and how far the record is.

See also: LEADERBOARD, COMPARE, LORE, DAMAGENUMBERS
~

0 LEADERBOARD BENCHMARK~
Syntax: dummy leaderboard

The training yard's benchmark board: the best twenty-five-round standard run
each character has posted, best first.  It shows the top ten, and tells
you where you stand among everybody who has ever run it.  Each line
gives class and guild the way WHO does: T/T for a thief in the thieves'
guild, T/W for a thief among the warriors, and Monk or Necro.

ONLY THE STANDARD FIGHT IS RANKED.  That is the default fight in the
training yard: twenty-five rounds against a dummy at your own level that
defends itself and hits back, with every setting exactly as DUMMY RESET
leaves it, run all the way to the bell.

Anything else is not ranked:

  - a longer or shorter fight (DUMMY ROUNDS), or an endless one
  - a dummy that does not defend itself (DUMMY DEFENDS NO)
  - a dummy that does not hit you (DUMMY FIGHTS NO)
  - any other change: its level, shape, damage type, spell or haste
  - stopping early with DUMMY REPORT
  - somebody changing the dummy in the middle of your run
  - an immortal's run - though a player trusted with staff commands is
    ranked like anybody else, since they fight at their own level

The game tells you which of these applies: the dummy's menu has a
Ranked line, every change you make says whether runs still count, and
an unranked run names the reason at the bell.

When a ranked run ends you are told whether it is a personal best, your
place on the board, and how far ahead the record is.  Post the best run
anybody has run and you earn the achievement Top of the Board.

To run it: DUMMY RESET in the training yard, then KILL DUMMY.

Staff can tidy it: DUMMY LEADERBOARD REMOVE <name> takes one
character's run off, and DUMMY LEADERBOARD CLEAR CONFIRM empties it.
Both are logged.

See also: DUMMY, ACHIEVEMENTS
~

0 GMCP~
GMCP is the out-of-band protocol used by modern MUD clients.  It is not a game
command and does not add text to normal Telnet output.  A client that enables
GMCP can receive:

  Char.Vitals   - hit points, mana, movement, experience, and level
  Char.Status   - character name, level, race, and class
  Char.Affects  - the spells currently on you, with their durations
  Room.Info     - room number, name, area, terrain, visible exits, the
                  state of any doors, room flags, and what the room is
                  for (shop, questmaster, healer, practice, trainer)
  Char.Quest    - your current quest, its timer, your streak, and
                  where the target was last seen
  Char.Target   - who you are fighting and how they are doing, and
                  your damage per round in this fight or the last
  Char.Online   - who else is playing, as far as you could see
  Char.Items    - what you carry and what you wear
  Char.Achievement - one achievement, at the moment you earn it
  Room.Chars    - who and what is in the room with you
  Comm.Channel  - one line of channel talk, one say, or one tell, as
                  you hear it, in the colour you chose for it
  Client.Map    - location of the official world atlas
  Client.GUI    - version and location of the official Mudlet package

Comm.Channel is sent where the line is delivered, so a client receives
exactly what the character heard and nothing else: a yell from another
area, or a channel you have switched off, never arrives.

Core.Ping requests are also answered.  Room.Info excludes secret exits and
destinations the character cannot see.  Clients that do not negotiate GMCP
continue to work normally.

See also: MUDLET, MAP, PROMPT
~

0 WEBCLIENT BROWSERCLIENT BROWSER~
The first-party browser client provides ANSI color, masked password entry,
scrollback, command history, reconnect support, aliases, quick actions,
semicolon command chains, and transcript download.  It connects through the
same private web service as the administration dashboard.

On a local installation use:
  http://127.0.0.1:9001/client

On the dedicated Raspberry Pi's private LAN use:
  http://toc.local:9001/client

The :9001 port is required.  The hosted public game does not expose its admin
service merely because the game is reachable on port 9000.  Ask the operator
for a secure browser-client address when playing remotely.  The browser client
does not currently include Mudlet's automatic mapper.

See also: WEB, MUDLET, MAP, ALIAS
~

0 NE NORTHEAST NW NORTHWEST SE SOUTHEAST SW SOUTHWEST~
Syntax: <direction>

These are shorthand movement commands for the diagonal exits in a room.
Each direction functions the same way as typing the full diagonal, for
example "ne" moves northeast and "sw" moves southwest.  If the exit exists
and is open, you will step through it; otherwise you will be told the way
is closed or blocked.

See also: NORTH, EXITS, RUN
~

0 ENTER~
Syntax: enter

ENTER steps into a portal, vehicle, or structure that does not map to a
standard directional exit.  Use it for magic gates, wagons, boats, and any
special entry point described in the room.  Look for keywords such as
"portal", "gate", "boat", or "door" to identify what you can enter.

See also: RIDE, MOUNT, RECALL
~

0 RIDE MOUNT~
Syntax: ride <mount>
Syntax: mount <mount>

RIDE and MOUNT climb onto a mountable creature or vehicle.  Only mobs with
the MOUNTABLE flag can be ridden.  While mounted you move with your mount
and benefit from reduced movement costs.  Some areas restrict mounted
travel.  Use DISMOUNT to climb off.

See also: ENTER, DISMOUNT
~

0 CASTLE~
Syntax: castle
Syntax: castle <message>

Castle chat is the private channel for members of your castle.  Typing
castle with no arguments toggles whether you hear castle chat.  Supplying a
message sends it to every logged-in member of your castle.  Synonyms: CC and
"-".

See also: CHANNELS, CGOS, CC
~

0 IGNORE~
Syntax: ignore <name>
        ignore none
        ignore list

Adds or removes players from a personal ignore list.  Characters on your
ignore list cannot contact you via tells or channels.  The LIST option shows
who you are currently ignoring; NONE clears the entire list.

See also: TELL, REPLY, AFK
~

0 LISTEN~
Syntax: listen

LISTEN receives a brief ambient sound description of your current room:
wind through cracks, distant dripping water, faint movement in the
shadows, and other atmospheric details that add flavor to the environment.
The description varies by room type and current conditions.

LISTEN is a flavor command, not a tactical warning.  It does not reveal
hidden characters or notify you of nearby enemies.  Use SEARCH to
uncover hidden exits or traps, and SCAN to see what mobs are in
adjacent rooms.

See also: SEARCH, SCAN, LOOK
~

0 REPAIR~
Syntax: repair <object>

Requests repairs from an NPC repair vendor.  The item may be worn or in your
pack; you do not have to take it off first.  The smith quotes a price from
how badly worn the item is and how high a level it is, charges you, and
restores it to full condition.  If the item cannot be repaired you will be
told why.

An item remembers how often it has been mended.  After about two dozen
repairs the metal has had enough, and the next attempt breaks it for good --
the smith will not charge you for that one.  REPAIR tells you the running
count each time, so you can see it coming.

Repair vendors are found in some guild halls, market districts, and weapon or
armor shops throughout the world.  Not all shopkeepers offer repair services;
look for vendors who mention repairs in their room description or greeting.

See also: VALUE, BUY, LIST
~

0 SECONDARY~
Syntax: secondary <weapon>

Equips the specified weapon in your off-hand if you have trained the
SECONDARY weapon skill.  This toggles dual wielding; to return to a single
weapon, wield your main-hand weapon normally.

Dual wielding with low skill imposes accuracy penalties to both weapons.
As your SECONDARY skill improves, the off-hand penalty diminishes.  Smaller
or lighter weapons are generally better choices for the off hand.

See also: WIELD, DISARM, DUAL WIELD
~

0 DUAL WIELD~
Dual wielding is the act of fighting with a weapon in each hand
simultaneously.  To dual wield you must:
  1. Have the SECONDARY skill (gain it from a trainer).
  2. WIELD your main weapon normally.
  3. Use SECONDARY <weapon> to equip the off-hand weapon.

Off-hand accuracy starts lower than the main hand and improves as your
SECONDARY skill increases.  Lighter weapons work better in the off hand;
very heavy or two-handed weapons cannot be used this way.

To return to single-weapon combat, WIELD your main weapon again -- this
replaces the off-hand weapon.  You can also use REMOVE on the off-hand
item, but WIELD is faster.

See also: SECONDARY, WIELD, DISARM
~

0 TELEKINESIS TK~
Syntax: telekinesis <target>
Syntax: tk <target>

TELEKINESIS (TK) attempts to move or lift a distant target using pure
mental force.  It can retrieve objects in adjacent rooms, push enemies
back, or interact with distant levers and switches.  Requires psionic
training and sufficient mana.  Difficulty scales with the weight and
distance of the target.

See also: CLAIRVOYANCE, PROJECT, PSIONICS
~

0 CLAIRVOYANCE~
Syntax: clairvoyance

CLAIRVOYANCE briefly lets you scry your immediate surroundings for
concealed exits or threats without moving.  You receive a momentary
mental impression of nearby rooms and any significant dangers within
them.  Range and clarity improve with your psionic skill level.

See also: TELEKINESIS, DANGER, PROJECT, PSIONICS
~

0 DANGER~
Syntax: danger

DANGER SENSE gives you a quick pulse of psionic awareness about nearby
threats.  It warns you if the current room or an adjacent area contains
a particularly perilous mob, active trap, or other significant hazard.
Useful for scouting before stepping into an unknown area.

See also: CLAIRVOYANCE, SEARCH, PSIONICS
~

0 PROJECT~
Syntax: project <direction>

PROJECT sends your spirit one room at a time in the chosen direction,
letting you scout ahead while your body remains in place.  You can look
around the projected location and step further.  Running out of mana or
losing concentration snaps you back to your body immediately.

See also: ASTRAL WALK, CLAIRVOYANCE, PSIONICS
~

0 SHIFT~
Syntax: shift <player>

SHIFT teleports you directly to another player's location if the
destination and your current room permit travel.  Many safe, private,
or magically warded rooms block shifting.  The target receives no
warning of your arrival.

See also: ASTRAL WALK, PROJECT, PSIONICS
~

0 SHADOWMELD~
Syntax: shadowmeld

Draw the shadows around yourself.  You stay in them until you move.

Shadowmeld is STEALTH with the timer taken off and the room nailed
down.  It is exactly as good at hiding you as stealth is - the same
roll, the same weather, the same odds - and unlike stealth it never
runs out.  What you give up is the ability to walk anywhere.

Nothing but moving ends it.  Sit, sleep, read your notes, count your
coin - the shadows hold.

WHAT IT IS FOR
  Two things.

  Going AFK.  This is the one state in the game that is safe to leave
  the keyboard in: while you are melded no creature in the world can
  see you, so nothing will wander in and kill you while you are away.

  Laying in wait.  Meld in a room somebody has to walk through, and
  wait.  They will not see you unless they beat your skill, and you
  strike first.  The blow itself is what ends the meld.

Three things break it.  Leaving the room, however you leave it -
walking, a portal, a recall, being dragged.  Striking at somebody.
And VIS, which drops every concealment you are carrying.  Typing
SHADOWMELD again steps you out deliberately.

Against another player it behaves exactly like stealth: they roll
against your skill, and looking for the hidden does not help them.
The gods, with holylight, always see you.  Faerie fog reveals you.

You cannot start a meld in the middle of a fight.

THE SKILL
  Shadowmeld is an ordinary skill and improves the ordinary way, with
  use and with failure.  It cannot be practised from nothing, gained
  at a guildmaster or bought: the only ways to hold it are the fourth
  remort, which grants it at 50%, and the gift of a god.

  Once you hold it you may practise it up like anything else, and no
  later remort will take it away.

See also: HIDE, SNEAK, STEALTH, VISIBLE, REMORT


~

0 HISTORY REPLAY~
Syntax: history                  -- the last 20, every channel
        history <number>         -- that many instead
        history <channel>        -- just that one
        history <channel> <num>  -- that many of just that one
        history tell             -- your own tells, and nobody else's
        history list             -- what is held, and how much

Read back what was said on gossip and the other channels, including
what you missed while you were offline.

If your client stopped capturing, or you were fighting, or you simply
scrolled past it - HISTORY on its own shows the last twenty lines from
every channel you can hear, oldest first, with the time against each.

    history            the last twenty
    history 50         the last fifty
    history gossip     only gossip
    history gossip 5   the last five gossips
    history tell       your tells

CHANNELS WITH A HISTORY
  gossip, shout, yell, question, answer, music, leveling, and - for
  those who can hear them - hero, immtalk and godtalk.

  HISTORY LIST names them and says how many lines each is holding.

WHAT YOU CAN SEE
  Only channels you are allowed to hear.  A mortal cannot read immtalk
  back any more than they could hear it live.

  Tells are private and are never in the shared history.  Each
  character keeps their own copy of the tells they sent and received,
  and no one else can read them.

  SAY is not in the history either, and cannot be.  A say is heard by
  the room, and the history is handed to whoever asks for it - there
  is no honest way to replay a room conversation to somebody who was
  not standing there.  Says are still recorded for staff, and still
  reach the chat window of everyone who was in the room at the time.

WHAT YOU MISSED
  Nothing is thrown at you when you log in.  Type HISTORY and a line
  reading '--- while you were away ---' is drawn at the moment you last
  left, with everything said since below it.

  So the lines you would have seen had you been here are there when you
  want them, and not before.

  Tells are not in it.  A tell to somebody who is not logged in is
  never delivered, so there is nothing there to have missed.

IT DOES NOT SURVIVE A REBOOT
  History is held in memory.  When the game restarts it is gone.  It is
  a way to catch up on what you missed, not a transcript.

Only the last 30 lines of each channel are kept, and the last 20 tells.

See also: CHANNELS, GOSSIP, TELL

~

0 STASH~
Syntax: stash                          -- what is in it
        stash put <item>               -- from anywhere in the world
        stash put all                  -- everything you can spare
        stash get <item>               -- at the altar only
        stash buy                      -- more room, at the altar
        stash link <character>         -- share with another of yours
        stash unlink <character>       -- stop sharing
        stash of <character>           -- what is in theirs
        stash take <character> <item>  -- at the altar only
        stash deny <character>         -- refuse an offer
        stash block <character>        -- and stop them asking
        stash unblock <character>      -- let them ask again

Storage at the altar that survives a reboot: a place to keep items and
loot you do not want to carry around.

STASH on its own lists what is in it, and you can read that from
anywhere - knowing what you own is not the same as reaching it.

STASH PUT works from anywhere in the world, so you can set something
aside the moment you find it.  STASH PUT ALL sends everything in your
hands that is allowed in.  STASH GET works only at the altar, one step
west of where you land when you recall.  That is deliberate: the stash
stores loot, it is not a way to carry it.

You start with room for fifty things, counted as things rather than
weight - a bag of gems is one.  STASH BUY at the altar adds another
twenty-five, and the price climbs each time, up to five hundred.  The
first purchase costs 250 gold, the second 1,000, the third 2,250 -- 250
gold times the square of the purchase number -- and all eighteen come to
527,250 gold.  STASH on its own always shows the price of the next one.

Anything with a timer on it is refused, and so is a corpse.  They would
only rot where you put them.

SHARING BETWEEN YOUR CHARACTERS
  Two characters who each name the other share one pool: either can
  take what the other left.  It takes both halves, so nobody can reach
  into your stash without you naming them back.  See HELP STASHLINK -
  that entry covers linking, denying and blocking in full.

Your stash is saved the instant anything goes in or comes out, so a
crash cannot cost you what you put away.

See also: STASHLINK, DONATE, RECALL

~

0 STASHLINK LINK~
Syntax: stash link <character>         -- offer, or accept
        stash unlink <character>       -- stop sharing
        stash of <character>           -- what is in theirs
        stash take <character> <item>  -- at the altar only
        stash deny <character>         -- refuse an offer
        stash block <character>        -- and stop them asking
        stash unblock <character>      -- let them ask again

How to share one stash between two of your own characters.

IT TAKES BOTH HALVES
  Naming somebody is only half of a link.  Log in as your first
  character and type:

      stash link <your other character>

  You will be told that nothing is shared yet.  Log in as that other
  character and type the same thing back, naming the first.  Now the
  two of you share: either can see and take what the other left.

  This is why nobody can reach into your stash uninvited.  Someone can
  name you all they like; until you name them back it does nothing.

WHEN SOMEBODY NAMES YOU
  You are told at login that an offer is waiting and who made it.
  Three answers:

      stash link <name>    accept, and share from then on
      stash deny <name>    refuse, and clear the offer
      stash block <name>   refuse, and stop them asking again

  DENY clears one offer.  If the same person keeps offering, BLOCK
  turns them away before the offer is ever recorded - they are simply
  told you are not accepting.  STASH BLOCK on its own lists everybody
  you are turning away, and STASH UNBLOCK lets one of them ask again.

USING A SHARED STASH
  STASH OF <character> lists what is in theirs, from anywhere.
  STASH TAKE <character> <item> pulls it out, at the altar only - the
  same rule your own stash follows.

  The other character does not have to be logged in.  Their saved
  stash is read straight off the disk.

UNLINKING
  STASH UNLINK <character> ends your half at once.  Nothing is lost:
  every item stays in the stash of whoever put it there.

  You may keep up to eight links.

See also: STASH, RECALL

~

0 BUFF~
Syntax: heal
        heal <spell>
        buff <spell>

Ask Hermie for a spell.  She works exactly like the healer at the pit -
HEAL on its own reads out what she offers, HEAL <name> asks for one -
except that everything she does is free.

BUFF is the same command under another name, for when a healer and
Hermie are somehow in the same room and you want to be sure which one
you are talking to.

Name a group instead of a spell to take a handful at once: DEFENSE,
DETECTS, TRAVEL, CURES, RESTORE, COMBAT, or ALL for the lot.

REFRESH, HEAL and MANA fill the pool rather than topping it up, and
VITALS does all three, so there is no reason to ask twice.

See also: HEAL, SPELLUP
~

0 REPORTS~
Syntax: reports
        reports <bugs|typos|ideas> [count]
        reports clear <bugs|typos|ideas>

What players have filed with BUG, TYPO and IDEA.  Each of those adds one
line to a file, and until now nothing in the game ever mentioned them
again, so they piled up unread - several hundred of them.

REPORTS on its own counts what has been filed and how much of it is new
since you last looked.  Naming a kind shows the most recent ones, twenty
by default, and marks them read; you are told at login when more arrive.

Be warned that most of what is in there is not a report.  Typing BUG and
your next command on the same line files the command, so these files are
full of "wear all" and "short sword" among the real reports.

REPORTS CLEAR sets a file aside under a dated name rather than deleting
it.  What a player told us is worth keeping even when the file is mostly
noise, and a mistyped command should not destroy it.

See also: BUG, TYPO, IDEA, NOTE
~

0 MIRROR~
Syntax: mirror <player>
        mirror restore
        mirror clear

MIRROR takes off everything you are wearing and puts on what somebody
else is wearing, so you can look at a character's setup from the inside -
why they are taking the damage they are, whether a slot is empty, what a
build actually adds up to - without asking them to hand anything over.

It works whether or not they are logged in.  An online character is read
from the game; an offline one from their last save, and MIRROR says which
it used.

Nothing about you can refuse a piece.  An item that would normally zap
itself off someone of your alignment goes on anyway, and a two-handed
weapon will not knock the shield back off.  The one exception is an
ITEM_ACTION - wearing one of those recalls or kills you - and MIRROR says
so when it leaves one out.

MIRROR RESTORE undoes it: the borrowed kit goes into a pack called
"<player>'s gear" in your inventory, and what you were wearing when you
started goes back on.  The pack is yours to keep, look in, or purge.

MIRROR CLEAR just takes everything off, borrowed or not, and remembers
nothing.

What you get is a fresh copy of each piece made from its prototype, not
their copy of it: an enchantment they put on their own sword is not
reproduced, and neither is its wear and tear.  Anything you already had
on comes off into your inventory and is never destroyed.

Use of the command is logged.

See also: INVULN, LOAD, CLONE, FINGER, STAT
~

0 INVULN INVULNERABLE~
Syntax: invuln
        invuln damage
        invuln absorb
        invuln off

INVULN is an immortal toggle.  While it is on, nothing takes hit points
off you: no mobile, no player, no spell, and your equipment stops taking
damage too.  Typing INVULN on its own turns it on, and again turns it off.

The argument chooses only how a blow reads, not whether it lands:

  DAMAGE   the default.  Attacks connect and are described exactly as
           they would be normally, damage numbers and all - you simply
           do not lose anything.  This is what you want when you are
           watching a fight to see how it behaves.

  ABSORB   attacks visibly do nothing.  They read exactly as they would
           against something immune to the weapon being used - "Zog is
           unaffected by your slash!" - and name the attack that failed.

  OFF      turn it off.

Nothing about it is shown to anyone else, and it does not appear on your
score sheet.  Use of the command is logged.

See also: HOLYLIGHT, WIZINVIS, CLOAK, RESTORE
~

0 CHANGES~
Syntax: changes

What has changed in the game lately, newest first.  Type HELP on any
command named here for the detail.

1 Oct 2026
  SWEDISH works again.  The command set a flag but nothing read it: the
  speech filter that garbles a swedished (or drunk) player's SAY and
  channel text had been dropped in a refactor and left only as an
  uncompiled file.  It is compiled and wired back into SAY and the
  channels.  Drunk slurring returns with it.

  Mudlet 1.5.0 adds a combat-awareness set: a lag bar that shows when
  you can act again, an enemy health gauge beside your own bars, a
  Buffs tab with a countdown bar per spell, a Group tab showing each
  party member's level and health, a low-health warning, and a Tells
  tab that lights up on a new tell.  Update the package to get them.

  Mudlet: the quest strip now carries the target room, and clicking it
  (or typing WALK QUEST) walks you straight there. The game sends the
  room's vnum with the quest, so it is an exact path, not a guess by
  name. Update the package to 1.4.0 to get it.

  The dashboard's gear finder names the best piece for each of your
  eighteen slots for your class and level - both rings, both necks and
  both wrists, two different items for each pair - then ranks every
  item you could wear in each slot, Light included.  Plain
  armour and shields were missing because their own armour class was
  never counted, lights because they carry no wear flag, and an item
  that made your saves worse was being ranked as if it made them
  better.

  Staff can eat anything again, to destroy an item they carry.  It
  checked the character's level rather than their trust, so a builder
  trusted to immortal rank was told the item was not edible.

  The training dummy can no longer be finished off.  A fatality
  backstab killed it outright, because an instant kill never passed
  the check that keeps it standing.  Backstab it and you get an
  ordinary backstab, counted on the meter like any other blow; and
  anything else that kills on the spot now finds it back on its post.

  AFFECT now lists psionic wards.  MINDBAR, PSIONIC ARMOR and PSYCHIC
  SHIELD change no statistic - they are a named protection and a
  duration - and the list was skipping exactly that shape, so the one
  kind of effect you most want to confirm was the one you could not
  see.  Reported by Alaric.  Psionics and skills are also no longer
  announced as spells.

  Damage numbers now appear for every weapon.  Fifteen weapons in the
  world carry no particular attack noun, and those went down a branch
  of the combat message code that had never been given the bracketed
  figure - so DAMAGENUMBERS worked for every weapon except the odd
  ones, and for bare hands in some cases.  Reported by Alaric.

  A training yard, and a dummy in it that cannot be killed.  Go down
  the staircase in the centre of the Oak Tree Square, then south -
  WALK DUMMY in Mudlet takes you there - and KILL DUMMY.  HELP DUMMY
  explains all of it.  The yard takes one fighter at a time, so nobody
  else can walk into your run.

  Every run is the standard fight by default: twenty-five rounds, then a
  bell, against a dummy at your own level that fights normally - it
  dodges, parries, blocks and hits back.  Your best goes on a
  leaderboard - DUMMY LEADERBOARD - and when a run ends you are told
  whether it was a personal best, where you stand among everybody who
  has run it, and how far the record is.  Post the best run anybody has
  and you earn Top of the Board.

  Just attacking the dummy gives you the standard fight: when the yard
  empties, it goes back to it, whatever the last fighter set.

  Only the standard fight is ranked.  DUMMY DEFENDS NO and DUMMY FIGHTS
  NO take the dummy's own part out so you can measure your damage
  alone, and DUMMY ROUNDS and DUMMY ENDLESS change the length - but
  changing any setting at all means the run is not ranked, and the game
  says so the moment you change it.

  Neither of you can die in the yard.  When a run ends you are put back
  the way you were when it began - hit points, mana and moves - not
  healed to full, so it is no place to recover.  Nothing is learned
  there either: skills and spells do not improve in the yard.

  The report breaks the fight down attack by attack, both ways - every
  spell you cast and every special attack on its own line, with its
  damage, share, hit rate, average and best - alongside your totals,
  damage per second, per swing and per round, and everything it did
  to you.  Set the dummy up however the question needs: any level,
  four shapes, nine damage types, a spell for it to cast, haste, a
  different number of rounds, or DUMMY ENDLESS for a run with no
  bell.

  A portal now refuses to take you into a private room that is full,
  the way walking in always has.  It used to be the one way past.

  The quest master no longer asks you to kill the training dummy, which
  cannot die.

  Mudlet shows your damage per round under the target: live during a
  fight, then the last fight's once it ends.  Every fight counts, and
  the game measures it, so it works with damage numbers off.  A new
  line below the quest lists who else is online - only players you
  could see, so nobody wizi, cloaked, stealthed or hidden.  Mudlet
  offers the 1.3.0 package update automatically.

  City guards hunting a wanted player no longer follow them into the
  Temple.  A hunter stops at the edge of a room mobiles may not enter,
  as it always has at a safe one.

  If WALK DUMMY in Mudlet still heads somewhere else, TOCGUI ATLAS
  reloads the map with the yard where it is now.

  The Mudlet chat window now shows each channel in the colour you
  chose for it with COLOR, the same as the main window, and the quest
  strip says where the quest master last saw your target.  Mudlet
  offers the 1.3.1 package update automatically.

  The plaque in the Hall of Heroes lists every hero there has ever
  been, and a new hero's name is on it the moment they are made - as
  the room always said it would be.  LOOK PLAQUE, down from the
  centre of the Oak Tree Square.

  POKE pokes again.  It was taken as the start of the longer command
  POKER, so the social could never be used.  A word that is exactly a
  social's name is now that social.

  COMBINE is back.  It switches your inventory between the combined
  list - (3) a big pot pie - and the long one, and AUTOLIST shows which
  you have.  It had been switched off while everything it controls
  stayed on.  COMB is still the social, so type at least COMBI.

  Pyrotechnics now hits as hard as a fireball cast at your level.  It
  used to scale with your current hit points, which made it about three
  times its fellow psionics at level fifty and grew without limit with
  every remort.  A failed attempt now costs its wait, as a failed cast
  does, rather than letting you try again at once.  Heating the
  victim's gear is still what it adds over a fireball.

25 Sep 2026
  RECALL can be moved.  RECALL SET makes the room you are standing in your
  recall point, RECALL DEFAULT puts it back to the Temple, and RECALL WHERE
  tells you where it is.  Once every 30 minutes, and not mid-fight.

  The note board answers back.  NOTE REPLY <n> starts a note to
  whoever wrote that one with the subject already set, NOTE FORWARD
  <n> <player> passes one on, NOTE SEARCH finds a note by any word in
  it, NOTE UNREAD shows only what is waiting and NOTE CATCHUP clears
  the lot.  See HELP NOTE.

  HISTORY draws a line where you last logged out, so what was said
  while you were away is easy to pick out.  Nothing is shown to you
  automatically - you have to ask.

  HISTORY reads back what was said.  Type it on its own for the last
  twenty lines across every channel you can hear, HISTORY 50 for more,
  HISTORY GOSSIP for one channel, or HISTORY TELL for your own tells -
  which stay private, and are kept per character.  It is held in memory
  and is lost on a reboot, so it is a way to catch up rather than a
  transcript.  See HELP HISTORY.

  You can set your recall point at the quest giver now.  A room was
  being refused for being 'private', which is a count of who is standing
  in it - the questmaster and you already made two - and that count
  changes minute to minute, so a point set in a quiet moment would have
  quietly reverted to the Temple later.  Only the permanent flags refuse
  a recall point now.

  A mobile that lives in a room no longer fills it either.  The quest
  giver's room holds two, and the questmaster was one of them.

  An idle immortal no longer fills a one-person room.  Standing in the
  quest room was enough to shut questing down for everybody else, which
  is not what that flag is for - it exists to stop players walking in on
  each other.  Staff can also walk into such a room now rather than
  being turned away from it.

  STASH LINK has a help entry of its own at last: HELP STASHLINK covers
  linking, denying, blocking and sharing in full, and HELP STASH lists
  every subcommand rather than four of them.

  SHADOWMELD is a real skill now, and it is meant for two things: going
  AFK without being killed for it, and laying in wait in a room somebody
  has to walk through so that you strike first.

  The fourth remort teaches it at 50% and it improves from there with
  use and with failure, like anything else.  A god can grant it to
  anyone with SET SKILL.  No later remort takes it away, and once you
  hold it you may practise it up.

  It behaves exactly like STEALTH against other players now rather than
  like hide - the same roll against the same weather - so looking for
  the hidden no longer finds you.  Holylight still does, and faerie fog
  still reveals you.  VIS steps you out of it, WHO shows [SHADOW] beside
  your name, WHERE leaves you off the list and DANGER SENSE counts you,
  all exactly as they do for stealth.  Striking at somebody ends it,
  whether you swing or cast.

  The recall that never fails has moved from the fourth remort down to
  the third, to make room for shadowmeld.

  Remorting no longer cares which guilds you have belonged to, only
  which classes you have lived as: mage, cleric, thief, warrior, monk,
  necro.  Every life must be a class you have not played.  Join any
  guild you like, as often as you like.

  The third remort now carries three gifts instead of one: twice the
  carrying room, twice as long before the idle void, and a second psionic
  power from each discipline.  Psionics STACK from now on -- a remort
  remembers what it gave you and hands it back, so a later life never
  costs you a power you already had, and the fifth remort gives you all
  seventeen.

  Remorting no longer reads your class and guild history as one list.  It
  used to, and because a guild is stored as its matching class, having
  been in the mage guild barred you from ever living as a mage.  Worse, a
  player who spent their choices badly could arrive at the fourth remort
  with nothing legal left to pick, stuck at 57 with 59 out of reach.  The
  two histories are separate now, 'none' is always a legal guild, and a
  refusal lists everything still open to you.

  Two new gifts for remorting.  The remort taken at level 56 doubles
  how long you may idle before the void takes you.  The one taken at
  level 57 gives SHADOWMELD: draw the shadows around yourself and stay
  in them until you move, sleeping if you like, with no creature in the
  world able to see you.  It is meant for stepping away from the
  keyboard.  See HELP SHADOWMELD.

  There is a stash at the altar now, one step west of recall.  STASH
  PUT sets something aside from anywhere in the world; STASH GET takes
  it back out, and that part only works at the altar.  Fifty things to
  begin with, more for coin.  See HELP STASH.

  Hermie answers HEAL now, exactly like the healer at the pit and
  with the same menu, except that everything she does is free.  She
  no longer listens to what you say in the room.  She has ten more
  spells than she did, six named groups, and REFRESH, HEAL and MANA
  now fill the pool instead of handing out a slice at a time.  See
  HELP BUFF.

  The healer at the pit tells you when a curse resists instead of
  taking your gold in silence.

  The game says when something is waiting for you.  Unread notes are
  announced at login instead of sitting unmentioned, and staff are told
  how many bug, typo and idea reports have come in since they last
  looked.  See HELP REPORTS.

  Staff can now MIRROR a player's gear onto themselves to look at a
  build from the inside, online or not, and INVULN stops anything hurting
  them while they do it.  MIRROR RESTORE hands the borrowed kit back in a
  pack and puts their own gear on again.

  Herbs and spell components actually appear now.  They had barely been
  landing at all for years, and the scattering is deliberately sparse -
  finding one should be a small event - and stops entirely when nobody is
  logged in.

  Two monster wards that had never worked now do.  A bloody aura turns
  magic aside; a dominion shield turns aside everything, and only goes up
  when the creature is badly hurt.  Four end-game creatures use them: the
  Lord General and the Fade in the Battleground, and Zoltan and the second
  succubus in Valhalla.  See HELP DOMINION SHIELD.

  WORD OF RECALL is worth casting now.  It always goes to the Temple
  whatever you have set your own recall to, so a cleric has two places to
  reach in a hurry instead of one, and it works while you are cursed,
  where the RECALL skill does not.  A scroll or potion of recall carries
  the same spell and does the same two things.

  Shops take the coins you actually have.  Buying used to look only at the
  loose copper in your purse, so a character carrying platinum could be
  told they could not afford a loaf of bread.  Shops now make change, and
  they pay for what you sell them in coins you can carry.

  Prices in low-level areas are priced for low-level characters.  Mud
  School sells bread for a copper and a meal for sixteen; a full set of
  leather in Dresden is a few gold rather than a few platinum.  Nothing
  above level 10 changed.

23-25 Sep 2026
  AQUEST INFO, POINTS, TIME and GAMBLE work wherever you are standing;
  only the questmaster's business needs a questmaster.  Every subcommand
  can be shortened to its first letter.  Characters under level 4 can be
  given a quest at last, and the questmaster now names an area you would
  recognise instead of reading out its level range.

  REMORT no longer quietly costs you armour.  Gear you keep on through a
  remort keeps its bonuses.  Remorting also works if your password has a
  capital letter in it, which it did not before.

  REPAIR works on gear you are wearing, quotes its price in coins, and
  restores an item's armour and worth as well as its condition.

  Travel directions to every area in the game are published on the website
  at toc.jeremybean.com, and in the Routes panel of the browser client.

  Hyrule lets you recall out of it, except from inside the nine dungeons,
  and its overworld no longer goes pitch dark at sunset.

Sep 2026
  Prices are counted in copper.  LIST and APPRAISE write them in the coins
  they will cost you - 5g 20s, 1c, free - instead of one long number.

  The necromancer's undead line works: CREATE SKELETON, CREATE ZOMBIE and
  the rest.  ANIMATE PARTS no longer needs a body part, and hits harder
  when it has one.

  CAST no longer needs quotes around a spell with a space in its name.

  Rooms built in game survive a reboot.

See also: NEWS, MOTD, RECALL, AQUEST, REMORT, REPAIR
~

0 MOTD~
Syntax: motd

Reprints the message of the day that is shown each time you log in.
The MOTD is used to announce urgent news, maintenance warnings, or
temporary game changes that affect all players.  If you miss it on
login you can review it at any time with this command.

See also: CHANGES, NEWS, RULES
~

0 NEWS~
Syntax: news

Shows recent news posts from the game staff.  News covers ongoing events,
story updates, policy announcements, and other information that is relevant
to all players.  Older entries scroll off as new ones are added.

See also: CHANGES, MOTD
~

0 RULES~
Syntax: rules

Displays the official game rules and player conduct policies.  All players
are expected to read and follow these rules.  Ignorance of the rules is
not an accepted excuse for violations.  Contact an immortal if you have
questions about how a rule applies to a specific situation.

See also: WIZLIST, CREDITS
~

0 STORY~
Syntax: story

Recounts the background lore and history of the game world.  Reading the
story gives context for the setting, the major factions, and the events
that brought the world to its current state.  Useful for new players or
anyone who wants to understand the roleplaying background.

See also: CHANGES, NEWS
~

0 LORE~
Syntax: lore <object>

Researches a carried object and gives approximate weight, value, combat
properties, magical effects, and spell charges. Higher Lore skill improves
the chance of recognizing every spell on a multi-spell item. Research may
cost gold based on item level; skill and character level reduce that fee,
possibly to zero for simple items.

See also: COMPARE, IDENTIFY, INVENTORY
~

0 AFK~
Syntax: afk

Toggles an "away from keyboard" flag on your character so other players
know you are temporarily unavailable.  While AFK, auto-reply to tells is
suppressed and your name may display differently in WHO.  Type AFK again
to clear the flag when you return.  The flag does not persist across
logins.

See also: TELL, REPLY, WHO
~

0 DAMAGENUMBERS~
Syntax: damagenumbers

Toggles numerical combat feedback on or off.  When enabled, the exact
damage dealt or received is shown in brackets after each hit, for example:
  Your slash devastates the orc! [-47]
  The orc claws you! [-12]
Bystanders in the room always see only the descriptive message regardless
of their own setting.  The flag is saved across logins.

See also: SCORE, REPORT
~

0 CRAWL~
Syntax: crawl <thing>

Crawls through something in the room -- a hole, a tunnel, a gap under a
wall -- and out the other side.  Not every hole leads anywhere; one that
does not answers "Nothing seems to happen."  These ways through are not
listed among a room's exits, so read the room description and LOOK at
what it mentions.

Example: crawl hole

See also: CLIMB, JUMP, ENTER, LOOK
~

0 JUMP~
Syntax: jump <thing>

Jumps up or down something in the room -- a ledge, a pit, a chasm -- to
wherever it leads.  As with CRAWL and CLIMB, these ways through are part
of the room's scenery rather than its exits, so read the description.

Example: jump pit

See also: CRAWL, CLIMB, ENTER, LOOK
~

0 DELET DELETE~
Syntax: delet
Syntax: delete <password>

DELET is a safety guard for character deletion.  First type DELET alone to
arm the command, then confirm with DELETE <yourpassword>.  Both steps must be
done in sequence to complete the deletion.

Deletion is permanent.  The character's name becomes available for other
players and will not be restored by immortals.  All equipment and bank gold
are permanently lost.

See also: PASSWORD, QUIT, SAVE
~

0 DAMAGENUMBERS DAMAGE~
Syntax: damagenumbers

Toggles the exact damage figure alongside each combat message. With it off
you see "You slash the goblin hard" and nothing more; with it on you also
see the number behind that description.

Off for new characters. The wording on its own tells you how hard you hit
relative to your own attacks, so the numbers are for people who want to
compare weapons and tune gear rather than read a fight.

See also: NEW, AUTOLIST, CONFIG
~

0 NEW SETTINGS CUSTOMIZE OPTIONS~
Everything you can change about how the game reads. None of it affects the
world -- only what your own screen shows you.

Display
  COLOR           turn colour on or off, and pick which colours
  PROMPT          the status line at the bottom; PROMPT CODES lists the
                  placeholders, PROMPT DEFAULT puts it back
  BRIEF           short room descriptions once you know your way around
  COMPACT         drop the blank line between messages
  DAMAGENUMBERS   exact damage figures in combat (off by default)
  AUTOEXIT        list the exits every time you enter a room

Automatic actions
  AUTOLOOT        take from corpses you kill
  AUTOGOLD        take coins from corpses you kill
  AUTOSAC         sacrifice the corpse afterwards
  AUTOSPLIT       share coins with your group
  AUTOASSIST      join a fight your group is already in
  WIMPY           flee automatically below a set number of hit points

Channels and privacy
  CHANNELS        which channels you are listening to
  NOSUMMON        stop other players summoning you (on by default)
  NOFOLLOW        stop other players following you

Type HELP followed by any of those for the detail. Your settings are saved
with your character, so you only need to do this once.

See also: DAMAGENUMBERS, PROMPT, COLOR, CHANNELS, SCORE
~

0 PRACTICELIST~
Syntax: practicelist

Lists every guildmaster your class and guild allow you to use, and for each
one the skills you already know that they can practise you in -- which is
exactly what PRACTICE will accept from them. Your current percentage is
shown beside each, and your remaining practice sessions at the end.

A skill you have just gained sits at 1% until somebody raises it, and
nothing else in the game tells you who can.

See also: PRACTICE, GAINLIST, GAIN, GROUPS
~

0 GAINLIST~
Syntax: gainlist

Lists every guildmaster your class and guild allow you to use, and what
each one can sell you. The cost column shows the training cost, or:

  have    you already have it
  n/a     it cannot be gained at your class

See also: GAIN, PRACTICELIST, TRAIN, GROUPS
~

0 PROMPT~
Syntax: prompt
Syntax: prompt codes
Syntax: prompt <format string>
Syntax: prompt default
Syntax: prompt on | off
Syntax: prompt room on | off      (staff)

Customizes the status line shown at the bottom of your screen.

PROMPT on its own shows the prompt you are using now and what else you can
do with the command.  PROMPT CODES lists every placeholder, so you do not
have to come back to this help file to build one.

The codes are %h and %H for current and maximum hit points, %m and %M for
mana, %v and %V for movement, %e for experience to level, %X for total
experience, %g for gold, %s for silver, %r for the room name, %c and %C for
your regular and highlight colours, and %% for a literal percent sign.
Everything else is printed as you typed it.

%R shows the room vnum and works for staff only; in a mortal's prompt it
renders as nothing.

  prompt %C<%hhp %mm %vmv>%c     the default
  prompt <%h/%H hp %m/%M mana>   current and maximum together
  prompt <%hhp %mm %vmv> [%R]    with the room vnum on the end

PROMPT DEFAULT restores the standard prompt.  PROMPT OFF hides the prompt
entirely and PROMPT ON brings it back.

Staff can use PROMPT ROOM ON and PROMPT ROOM OFF to add or remove the room
vnum without retyping the whole format.  Anyone can use %R directly.

See also: COLOR, COMPACT, BRIEF, HOLYLIGHT
~

0 QUESTION~
Syntax: question
Syntax: question <message>

Toggles or uses the Q/A channel.  With no message, the command toggles your
subscription to the Q/A channel.  With a message, it broadcasts your question
to everyone listening on that channel so other players can help.

See also: GOSSIP, TELL, CHANNELS
~

0 BANK~
There is no BANK command: banking is four commands, and all of them work
only inside a bank room.  Entering one tells you that you are in a bank.

  BALANCE                       shows what you have on deposit
  DEPOSIT <amount> [coin type]  pays coins in (platinum if no type given)
  WITHDRAW <amount> [coin type] takes coins out, if you can carry them
  CONVERT                       changes your carried coins into the
                                largest denominations they make

Notes:
  - The bank pays 0.25% interest per real day on your balance.  Interest
    is applied automatically while you are logged in (catching up for up
    to 7 days of offline time).  Accounts below 1 platinum do not earn
    interest.
  - No fees are charged on deposits or withdrawals.
  - Successful deposits and withdrawals save your character immediately.
  - Where a banker stands behind the counter, pkillers are refused service.
  - Your bank balance is retained across deaths and remorts.

See also: BALANCE, DEPOSIT, WITHDRAW, CONVERT, WORTH
~

0 BALANCE~
Syntax: balance

Shows your current bank account balance in all denominations: platinum,
gold, silver, and copper.  You must be inside a bank room.

See also: BANK, DEPOSIT, WITHDRAW, WORTH
~

0 DEPOSIT~
Syntax: deposit <amount> [platinum|gold|silver|copper]

Moves coins from your inventory into your bank account.  An optional coin
type may be specified; if omitted, platinum is assumed.  You must be inside
a bank room.

Examples:
  deposit 100 gold
  deposit 500 platinum

See also: BANK, WITHDRAW, BALANCE, CONVERT
~

0 WITHDRAW~
Syntax: withdraw <amount> [platinum|gold|silver|copper]

Retrieves coins from your bank account into your inventory.  Same syntax
as DEPOSIT.  You must have room in your inventory to carry the coins and
must be inside a bank room.

See also: BANK, DEPOSIT, BALANCE
~

0 CONVERT~
Syntax: convert

Exchanges all your carried coins upward to the highest denomination
possible (copper -> silver -> gold -> platinum).  Handy before depositing
to keep your inventory tidy.  Must be inside a bank room.

See also: BANK, DEPOSIT, BALANCE
~

0 CASINO LUCKY DRAGON GAMBLING~
The Lucky Dragon Casino is located in Dresden's Market Plaza -- head south
from the plaza to find the gilded entrance doors.

Games available:
  SLOTS     -- Three-reel slot machine (cost: 1 gold per pull)
  BET       -- Hi/Lo dice game (1-100,000 gold per bet)
  ROULETTE  -- European single-zero roulette wheel (1-100,000 gold per bet)
  POKER     -- Video poker, dealt once, paid on hand rank (5-200 gold)

Type the game's name alone for full payout tables and instructions.
See also: SLOTS, BET, ROULETTE, POKER, BANK
~

0 SLOTS~
Syntax: slots

Play the three-reel slot machine in the casino.  Costs 1 gold per pull.

  Symbols: SEVEN, BAR, CHERRY, LEMON, ORANGE, GRAPE
  Payouts:
    Three SEVENS -- Jackpot!  Win 100 gold
    Three BARs   -- Win 50 gold
    Three of any -- Win 20 gold
    Two matching -- Win 3 gold
    Any CHERRY   -- Get your 1 gold back
    No match     -- Lose 1 gold

You must be inside the Lucky Dragon Casino to use this command.

See also: CASINO, BET, ROULETTE, POKER
~

0 BET~
Syntax: bet <amount> hi
Syntax: bet <amount> lo

Hi/Lo dice game at the casino.  Place a wager of 1 to 100,000 gold on the
outcome of two dice.  Bets above 500 gold require confirmation.

  HI: wins if the two-dice total is MORE than 7  (pays 1:1)
  LO: wins if the two-dice total is LESS than 7  (pays 1:1)
  Exactly 7: the house always wins.

Examples:
  bet 50 hi
  bet 200 lo

You must be inside the Lucky Dragon Casino to use this command.

See also: CASINO, SLOTS, ROULETTE, POKER
~

0 ROULETTE~
Syntax: roulette <amount> <bet>

European single-zero roulette at the casino.  Place a bet of 1 to 100,000
gold.  Bets above 500 gold require confirmation.

  EVEN-MONEY bets (1:1): red  black  even  odd  low(1-18)  high(19-36)
  DOZEN bets      (2:1): first(1-12)  second(13-24)  third(25-36)
  STRAIGHT-UP    (35:1): any single number from 0 to 36

  Zero (0) is green and loses all even-money and dozen bets.

Examples:
  roulette 100 red
  roulette 50 17
  roulette 200 first
  roulette 25 odd

You must be inside the Lucky Dragon Casino to use this command.

See also: CASINO, SLOTS, BET, POKER
~

0 POKER~
Syntax: poker <amount>

Video poker at the casino.  A hand of five cards is dealt from a standard
52-card deck.  The hand is evaluated immediately; there is no hold mechanic.
Minimum bet: 5 gold.  Maximum bet: 200 gold.

  Payout table (net multiple of wager):
    Royal Flush     (A-K-Q-J-10 same suit) -- 100x
    Straight Flush  (5 consecutive, flush)  --  20x
    Four of a Kind                          --  10x
    Full House      (trips + pair)          --   7x
    Flush           (5 same suit)           --   5x
    Straight        (5 consecutive)         --   3x
    Three of a Kind                         --   2x
    Two Pair                                --   1x
    Jacks or Better (pair J/Q/K/A)         --  push (bet returned)
    Anything else                           --  lose

Cards are shown as rank + suit letter, e.g. "Ac Kc Qc Jc 10c" = royal flush.
Suits: c=clubs  d=diamonds  h=hearts  s=spades

You must be inside the Lucky Dragon Casino to use this command.

See also: CASINO, SLOTS, BET, ROULETTE
~

0 BREW CONCOCT~
Syntax: brew <herb>
Syntax: concoct <component> <component>

BREW turns one carried herb into an herbal tea. CONCOCT combines two carried
spell components into a potion when they form a valid recipe. Component order
does not matter. Failed work on a valid recipe consumes its ingredients;
unknown combinations are left untouched. Both commands require the matching
crafting skill.

See also: SCRIBE, QUAFF
~

0 SCRIBE~
Syntax: scribe <component> <component> <component>

SCRIBE combines three carried spell components into a single-use magical
scroll when they form a valid recipe. Component order does not matter, and
you do not need a separate blank scroll or quill. Failed work on a valid
recipe consumes its ingredients; unknown combinations are left untouched.
The resulting scroll is cast at your current level.

See also: BREW, RECITE
~

0 SHOOT~
Syntax: shoot <target>

SHOOT fires a wielded bow at a visible mobile through an open adjacent exit.
You must know the ARCHERY skill and cannot shoot while already fighting.
Successful and missed shots can improve ARCHERY.  The current bow system does
not require or consume a separate ammunition object, and it does not support
ranged attacks against players.

Hyrule's own bow and boomerangs shoot at an enemy in the same room, and need
no ARCHERY.  The bow wants a quiver of arrows carried, and each arrow costs
a rupee; a boomerang comes back to your hand and stuns what it hits.  See
HYRULE ITEMS.

Hyrule's Silver Arrow is a special exception.  A level 54 or higher character
who has it wielded can use SHOOT GANON in Ganon's room after he reaches one hit
point and flashes bright red.  This finishing shot does not require ARCHERY
and does not consume the Silver Arrow.  Normal attacks can wound Ganon but
cannot deliver the final blow.

See also: ARCHERY, WIELD, SCAN
~

0 HYRULE DUNGEONS 'HYRULE ORDER'~
Hyrule is entered through the arcade cabinet in the Games Room, on the
university campus.  ENTER CABINET and the screen takes you.  RECALL works
on the overworld but not inside the nine numbered dungeons.

The nine dungeons are done IN ORDER, and every one works the same way:
  - you need the previous dungeon's Triforce piece to enter it;
  - inside, the way to its guardian needs the previous dungeon's treasure;
  - the guardian drops a key.  UNLOCK the door behind it, then UNLOCK and
    OPEN the chest there: it holds this dungeon's piece and its treasure.
Carry what you need in hand, worn or in a bag (not the stash).

  Level  Levels  To enter     Before the guardian   Chest holds
  1      2-8     --           --                    piece 1, boomerang
  2      8-14    piece 1      a boomerang           piece 2, Mag. Boomerang
  3      14-20   piece 2      Mag. Boomerang        piece 3, raft
  4      20-27   piece 3+raft the raft              piece 4, stepladder
  5      27-33   piece 4      the stepladder        piece 5, bow
  6      33-40   piece 5      the bow               piece 6, Recorder
  7      40-46   piece 6      the Recorder          piece 7, Red Candle
  8      46-52   piece 7      the Red Candle        piece 8, Magical Key
  9      53-59   pieces 1-8   the Magical Key       piece 9, Master Sword

Each guardian is a hard fight for its band: alone, plan to be well above
it or bring friends.  A lost piece or treasure comes back from the same
chest after the next reset, when the guardian has a new key.  Pieces cannot
be dropped, given or sold.  COMBINE TRIFORCE joins all nine.  Each dungeon's
map and compass show you the way (HYRULE ITEMS).

See also: GANON, COMBINE, RECALL, HYRULE ITEMS, HYRULE SECRETS, HEARTS
~

0 GANON RELICS 'MASTER SWORD' TRIFORCE 'RED RING'~
Ganon is a fight for a group.  He blinks about the dark hurling fireballs
that no parry stops, and his wounds close if he is left alone.  He can be
wounded normally, but at one hit point he flashes red, becomes stunned, and
can only be finished by a level 54 or higher character wielding the Silver
Arrow and typing SHOOT GANON.  The Silver Arrow lies in one of Death
Mountain's cellars; a group does best with two.

The Red Ring of Hyrule (level 58) lies in Death Mountain's other cellar.
Worn, it gives sanctuary and takes a fifth off all damage you take.

Ganon carries the Golden Key, his crown and his trident, and leaves one
random relic:

  Hero's Tunic, level 54 - heals after personally killing an NPC
  Blue Ring of Hyrule, level 54 - reduces all incoming damage by 10 percent
  Pegasus Boots, level 55 - walking on foot costs 25 percent fewer moves
  Mirror Shield, level 56 - reduces nonphysical damage by 15 percent

Only the strongest Blue or Red Ring ward applies, even if two rings are worn.
The Mirror Shield can combine with one Ring ward.  EXAMINE each relic for its
exact limits, and COMPARE understands these special effects.

The Golden Key opens Zelda's chamber and Ganon's great chest in it, which
holds the ninth piece, the Triforce of Power, and the Master Sword (level
58): the finest blade a mortal can carry, which hastens its wielder.

With all nine pieces, COMBINE TRIFORCE makes The Triforce itself (level
58).  WEAR it and it takes your light slot, and you see what a level 59
hero with HOLYLIGHT would see: the invisible, the hidden and the dark --
but not gods hidden above that.  It counts as every piece at every door.

See also: HYRULE, SHOOT, COMBINE, COMPARE, EXAMINE
~

0 PICK~
Syntax: pick <direction>
Syntax: pick <container>

PICK attempts to open a lock on a door or container using lockpicks and
the PICK LOCK skill.  Success opens the lock without a key.  Difficulty
depends on the lock's strength and your skill level.  A failed attempt
may alert nearby mobs.

See also: OPEN, LOCK, UNLOCK, STEAL
~

0 JUNK~
Syntax: junk <object>

JUNK destroys an unwanted object from your inventory, permanently removing
it from the game.  Once junked, an item cannot be recovered.  Use JUNK to
clear inventory space without dropping items on the ground.

See also: SACRIFICE, DROP, DONATE
~

0 BACKSTAB BS~
Syntax: backstab <victim>
Syntax: bs <victim>

Strike an enemy from hiding, delivering a single devastating blow before
they can react.  The attack requires a piercing weapon and that you are
hidden when you initiate it.  Damage is multiplied based on your backstab
skill level; a higher skill means a deeper wound.  You are automatically
revealed after the strike whether it succeeds or fails.  You cannot
backstab a target you are already fighting.

See also: HIDE, SNEAK
~

0 BASH~
Syntax: bash <victim>

Hurl yourself into an enemy with full body force, knocking them to the
ground.  A successful bash interrupts your foe's next action and leaves
them vulnerable to follow-up attacks for a brief moment.  Failure means
you stumble and lose your own next action instead.  Two-handed and large
weapons give a better chance to knock the target down; smaller frames are
easier to topple than giants or quadrupeds.

See also: TRIP, KICK
~

0 BERSERK~
Syntax: berserk

Enter a battle frenzy that sharpens your instinct to kill at the cost of
your defenses.  While berserk, you deal more damage and gain offensive
bonuses, but your armor class worsens and you cannot use skills that require
calm or concentration.  The frenzy sustains itself while you are fighting
and fades shortly after combat ends.  Only warriors with sufficient martial
training can enter a true berserk.

See also: KICK, BASH
~

0 BLINDING~
Syntax: blinding <victim>

A precision strike or magical technique aimed directly at an enemy's eyes.
On a failed save the target is blinded, suffering significant penalties to
hit chance and losing the ability to see room descriptions or other
characters clearly.  The blindness lasts for a limited duration and can be
cured by spells or natural recovery.

See also: DIRT, CONFUSE, STUNNING
~

0 CONFUSE~
Syntax: confuse <victim>

A psionic assault that muddles the target's thoughts, causing them to act
erratically.  A confused foe may attack random targets, forget commands,
or simply stand dazed for the duration.

Holding your concentration is the only limit on a practiced psion.  At
full skill the intrusion reaches any mind that is not specifically warded
against mental attack.  A target immune to mental damage can never be
confused, and a mentally resistant target gets a single bounded chance to
throw the intrusion off.  A normal mind cannot resist it at all.

See also: BLINDING, NIGHTMARE, TORMENT
~

0 CRANE~
Syntax: crane

Toggle the crane fighting stance.  CRANE adopts a balanced, evasive style
that improves your dodge and parry while sacrificing some raw damage output.
Suitable for characters who prefer to outlast their foes rather than
overwhelm them quickly.  Requires the crane skill.  Entering combat at full
speed or running out of movement breaks the stance.

See also: FISTS, IRON, ROLL, KICK
~

0 FISTS~
Syntax: fists

Toggle the iron-fist fighting stance.  FISTS adopts an aggressive,
forward-pressing style with emphasis on raw hand-to-hand damage.  You deal
more damage unarmed but take more hits in return.  Best used when you intend
to end the fight quickly.  Requires the fists skill.

See also: CRANE, BERSERK, KICK
~

0 DIRT DIRT-KICK~
Syntax: dirt <victim>

Scoop dirt, sand, or debris from the ground and fling it into an enemy's
eyes.  On a failed save the target is blinded temporarily, lowering their
to-hit chance significantly.  The technique costs no mana and relies on
your DIRT KICK skill; a higher skill means a better chance to land and a
longer duration.  Only works in outdoor or appropriate terrain rooms.

See also: BLINDING, BASH, TRIP
~

0 DISARM~
Syntax: disarm

Attempt to knock your opponent's weapon from their grip during combat.
A successful disarm sends the weapon flying to the floor, forcing the
enemy to fight barehanded until they pick it up or draw another.  The
chance to succeed is influenced by your disarm skill, relative weapon
sizes, and your opponent's dexterity.  You must be in combat and wielding
a weapon to attempt it.

Two things stop a disarm outright.  A weapon under BEWITCH WEAPON cannot
be taken from its owner's hand at all.  A warrior/warrior with HEROIC GRIP
may hold on to theirs, and once fully practiced always will.

See also: BASH, TRIP, HEROIC GRIP, BEWITCH WEAPON
~

0 "HEROIC GRIP" HEROICGRIP HEROIC GRIP~
Syntax: (passive -- there is nothing to type)

Warrior/warrior only.  Available from level 35.

A warrior who has spent their whole career with a weapon in hand learns
to keep it there.  Heroic grip is a chance, every time somebody tries to
disarm you, that the weapon simply does not leave your hand.  When you are
fully practiced in it, you cannot be disarmed.

  You wrench at Karg's weapon, but he grips it like iron!

This is nothing like BEWITCH WEAPON, and it is worth knowing the
difference.  Bewitch curses the weapon so it cannot be removed at all --
by anyone, including you, until the curse is lifted.  Heroic grip does
nothing to the weapon.  You are just holding on.  REMOVE and WIELD work
exactly as they always did, and you can change weapons mid-fight.

It works only for you.  There is no way to put it on a weapon, on a
follower, or on anyone else, and it does not stack with anything.

Dual wielding is covered.  Nothing in the world reaches for your off hand,
and the grip answers for the hand that anything does reach for -- including
creatures that knock a weapon loose without calling it a disarm.

Raising it:

  A guildmaster will take you a good way, and quickly -- further per
  session than most skills.

  Past that point there is nothing left to teach.  The rest is earned in
  a fight, by having your weapon grabbed at and keeping hold of it, and
  it is a long road.  Every attempt against you is a chance to improve,
  whether you keep the weapon or lose it.

Who trains it:

  Vladamir the veteran   -- the warrior guild's own master, and the only
                            one who will teach it to you in the first place
  Dolonar the blacksmith -- practice
  Breark the troll       -- practice

Every warrior/warrior already has heroic grip, including characters made
before it existed.  You do not need to go and find it; you need to go and
practice it.

See also: DISARM, BEWITCH WEAPON, PRACTICE, PRACTICELIST, GAIN
~

0 DISMOUNT~
Syntax: dismount

Climb down from your current mount, returning to normal movement.  If
you are thrown from or killed while mounted, dismounting happens
automatically.  Some rooms or area transitions may also force a dismount
if mounted travel is not permitted there.

See also: MOUNT, RIDE
~

0 DOORBASH~
Syntax: doorbash <direction>

Charge a closed or locked door with brute force, attempting to break it
open without a key.  Success destroys the door; failure leaves you
winded for a short period.  The chance to succeed depends on your
strength, the BASH DOOR skill, and the door's construction.  Magically
sealed or wizard-locked exits cannot be doorbashed.

See also: BASH, PICK
~



0 HIT~
Syntax: hit <victim>

HIT is an alias for the KILL command and starts or continues combat
against the chosen target.  Use it when you prefer not to type "kill".
HIT does not carry the additional PK logging that MURDER does.

See also: KILL, MURDER, BACKSTAB
~

0 MURDER MURDE~
Syntax: murder <victim>
Syntax: murde <victim>

MURDER (and its abbreviation MURDE) explicitly flags your action as a
hostile assault against a player character.  Unlike HIT or KILL, MURDER
is always logged and counts as a pk-flagged attack.  Against mobs it
functions identically to KILL.  Use with care in populated areas.

See also: HIT, KILL, PKILL
~

0 KICK~
Syntax: kick <victim>

A quick boot to disrupt an enemy or deal extra damage during combat.
Kick costs no mana and triggers on its own timer, separate from your
main weapon attacks.  Damage scales with your KICK skill and your
strength.  The attack can occasionally knock a smaller or off-balance
opponent back a step, delaying their next action.

See also: TRIP, NERVE, BASH
~

0 MINDBLAST MIND BLAST~
Syntax: mindblast <victim>

Unleash a raw burst of psionic energy at a single target.  The blast
deals direct mental damage and, on a failed save, leaves the victim
disoriented: their attacks may miss wildly for a short time.  Mindblast
draws from your mana pool and scales with your psionic skill level.  It
can be used freely in or out of combat and does not require line-of-sight
to a room exit.

See also: CONFUSE, CLAIRVOYANCE, TELEKINESIS
~

0 NERVE~
Syntax: nerve <victim>

Strike a precise nerve cluster to temporarily cripple an opponent.  On a
successful hit the target suffers reduced movement or attack speed,
slowing their ability to act in combat.  The technique requires hand-to-
hand training or knowledge of anatomy; the higher your skill the more
effective and longer-lasting the effect.

See also: KICK, TRIP, BASH
~

0 NIGHTMARE~
Syntax: nightmare <victim>

Plant a waking nightmare in your target's mind, flooding their perception
with fear and confusion.  Victims who fail the mental save cower, flee,
or act irrationally for the duration.  The spell is psionic in nature and
is resisted by mental fortitude.  It is especially devastating against
foes with low willpower or those already suffering other mental effects.

See also: CONFUSE, TORMENT, ENERVATE
~

0 RESCUE~
Syntax: rescue <ally>

Leap into the fray to defend an ally under attack, drawing that enemy's
aggression onto yourself.  A successful rescue shifts the attacker's
focus from your ally to you, giving your companion a moment to recover or
flee.  The maneuver requires the RESCUE skill and works best when you
have higher combat stats than the enemy.  You cannot rescue someone if
you are already engaged with a different opponent.

See also: SHIELD BLOCK, BASH, FLEE
~

0 SMITE~
Syntax: smite <victim>

Channel divine or magical wrath through your weapon in a single brutal
strike.  The smite deals additional holy or elemental damage on top of
your normal weapon hit, and on a failed save the target is briefly
stunned or staggered.  Smiting consumes mana and requires that you are
wielding a weapon attuned to the ability.

See also: STUNNING, BASH, BLINDING
~

0 STUNNING~
Syntax: stunning <victim>

A focused blow or magical technique designed to leave a target senseless.
On a failed save the victim is stunned: they cannot act, lose their
dexterity bonus, and are easier to hit for the duration.  The chance to
land and the stun length both improve with your skill level and the
victim's current condition.

See also: BASH, SMITE, BLINDING
~

0 TORMENT~
Syntax: torment <victim>

Inflict sustained psychic or magical pain on a target, causing them to
writhe in agony.  Tormented victims suffer a damage-over-time effect
and penalties to concentration, making spellcasting or skill use risky.
The effect stacks poorly with other pain-inflicting debuffs.  Mental
and magical resistances reduce both the initial application chance and
the recurring damage.

See also: NIGHTMARE, ENERVATE, CONFUSE
~

0 TRIP~
Syntax: trip <victim>

Hook or sweep a foot to send your opponent stumbling to the ground.  A
tripped target falls prone, losing their next attack and suffering an
armor-class penalty until they stand.  Trip works best against bipedal
targets; quadrupeds and very large creatures are much harder to topple.
Requires the TRIP skill; failure makes you lose footing yourself briefly.

See also: BASH, KICK, NERVE
~

0 AQUEST~
Syntax: aquest <info|points|time|gamble>          anywhere
Syntax: aquest <request|complete|list|buy|abort>  at a questmaster

Every subcommand may be abbreviated to its first letter, and AQUEST on its
own prints the list.  Checking on yourself works wherever you are standing;
the rest is business with the questmaster and needs one in the room.

AQUEST INFO shows the active objective and time remaining, or your cooldown,
quest points, pending gamble, and win streak. Suitable targets are selected
from live, level-appropriate mobiles in rooms your character may enter.

Every level may quest, immortals included. A target is never above your
own level, so above the mortal cap that ceiling stops binding and the
whole world is fair game.

Kill credit includes eligible group members present with the final attacker,
including kills finished by a controlled pet. Recovery items are bound to the
assigned player, may be carried inside a container, and disappear when the
quest ends. Completed quests may be turned in to any questmaster for gold and
a pending quest-point reward.

Resolve that reward with AQUEST GAMBLE YES for a 50% double-or-nothing chance,
or AQUEST GAMBLE NO to collect it safely.

CONTRACTS
  Most quests allow 10 to 30 minutes. About one in five is a RUSH CONTRACT:
  5 to 8 minutes for double the base point reward. About one in twenty is an
  EMERGENCY CONTRACT: five minutes flat for five times the base reward. A
  quest is one or the other, never both.

STREAKS
  Consecutive completions add a point bonus that keeps growing: 10% for each
  of the first five, then 5% each to fifteen, 3% each to thirty, 2% each to
  fifty, and 1% each after that, to a ceiling of 300%. Aborting, timing out,
  or quitting during an active quest breaks the streak and loses all of it.

See also: QUEST, TOPTEN
~

0 TOPTEN~
Syntax: topten

Displays the current leaderboard of notable characters ranked by pkill
score, quest points, or other tracked statistics depending on the current
season or game mode.  Use it to see how you compare with other players
and to identify top-ranked targets or allies.

See also: AQUEST, SCORE
~

0 SEARCH~
Syntax: search

Actively examines the current room for concealed doors, hidden exits, or
lurking traps.  A successful search reveals the object and describes it
briefly.  Your chance to find something depends on your intelligence,
the SEARCH skill level, and how well hidden the object is.  Some things
can only be discovered through careful searching, not normal movement.

See also: TRACK, HIDE, SNEAK
~

0 TRACK~
Syntax: track <target>

Attempt to follow the scent trail or footprints left by a character
across rooms.  If sign is found you will automatically move one step
in the direction the target went.  Repeat the command to keep following.
The skill degrades with distance, time, and weather; rain or magic can
erase a trail entirely.  NPCs with the HUNTER flag track back at you
automatically.

See also: SEARCH, HUNT
~

0 HIDE~
Syntax: hide

Attempt to conceal yourself in the shadows of the current room.  While
hidden you do not appear on the room's character list to non-sneaking
players and most mobs.  Moving, attacking, or casting a spell breaks
hiding immediately.  The chance to hide successfully depends on your
HIDE skill and the lighting level in the room.

See also: SNEAK, STEALTH, STEEL, BACKSTAB
~

0 SNEAK~
Syntax: sneak

Toggle silent movement.  While sneaking, your footsteps do not generate
entry or exit messages in adjacent rooms, making it harder for players
and mobs to notice you passing through.  Sneaking costs extra movement
per step and requires the SNEAK skill.  The toggle turns off if you run
out of movement or enter combat.

See also: HIDE, STEALTH, STEAL
~

0 STEALTH~
Syntax: stealth

Toggle the enhanced stealth mode.  STEALTH combines elements of hiding
and sneaking into a sustained stance that makes you harder to detect
overall, including by mobs that might see through ordinary hiding.
It requires a higher skill investment than SNEAK or HIDE alone and
drains movement steadily while active.

See also: HIDE, SNEAK, STEEL
~

0 STEEL~
Syntax: steel

Toggle the STEEL stealth stance.  An advanced self-concealment technique
that renders you nearly invisible to passive detection by other players
and mobs.  STEEL is distinct from HIDE in that it persists through slow
movement and requires significantly higher skill to maintain.  It cancels
if you attack, cast, or are struck.

See also: HIDE, SNEAK, STEALTH
~

0 LEVITATE~
Syntax: levitate

Toggle psionic levitation.  While levitating you float just above the
ground, allowing you to pass over certain terrain hazards such as lava
flows, shallow water crossings, and floor-based traps.  Levitation
requires psionic training and a small continuous mana drain.  It offers
no protection against falling damage from great heights.

See also: FLY, PSIONICS, TELEKINESIS
~

0 STEAL~
Syntax: steal <object> <victim>

Attempt to take an object from another character's inventory without
their knowledge.  Success depends on your STEAL skill, the victim's
dexterity and awareness, and whether you are hidden or sneaking when
you attempt it.  A failed steal is always noticed by the target and
nearby bystanders, flagging you as a criminal.  You cannot steal from
players in safe rooms or during active combat.

See also: SNEAK, HIDE, PICK
~

0 SHOVE~
Syntax: shove <victim>

Push a target out of the room in a random or specified direction.  A
successful shove can separate enemies from allies, break formations, or
force a foe into a dangerous adjacent room.  The target gets a saving
throw based on their size and strength versus yours.  Shoving another
player counts as a hostile action.

See also: BASH, TRIP, DRAG
~

0 ROLL~
Syntax: roll

Toggle the ROLL defensive stance.  While in this stance you improve
your chance to dodge incoming melee attacks by moving with the blows
rather than taking them head-on.  The stance lowers your own offensive
accuracy slightly and requires the ROLL skill.  It disengages if you
are stunned, knocked prone, or run out of movement.

See also: IRON, BERSERK, CRANE
~

0 IRON~
Syntax: iron

Toggle the IRON defensive stance.  Brace yourself to absorb incoming
hits rather than avoid them.  IRON increases effective armor and
reduces damage from each blow at the cost of slower attack speed.
Particularly useful when tanking multiple enemies or absorbing burst
damage.  Requires the IRON skill; cancels if you move or are knocked
down.

See also: ROLL, BASH, CRANE
~

0 MINDBAR~
Syntax: mindbar

Build a powerful, temporary fortress around your own mind.  Mindbar halves
incoming mental damage.  It does not reduce physical or elemental damage and
does not make you immune to non-damaging mental effects.  Mindbar cannot be
combined with Psionic Armor or Psychic Shield.

See also: PSIONIC, PSYCHIC, PSIONICS, TRANSFUSION
~

0 PSIONIC~
Syntax: psionic [character]

Create temporary Psionic Armor around yourself or a character in the same
room.  The armor reduces incoming mental damage by one quarter.  It cannot be
combined with Mindbar or Psychic Shield.

See also: PSYCHIC, MINDBAR, PSIONICS
~

0 PSYCHIC~
Syntax: psychic

Raise a temporary Psychic Shield around every unprotected member of your
group in the room.  The shield reduces incoming mental damage by one quarter.
It does not replace an existing Mindbar, Psionic Armor, or Psychic Shield.

See also: PSIONIC, MINDBAR, PSIONICS
~

0 PSIONICS~
Psionics are mental disciplines available to characters who have remorted at
least twice (remort 2+).  Upon qualifying, you receive one randomly selected
skill from each of four disciplines, for four skills total.  At remort 5
(final), all 17 psionic skills are granted.

Psionic powers are practiced with Salir the Monk, who hovers in the
Study of the Sage in the Astral Plane.

PSIONIC DISCIPLINES

  Assault:     ego whip, torment, nightmare, mindblast

  Astral:      astral walk, shift, project, telekinesis (TK)

  Defense:     mindbar, psionic armor, psychic shield, transfusion

  Control:     clairvoyance, confuse, mind leech, enervate, pyrotechnics

PASSIVE BENEFITS
  Remort 2+: access to psionic skills; immune to hunger and thirst.
  Remort 4+: recall always succeeds (no skill check).
  Remort 5:  all 17 psionic skills granted; unlimited carry capacity.

RESOURCE COST
  Psionic skills draw from your mana pool when used.  Defensive powers last
  for a limited duration; they are not toggles and do not drain mana per tick.

See also: REMORT, MINDBAR, PSIONIC, PSYCHIC, TELEKINESIS, CLAIRVOYANCE,
          MINDBLAST, CONFUSE, NIGHTMARE, TORMENT, ENERVATE, TRANSFUSION,
          ASTRAL WALK, EGO WHIP
~

0 QUIT QUI~
Syntax: quit
Syntax: qui

Save your character and cleanly disconnect from the game.  QUI is a
shortened alias for QUIT that requires you to type the extra letter to
prevent accidental disconnection.  Both commands behave identically.

You cannot quit while in active combat.  Quitting in a non-safe room
is allowed but your character will remain visible in the room briefly
before fully departing.  All inventory, equipment, and bank balance are
saved automatically when you quit.

See also: SAVE, RECALL, AFK
~

62 IMMORTAL~
This help topic covers miscellaneous immortal tools.  Type WIZHELP for a
complete listing of all immortal commands available to your trust level.
For staff communication see IMMTALK (:) and WIZINFO.  Immortal channels
and flags are logged; all actions with LOG_ALWAYS are permanently recorded.

See also: WIZHELP, PSTAT, WIZCHECK, FINGER
~

62 BEEP~
Syntax: beep <immortal>

BEEP sends a ping-style private message to the named immortal regardless of
whether they have the immortal channel muted.  Use it to get the attention
of an AFK immortal without broadcasting to the whole staff channel.

See also: IMMTALK, WIZINFO
~

62 TRANSFUSION~
Syntax: transfusion <target>

TRANSFUSION is a psionic skill that lets you sacrifice a portion of your
own hitpoints to restore a target's health.  The amount transferred
depends on your psionic skill level and constitution.  Cannot be used
if it would reduce you below a safe minimum HP threshold.

See also: PSIONICS, MINDBAR
~

0 ALIAS ALIASES~
Syntax:
alias <word> <command as a string>     (sets the alias)
alias delete <alias to delete>         (deletes an alias)
alias <word>                           (shows an alias)
unalias <alias to delete>              (same as alias delete)

e.g.: alias mm cast 'magic missile'
      alias delete mm

To see all the aliases you have, type alias and to see a specific alias
type: alias <alias name>.

There is a max of 20 aliases per character.

An alias may hold up to 10 commands separated by semicolons.  They run in
order, one at a time, exactly as if you had typed them: a command that
leaves you waiting (a bash, a spell) makes the next one wait too.
Whatever you type after the alias goes on the end of its last command.

e.g.: alias prep wear all;get all corpse;sacrifice corpse
      alias kb kill;bash
      kb orc                  (kill, then bash orc)

Write \; for a semicolon that belongs inside a command rather than
between two.  The browser client splits what you type on semicolons
itself, so there type the alias with \; between its commands:
      alias ne north\;east

An alias cannot run another alias: a command that comes out of an alias
is run as written, even if it has an alias's name.  If one of its
commands shows you more than a screen, the next command ends the paging
just as typing it would.  Typing ! repeats the alias, not its last
command.

See also: COMMANDS, HELP
~

0 AREAS~
Syntax: areas

AREAS shows you a list of areas in the game.  Area names are shown with
recommended levels.  However, those recommendations are just a rough range
and it is a good idea to read road signs to determine if you are entering
somewhere that may be particularly dangerous.

AREAS lists everywhere that exists.  WALKTO lists everywhere there is a
published walking route to, with the distance to each.

See also: WALKTO, ROUTES, WHERE, RECALL
~

0 ARRIVE~
Syntax: arrive <string>

Sets the message that others see when you enter a room.  The message must
include $n (your name) and $T (the direction you arrived from).

  $T (arrive) = the north, the south, the east, the west, above, below

Example:  arrive $n strides in from $T.

See also: DEPART
~

0 DEPART~
Syntax: depart <string>

Sets the message that others see when you leave a room.  The message must
include $n (your name) and $t (the direction you are heading).

  $t (depart) = north, south, east, west, up, down

Example:  depart $n slips away to the $t.

See also: ARRIVE
~

0 AUTO AUTOLIST~
Syntax: auto
Syntax: autolist

AUTO (or AUTOLIST) displays the current status of all automatic feature
flags: AUTOLOOT, AUTOGOLD, AUTOSAC, AUTOEXIT, AUTOASSIST, AUTOSPLIT, as
well as PROMPT, COMBINE, NOFOLLOW, NOSUMMON, and NOLOOT.  When you create
a new character all automatic actions are on by default.

See also: AUTOLOOT, AUTOGOLD, AUTOSAC, AUTOEXIT, AUTOASSIST, AUTOSPLIT
~

0 AUTOLOOT~
Syntax: autoloot

Toggle automatic looting.  When on, you automatically try to pick up all loot
from mobiles you kill.  Anything you cannot carry remains in the corpse, and
AUTOSAC will not sacrifice a corpse while it still contains loot.

See also: AUTO, AUTOGOLD, AUTOSAC
~

0 AUTOGOLD~
Syntax: autogold

Toggle automatic currency collection.  When on without AUTOLOOT, all money is
automatically taken from mobiles you kill.  Other objects remain in the corpse.

See also: AUTO, AUTOLOOT, AUTOSPLIT
~

0 AUTOSAC~
Syntax: autosac

Toggle automatic corpse sacrifice.  When on, dead monster corpses are
automatically sacrificed after automatic looting.  Only empty corpses are
sacrificed; a corpse containing money, equipment, or anything you could not
carry is left in the room and you are told why.

See also: AUTO, AUTOLOOT, SACRIFICE
~

0 AUTOEXIT~
Syntax: autoexit

Toggle automatic exit display.  When on, visible room exits are shown
every time you enter a room, alongside the room description.

See also: AUTO, EXITS, BRIEF
~

0 AUTOASSIST~
Syntax: autoassist

Toggle automatic group assist.  When on, you automatically join combat
if another member of your group is attacked.

See also: AUTO, GROUP, RESCUE
~

0 AUTOSPLIT~
Syntax: autosplit

Toggle automatic gold splitting.  When on, gold looted from kills is
automatically split among all group members in the same room.

See also: AUTO, SPLIT, AUTOGOLD
~

0 BRANDISH~
Syntax: brandish

BRANDISH activates a magical staff you are holding.  You must HOLD the
staff first.  Staves have multiple charges; each brandish uses one.  When
all charges are exhausted the staff is consumed.  May require the STAVES
skill.

See also: ZAP, HOLD, RECITE, QUAFF
~

0 QUAFF~
Syntax: quaff <potion>

QUAFF drinks a magical potion, triggering its effects immediately.  This
is distinct from DRINK, which consumes mundane beverages.  Potions have a
single charge and are consumed on use.  No skill is required to quaff.

See also: BRANDISH, RECITE, ZAP, DRINK
~

0 RECITE~
Syntax: recite <scroll> [target]

RECITE reads from a magical scroll, casting the inscribed spell.  The
target argument is optional and depends on the nature of the scroll.
Scrolls have a single charge and are consumed on use.  May require the
SCROLLS skill.

See also: BRANDISH, QUAFF, ZAP
~

0 ZAP~
Syntax: zap <target>
Syntax: zap

ZAP fires a magical wand at a target.  You must HOLD the wand first.  If
no target is given and you are in combat, the current opponent is targeted
automatically.  Wands have multiple charges; each zap uses one.  When all
charges are exhausted the wand is consumed.  May require the WANDS skill.

See also: BRANDISH, RECITE, HOLD
~

5 TEACHLIST~
Syntax: teachlist

TEACHLIST shows a list of guildmasters and what they can teach you for
skills and spells.  Each entry shows the trainer, their location, and the
skill or spell they offer.  Some skills require that you first gain the
group they belong to before you can practice them.

Use GAINLIST to see which skill groups are available for purchase, and
PRACTICE to improve skills once you have learned them.

See also: GAINLIST, GAIN, PRACTICE, TRAIN
~

5 GAINLIST~
Syntax: gainlist

GAINLIST shows a list of guildmasters and the skill groups or individual
skills they will allow you to GAIN.  Gaining a skill or group costs training
sessions (not practice sessions).  For a description of each group, consult
HELP GROUPS.  For a list of who teaches individual skills once you have
gained them, see TEACHLIST.

See also: TEACHLIST, GAIN, GROUPS, TRAIN
~

69 BUILDING BUILD OLC~
Building rooms without leaving the game.

GOTO <vnum> to a number no room uses makes one on the spot.  SET ROOM
gives it a name, a description, flags and a sector.  RLINK connects it to
its neighbours, with doors, locks and secret ways.  RSAVE writes the lot
to disk, and until you type it nothing you built survives a reboot.

  goto 29200                       a room forms out of nothing
  set room 29200 name The Long Gallery
  set room 29200 desc A hall of cracked mirrors runs north.
  set room 29200 flags +AJ         indoors and no-mob
  set room 29200 sector 0          inside
  rlink north 29201                dig on, and the way back comes free
  rsave confirm                    keep it

Rooms you make this way belong to the builder area, which is written to
area/custom.are.  Anyone from level 69 up may build there freely, and
saving takes no more than RSAVE CONFIRM.

Rooms that came out of a shipped area file are a different matter.  Only
an implementor may change one at all, and saving over that area's file
takes a confirmation naming the file, so a stray RSAVE cannot rewrite
Hyrule because you happened to be standing in it.  The previous file is
kept beside it as a dated backup either way.

What will not save yet: rooms with a second flag word, river or teleport
rooms, and rooms carrying a room affect.  Those hold data outside the room
itself, and rather than write a file that has quietly lost it, RSAVE stops
and names the room.  Move your building elsewhere, or ask for the format
to be finished.

See also: RLINK, RSAVE, SET, GOTO, RSTAT
~

69 RLINK DIG~
Syntax: rlink <dir> <vnum>      dig an exit, and the way back
        rlink <dir> none        take the exit out again
        rlink <dir> open        a plain doorway, no door
        rlink <dir> door        a door that can be closed
        rlink <dir> pick        a door that cannot be picked
        rlink <dir> secret      a door that does not show in EXITS
        rlink <dir> key <vnum>  the object that unlocks it
        rlink <dir> name <word> what to call it: 'door gate'
        rlink <dir> desc <text> what you see looking that way

Directions: north east south west up down, and the four diagonals.

RLINK works on the room you are standing in.  Digging to a vnum nothing
uses makes that room as well, so you can lay out a run of rooms without
stopping.  The matching exit back is dug for you, unless the far room
already has one that way, or it came out of an area file and you are not
an implementor -- then the link is one way and RLINK says so.

A secret exit is one the loader recognises by its keyword, so RLINK SECRET
sets the keyword to 'secret' as well as the lock.  Add more words with
RLINK <dir> NAME SECRET <word>, keeping 'secret' first; drop it and the
exit stops being hidden the next time the area loads.

Nothing is written to disk until RSAVE.

See also: BUILDING, RSAVE, SET, RSTAT
~

69 RSAVE~
Syntax: rsave
        rsave confirm
        rsave confirm <filename>

RSAVE writes the rooms of the area you are standing in back to that area's
file.  Without it, everything built in game is gone at the next reboot.

RSAVE on its own writes nothing.  It reports the area, the file, and how
many rooms would be written, and tells you how to confirm.

In the builder area -- the rooms you made yourself, held in custom.are --
RSAVE CONFIRM is enough.

In an area that shipped with the game, you must be an implementor and you
must name the file: RSAVE CONFIRM HYRULE.ARE.  That is deliberate.  A room
you are standing in for some unrelated reason should not be savable by
reflex.

What gets written is the rooms as they are loaded right now, including
changes another immortal made this boot.  Mobiles, objects, resets, shops
and specials in the file are left exactly as they are.  The old file is
kept beside the new one with a timestamp, so a bad save can be undone from
the shell.

See also: BUILDING, RLINK, SET
~

70 DNS~
Syntax: dns
        dns on | off
        dns list
        dns <address or host name>

DNS with no argument reports whether hostname lookups are on.

DNS ON | OFF controls whether a connecting address is resolved to a host
name.  The lookup happens while the game is single-threaded, so a slow or
unreachable resolver stalls everything for as long as it takes -- during a
login, which is the worst moment for it.  Turning lookups off records the
numeric address instead and removes that stall.

The cost of off: bans written against host names will not match, because
there is no host name to match against.  Bans on numeric addresses still
work.  See BAN.

DNS LIST shows every connected descriptor with the host name the resolver
produced and the numeric address behind it, which is how you tell a stale
or spoofed-looking name from the address it really came from.

DNS <address> resolves one thing now: an IPv4 address gets a reverse
lookup, anything else a forward one.  This blocks the game for the duration
of the query, exactly as a connection would, so do not point it at
something you expect to hang.

See also: BAN, ALLOW, SOCKETS
~

70 GRANTPSI~
Syntax: grantpsi <player> [now] [skill1,skill2,...]

GRANTPSI is a level 70 tool to guarantee a player receives psionics. Without
"now" it flags the target for a 100%% success at the level-based psionic check
and optionally locks in a comma-separated list of psionic skills to award when
the check fires (default behavior rolls normally). Using "now" grants psionics
immediately, sends the unlock messaging, and applies the chosen skills right
away. Valid picks include: psionic armor, psychic shield, mindbar, torment,
ego whip, enervate, mindblast, clairvoyance, astral walk, shift, project,
telekinesis, transfusion, nightmare, mind leech, pyrotechnics, and confuse.

See also: PSIONICS, ADVANCE


The target does not have to be online.  With nobody connected by that
name the saved character is loaded, flagged, and written back; an
offline grant is always deferred to their next level check, because
"now" would apply to a copy that is discarded a moment later.
~

67 FSAVE~
Syntax: fsave

This command will force all the players to save, but doesn't send them a message
when you do it.  This command is also invoked on reboots and shutdowns.

See also: SOCKETS, RESTORECHAR

~

0 BRIEF~
Syntax: brief

If brief is on, you will see only short descriptions of rooms when you
enter them and when you type LOOK.  Typing brief once, turns brief on; typing
it again, turns it off, allowing you to see full descriptions.

The default is for brief to be off.

See also: COMPACT, COMBINE, PROMPT
~

0 BUY~
Syntax: buy <object>
Syntax: buy <n>.<object>

BUY purchases an object from a shopkeeper.  When multiple items of the
same name are available, prepend the position index: 'buy 2.sword' buys
the second sword in the list.

See also: LIST, SELL, VALUE
~

0 LIST~
Syntax: list
Syntax: list <name>

LIST shows the items a shopkeeper will sell you.  With a name argument,
only items matching that name are displayed.

See also: BUY, SELL, VALUE
~

0 SELL~
Syntax: sell <object>

SELL offers an object to a shopkeeper in exchange for gold.  The price is
shown before the transaction completes.  The shopkeeper may refuse items
they do not buy; use VALUE to check first.

See also: BUY, VALUE, LIST
~

0 VALUE~
Syntax: value <object>

VALUE asks a shopkeeper how much they will pay for an item.  Use this
before SELL to confirm the item is wanted and check the offered price.

See also: SELL, BUY, LIST
~

0 CAST~
Syntax: cast <spell> <target>

Before you can cast a spell, you have to practice it.  The more you practice,
the higher chance you have of success when casting.  Casting spells costs mana.
The mana cost decreases as your level increases.

The <target> is optional.  Many spells which need targets will use an
appropriate default target, especially during combat.

A spell name of more than one word needs no quotes: cast cure critic frag
works, and so does cast 'cure critic' frag if you prefer them.  The longest
run of words that names a spell is taken as the spell, and whatever is left
is the target, so cast cure light dwarf finds cure light and heals the
dwarf.  You can abbreviate the spell name.

When you cast an offensive spell, the victim usually gets a saving throw.
The effect of the spell is reduced or eliminated if the victim makes the
saving throw successfully.

See also: SPELLGROUP, SPELLS, GAIN
~

0 BOMB BURN FEED PLAY~
Syntax: bomb <cracked surface|creature>
        burn <bush|creature>
        feed <guardian>
        feed bait
        play <instrument>

Hyrule's hidden ways open only to the act the NES asked for, with the tool
in hand; OPEN, PICK, DOORBASH and UNLOCK will not shift them.

BOMB uses up one of your bombs (the arrow shops sell four for 20 rupees).
At a cracked wall or rock it opens the way; at a creature it is a blast of
fire, and Dodongo swallows one whole and loses half his health.
BURN needs a lit candle in your light slot: a bush burns away to show what
it hid, and a creature takes a lick of flame (the Red Candle burns hotter).
PLAY needs the Recorder itself: it drains Level 7's lake, shrinks a
Digdogger to a third of its health, and out on the open land calls a
whirlwind to the next dungeon whose Triforce piece you carry.
FEED gives bait to the hungry Goriya, who eats it.  FEED BAIT anywhere else
sets it down: the room's enemies forget you and turn to the smell for a
while, and you keep the bait.  A guardian is not fooled.
PUSH moves an Armos, a gravestone or a block; the warp stones also need the
Power Bracelet.

See also: HYRULE ITEMS, HYRULE SECRETS, PUSH, SHOOT, LOOK
~

0 GAMBLE~
Syntax: gamble left|middle|right

GAMBLE plays the old man's money-making game in one of Hyrule's five
gambling caves, as the NES played it.  You need 10 rupees.  Three rupee
signs lie face down: one wins 20 or 50 rupees, one loses 10, and one loses
10 or 40, laid out at random.  Pick one and all three are turned.

Over many games it pays nothing either way, a game wins at most 50 and
loses at most 40, and each costs a round of lag.  It is not the casino and
does not count toward the casino's achievements.

See also: HYRULE SECRETS, BET, SLOTS
~

0 HEARTS~
Syntax: hearts

Shows how many hearts you have, counted the NES way: 3 to start, one for
each guardian of Hyrule's Levels 1 to 8 you have defeated, and one for each
Heart Container you have taken from a "take any one you want" cave or the
dock east of Lake Hylia -- 16 at most.

The old man at the top of the waterfall gives the White Sword to a hero of
5 hearts, and the old man beneath the graveyard's loose gravestone gives the
Magical Sword to one of 12.

See also: HYRULE SECRETS, HYRULE ITEMS
~

0 'HYRULE SECRETS' 'TAKE ANY' 'MONEY CAVE' 'DOOR REPAIR'~
Hyrule's caves keep the NES's rules, and what pays out once pays each
character once, for good:

  Money caves (14)    "It's a secret to everybody": the moblin pays 10, 30
                      or 100 rupees as you first walk in, and never again.
  Door repairs (9)    "Pay me for the door repair charge": 20 rupees, once.
  Money games (5)     GAMBLE LEFT, MIDDLE or RIGHT.  See GAMBLE.
  Take any one (4)    A Heart Container or a red potion.  Take one, and the
                      other is refused; the Heart Container is a heart.
  The dock heart      A Heart Container on a dock, reached by stepladder.
  White Sword         5 hearts.  Magical Sword: 12 hearts.  See HEARTS.
  Hint caves          Two old women sell hints: GIVE them rupees (one takes
                      10, 30 or 50, the other 5, 10 or 20).  Two old men
                      give theirs free.
  Bomb bags           The old men of Levels 5 and 7 each sell room for four
                      more bombs, 100 rupees: BUY BAG.
  Potion shops        The old woman sells only to someone carrying Princess
                      Zelda's Letter, from the old man in the north-east.
  Fountains           The great fairies restore anyone not fighting.

The swords, the Letter and the Power Bracelet are given one at a time: lose
yours and the cave will give you another.

See also: HEARTS, GAMBLE, HYRULE ITEMS, HYRULE
~

0 'HYRULE ITEMS' 'DUNGEON MAP' COMPASS 'BLUE RING' 'MAGICAL SHIELD'~
What Hyrule's tools do:

  Dungeon map     LOOK or READ it: the dungeon's floor plan, @ where you
                  stand, the guardian's chamber, the Triforce chest, and
                  what the guardian's door needs.
  Compass         Inside its dungeon: the first step toward the Triforce
                  chest, and how many rooms away it and the guardian are.
  Bombs           BOMB a cracked wall, or an enemy.  Each uses one.
  Bow + arrows    Wield the bow, carry a quiver: SHOOT an enemy in the room.
                  Each arrow costs a rupee; no Archery needed.  An arrow
                  kills a pols voice outright and finishes Gohma.
  Boomerangs      Wield one and SHOOT: it stuns an enemy out of the fight
                  until struck again, and kills a keese or a gel outright.
  Candles         A light; BURN a bush or an enemy.
  Bait            FEED a hungry Goriya, or FEED BAIT to calm a room.
  Recorder        PLAY it.  See BOMB.
  Magical Rod     ZAP it; it never runs dry.  With the Magic Book carried,
                  its blast bursts into flame as well.
  Potions         The red 2nd Potion heals twice and turns blue; the blue
                  Life Potion heals twice.
  Small keys      Each opens one locked door, and keeps through a quit.
                  The Magical Key opens them all and is never used up.
  Blue Ring       Takes a tenth off every blow, as the Blue Ring of Hyrule
                  does.  Rings never add together.
  Magical Shield  Takes a tenth off fire and magic (not with the Mirror
                  Shield, which does more).
  Power Bracelet  Strength while worn, and it moves the warp stones.
  Raft, stepladder  The crossings, from the docks and the gaps.

Each guardian also leaves two pieces of a five-piece drop table, each a
little better than anything else a character of its band can find.

See also: HYRULE SECRETS, BOMB, SHOOT, HEARTS, HYRULE
~

0 CLIMB~
Syntax: climb <object>

In order to get between certain rooms, you must CLIMB a rope, ladder or
other object.  Read room descriptions carefully to find the object you need
to climb.

See also: JUMP, CRAWL, EXITS
~

0 OPEN CLOSE~
Syntax: open  <object|direction>
Syntax: close <object|direction>

OPEN opens a door or container.  CLOSE shuts it again.  Both commands
accept either a direction (to act on that exit's door) or an object name
(to open a container in the room or your inventory).  In a room with more
than one door, name the door and its direction together -- OPEN DOOR EAST
-- or just the direction.

See also: LOCK, UNLOCK, PICK, DOORBASH
~

0 LOCK UNLOCK~
Syntax: lock   <object|direction>
Syntax: unlock <object|direction>

LOCK secures a closed door or container.  UNLOCK removes the lock.  Both
require the appropriate key in your inventory.  Without a key, characters
with the PICK LOCK skill can bypass the lock with the PICK command.

See also: OPEN, CLOSE, PICK, DOORBASH
~

0 COLOR~
Syntax:  color on/off/list/default
         color <color type> <color>

The COLOR command is used to toggle color on or off and to set colors for
the various channels.

Typing the word COLOR alone will toggle color on or off.

COLOR DEFAULT is used to load the default colors for the various
channels.  If you turn color on without having set defaults, you will not
see any colors as they will all be set to grey.

COLOR LIST shows a list of the available colors.

To set the color for an indivdual type of message, use
COLOR <TYPE OF MESSAGE> <# of color>
The color number can be obtained using COLOR LIST.
For example, to make all gossips appear in yellow, type:
COLOR gossips 7

See also: PROMPT, COMPACT, BRIEF
~

0 COMBINE~
Syntax: combine
Syntax: combine <item>

If combine is on, you will see like items in your inventory combined on
a single line.  The number of items of that type you have will be shown in
parentheses in front of the item.

For example, if, with combine off, you see:
a big pot pie
a big pot pie
a big pot pie

with combine on you would see:
(3) a big pot pie

If combine is on, typing combine again turns it off, showing you the long
inventory again.  The default is for combine to be on.

COMBINE <item> makes something whole from its parts instead.  The one
recipe is COMBINE TRIFORCE, which joins the nine Hyrule Triforce pieces,
carried in hand or loose in your inventory, into The Triforce.
See HYRULE.

See also: INVENTORY, COMPACT, BRIEF
~

0 ACHIEVEMENT ACHIEVEMENTS~
Syntax: achievements
Syntax: achievements <category>
Syntax: achievements earned
Syntax: achievements incomplete
Syntax: achievements all
Syntax: achievements <word or phrase>

ACHIEVEMENTS are permanent character accomplishments. They award achievement
points, an earned date, and recognition to nearby players, but do not provide
combat power. The summary shows your total points, category completion, and
five most recent achievements.

Categories are CHARACTER, COMBAT, ENCOUNTERS, QUESTS, EXPLORATION, COLLECTION,
CRAFTING, MISADVENTURE, ECONOMY, and HYRULE. Category views show each
requirement and your current progress. EARNED and INCOMPLETE filter the full
catalog; ALL shows everything. You may also search by a title, description, or
achievement key.
An unearned hidden achievement conceals its title and requirements until you
discover it.

Level, remort, play-time, player-kill, current quest-streak, bank, carried
money, casino-total, and currently owned collection-item milestones are checked
retroactively. Lifetime mobile kills, completed quests, and deaths begin when
the achievement system first records them because older player files did not
retain those totals. Listed boss credit is shared with grouped players who are
present in the boss room.

Achievement progress and earned dates are saved with your character. Use
SCORE for a compact points total.

See also: SCORE, QUEST, GROUP, HYRULE
~

0 COMMANDS~
Syntax: commands

COMMANDS shows you all the commands in the game.

See also: KEYWORDS, HELP, SOCIALS
~

0 COMPACT~
Syntax: compact

When COMPACT mode is on, your actions and the game responses are shown to
you without spaces between the lines.  Typing compact once turns compact
mode on; typing it again, removes compact mode, showing blank spaces between
lines again.

The default is for compact to be off.

See also: BRIEF, COLOR, COMBINE
~

0 COMPARE~
Syntax: compare <item> [item]
        compare upgrades [slot]
        compare profile
        compare defense ...

COMPARE tells you which gear makes you hit harder.

  compare <item>          against what you wear in that slot (for rings,
                          amulets and bracers, the weaker of your two),
                          or against the empty slot
  compare <item> <item>   two items against each other
  compare upgrades        the best piece for every slot that you could
                          wear today, from gear a mob in the world carries
                          or sells
  compare upgrades <slot> the top five for one slot, and where to get them
  compare profile         how your damage is made: swings, hit chance,
                          damage a hit, and mana for casters
  compare defense ...     rank by toughness instead (for tanks)

How it ranks:
  - Damage first: weapon damage per round for fighters, mana to cast with
    for casters (a spell's damage is set by your level; gear only changes
    how many casts you get).  Hybrids blend the two.
  - Toughness second: effective hp against an equal-level foe, counting
    hp, armor, saves, sanctuary, dodge, parry and shield block.  It counts
    a quarter as much as damage.
  - It is worked out for you: your level, skills, stats, race and the
    rest of what you wear.  Hitroll stops helping once you land 95%.
  - It says when you cannot use an item yet, and why.

The website's gear finder ranks the same gear by class and level; this
is the version that knows your character.  To settle it by hitting
something, see DUMMY.

See also: DUMMY, LORE, IDENTIFY, EQUIPMENT, SCORE
~

0 CONSIDER~
Syntax: consider <character>

CONSIDER tells you what your chances are of killing a character.
When you consider a character, you will see two lines of information.
The first line refers to the relative level of you and the character you
are considering; the second refers to the relative number of hit points
(based on max hp, not current hp).  Between these two you should have a
rough idea of whether or not it is worth attempting the fight.

See also: SCORE, WIMPY, FLEE
~

0 COUNT~
The count command displays the number of people (that you can see) logged
into the mud.  It also displays the highest number observed that day, if
it is higher.

See also: WHO, WHERE
~

0 CREDITS~
Syntax: credits

This command shows the list of the original Diku Mud implementors.

See also: DIKU, MERC, ROM
~

0 DELETE~
Syntax: delete <password>

Use the delete command to erase unwanted characters, so the name will be
available for use.  This command must be typed along with your password to
delete a character, and you cannot be forced to delete. Deletion is permanent.
You will not be restored for deleting yourself.

See also: QUIT, SAVE
~

0 DESCRIPTION DESC~
Syntax: description <string>
Syntax: description + <string>

Sets your long description to the given string.  If the description string
starts with a '+', the part after the '+' is appended to your current
description, so that you can make multi-line descriptions.

See also: TITLE, WHO, SCORE
~

0 DONATE~
Syntax: donate <object>

If you have items in your inventory that you no longer need, you may
wish to donate them for the use of other adventurers.  The donate command
allows you to do this even if you are far away from the Temple of Devota.
The donated item will be placed randomly in one of the donation pits -
either in the Temple of Devota or in one of the guild halls in Dresden.

See also: DROP, SACRIFICE, JUNK
~

0 DRINK~
Syntax: drink <object>

DRINK consumes a liquid from a drink container to quench your thirst.
Characters who become dehydrated suffer regeneration penalties.  Use
FILL to refill empty drink containers from a fountain or pool.

See also: EAT, FILL, QUAFF
~

0 EAT~
Syntax: eat <object>

EAT consumes a food item to satisfy your hunger.  Characters who become
hungry suffer regeneration penalties.  Some foods apply temporary magical
or status effects when eaten.

Staff can eat anything they carry, which destroys it on the spot, and
are never too full to.  An endless meal is still not used up.

See also: DRINK, FILL
~

0 FILL~
Syntax: fill <object>

FILL refills a drink container with fresh water from a nearby fountain
or pool.  You must be in a room with an accessible water source.

See also: DRINK, EAT
~

0 DROP~
Syntax: drop <object>
Syntax: drop <amount> coins
Syntax: drop all
Syntax: drop all.<keyword>

DROP places an object or coins on the ground in the current room.  'drop
all' drops everything; 'drop all.sword' drops all items named "sword".

See also: GET, PUT, GIVE, DONATE
~

0 GET TAKE~
Syntax: get <object>
Syntax: get <object> <container>
Syntax: take <object>
Syntax: get all
Syntax: get all.<keyword>

GET (or TAKE) picks up an object from the ground, from a container, or
from a corpse.  'get all' picks up everything available; 'get all.sword'
takes all items named "sword".

See also: DROP, PUT, INVENTORY
~

0 GIVE~
Syntax: give <object> <character>
Syntax: give <amount> coins <character>

GIVE hands an object or a quantity of coins to another character in the
same room.  The recipient must be present and able to receive it.

See also: DROP, SPLIT, DONATE
~

0 PUT~
Syntax: put <object> <container>

PUT places an object into a container.  The container must be in your
inventory or the room.  The object must fit within the container's weight
and size limits.

See also: GET, DROP, INVENTORY
~

0 EMOTE ,~
Syntax: emote <action>
Syntax: , <action>

EMOTE broadcasts a custom action to everyone in your current room.  The
comma (,) is a shortcut for EMOTE.  Unlike fixed socials, EMOTE lets you
type any free-form action.

Example:  emote grins wickedly.    or    ,grins wickedly.

See also: POSE, SOCIAL, SAY
~

0 POSE~
Syntax: pose

POSE displays a random, class-appropriate emote selected automatically by
the game.  Each class has its own set of poses.  Use POSE for quick flavor
text without typing a full EMOTE message.

See also: EMOTE, SOCIAL
~

0 SOCIAL SOCIALS~
Syntax: social
Syntax: socials

SOCIAL (or SOCIALS) lists all built-in social commands available in the
game.  Each social is a named action you can direct at yourself, another
player, or no target (e.g. smile, wave, bow, laugh).  Type the social's
name alone or with a target name.

The following is a (not necessarily complete) list of available socials:
gack        kiss        eve         bounce      smile       dance
cackle      laugh       giggle      shake       puke        growl
scream      comfort     sigh        sulk        hug         snuggle
cuddle      nuzzle      cry         poke        accuse      grin
bow         applaud     blush       burp        chuckle     clap
cough       curtsey     fart        flip        fondle      frown
gasp        glare       groan       grope       hiccup      lick
love        moan        nibble      pout        ruffle      shiver
shrug       sing        slap        smirk       snap        sneeze
snicker     sniff       snore       squeeze     stare       strut
thank       twiddle     wave        whistle     wiggle      wink
yawn        snowball    french      comb        massage     tickle
pat         curse       pray        beg         cringe      daydream
fume        grovel      hop         nudge       ponder      punch
snarl       spank       hand        yodel       faint       pinch
stroke      apologize   caress      stagger     snort       slobber
blink       tease       knee        flirt       tip         lust
flutter     bark        howl        babble      ramble      hush
threaten    roll        swoon       bird        eyebrow     serenade
grimace     boggle      beckon      wonder      worry       drool
nod         purr        point       rub         bleed       highfive
propose     peer        worship     bearhug     innocent    collapse
stretch     spam        boast       squirm      moo         moon
goose       wince       type        brb         mutter      rofl
sob         pant        whine       flex        embrace     duck
bonk        squeal      tackle      spit        life        mosh
flinch      air         tweak       peck        explode     raspberry
flash       strip       undress     tongue      view        grumble
cheer       plead       charge      criticize   run         judge
insane      cover       flare       head        pie         cower
noogie      yeehaw      pissed      passout     adjust      scratch
meditate    bkiss       beer        bcatch      claw        rose
laces       tag         tank        starve      aargh       homework
puff        differ      yae         lightbulb   voodoo      awkward
dab         derp        facepalm    micdrop     panic       slowclap

See also: EMOTE, POSE
~

0 EQUIPMENT~
Syntax: equipment

Lists all items you currently have equipped: worn armor, wielded weapons,
held items, and any light source.  Each item is shown at its wear location.
Use INVENTORY to see items you are carrying but not yet equipped.

See also: INVENTORY, WEAR, WIELD, HOLD, REMOVE
~

0 INVENTORY~
Syntax: inventory

Lists all items you are carrying in your pack but not currently equipped.
Use COMBINE to view stacked identical items as a single line.  Use
EQUIPMENT to see what you have worn or wielded.

See also: EQUIPMENT, COMBINE, COMPARE, LORE
~

0 LOOK READ~
Syntax: look
Syntax: look room
Syntax: look <object>
Syntax: look <character>
Syntax: look <direction>
Syntax: look <keyword>
Syntax: look in <container>
Syntax: look in <corpse>
Syntax: read <map or compass>

LOOK examines whatever you specify.  Looking at a character shows their
description and equipment.  LOOK alone describes the room you are in;
with BRIEF on, only the short description is shown.  Use 'look room' to
see the full, unbriefed room description.

READ normally behaves like LOOK. Some magical maps have a readable route
chart. A dungeon compass can be read while inside its matching labyrinth to
show the first general direction toward the boss and whether the chamber feels
close or far away. If you carry several, include the dungeon name, such as
'read eagle compass'.

See also: EXAMINE, SEARCH, INVENTORY
~

0 EXAMINE~
Syntax: examine <container>
Syntax: examine <corpse>

EXAMINE is shorthand for 'LOOK <container>' followed by 'LOOK IN
<container>'.  It shows the container itself and then lists all of its
contents in one step.  Particularly useful for inspecting corpses and packs.

See also: LOOK, INVENTORY, GET
~

51 EXCHANGE~
Syntax: exchange

If you are a hero, you may exchange experience for more practices.  The cost
is 5000 experience points.  This means that if you would rather get more
practices rather than another level, exchange the experience before leveling.
Otherwise you'll have to get 5000 more experience up and above your new level.
Exchanging experience for practices gives you 5-7 more; the exact number of
practices you get is random.  You may exchange only at the guru in Hero Hall.

See also: PRACTICE, GAIN, TRAIN, WORTH
~

0 EXITS~
Syntax: exits

Tells you the visible exits of the room you are in.  Closed doors are shown
in parentheses.  Not all exits are visible;  room descriptions will generally
provide some clues as to the existence of hidden exits and their names.

See also: AUTOEXIT, SEARCH, LOOK
~

0 FLEE~
Syntax: flee

Once you start a fight, you can't just walk away from it.  If the fight
is not going well, you can attempt to FLEE, or another character can
RESCUE you.  (You can also RECALL, but this is less likely to work,
and costs more experience points, then fleeing).

If you lose your link during a fight, then your character will keep
fighting, and will attempt to RECALL from time to time.  Your chances
of making the recall are reduced, and you will lose much more experience.

WIMPY will cause you to flee automatically if your hit points drop too low.

See also: RESCUE, RECALL, WIMPY
~

0 FLIP MOVE PULL PUSH TURN~
Syntax: flip <object>
        move <object>
        pull <object>
        push <object>
        turn <object>

Some rooms contain objects which need to be manipulated by flipping, moving,
pushing, pulling or turning them.  Manipulating these objects can have
various effects.  For example, one might push a lever or flip a switch to
open a secret door.  Read room descriptions carefully in order to find
objects that you can manipulate.

See also: LOOK, SEARCH, OPEN, CLIMB
~

0 FOLLOW GROUP ~
Syntax: follow <character>
Syntax: group
Syntax: group <character>

FOLLOW starts you following another character.  To stop following anyone else,
just FOLLOW <yourself>.  To keep yourself from being followed, use the
NOFOLLOW command.

GROUP <character> makes someone who is following you a member of your group.
Group members share experience points from kills and may use the GTELL and
SPLIT commands.  If anyone in your group is attacked, you will automatically
join the fight.

If the <character> is already a member of your group, then GROUP will
kick the <character> out of your group.  Characters can also leave your
group by using a FOLLOW command to stop following you.

GROUP with no argument shows statistics for each character in your group.

You may FOLLOW anyone but you may only GROUP with characters within 8 levels
of yourself.

See also: GTELL, SPLIT, NOFOLLOW, AUTOASSIST
~

0 GAIN~
Syntax: gain list
        gain <skill or group>
        gain convert

The gain command is used to learn new skills, once the proper trainer has
been found.  The following options can be used with gain:

gain list:    list all groups and skills that can be learned
gain <name>:  add a skill or skill group, at the listed cost
gain convert: turns 10 practices into one training session

Gain uses training sessions, not practices. (see 'help train').

Read the helps on the various spellgroups and skills to determine what
you want to gain.  The helps will show you the minimum level you need to be
to use a skill or the lowest level spell in a spellgroup.

See also: TRAIN, PRACTICE, GROUPS, TEACHLIST, GAINLIST
~

0 GROUPS~
Syntax: groups

The GROUPS command shows which spell groups you have (both default and
groups that you have gained).

See also: GAIN, SKILLS, SPELLS
~

0 HEAL~
Syntax: heal
        heal <spell>

The Temple of Devota and some other locations provide healers for the
assistance of adventurers.  The healer's services are generally free to
players of level 10 or below.  Higher level adventurers may be healed of
their wounds and ills on a fee for service basis.  The HEAL command without
an argument shows a list of the healer's services.  To receive healing, be
sure you have sufficient money and type heal <name of spell>.

For information on the heal spell used by those of the cleric class (and
members of the cleric's guild), read help for HEALSPELL.

See also: SCORE, AFFECT, RESTORE
~

0 HELP~
Syntax: help
Syntax: help <keyword>

HELP without any arguments shows a one-page command summary.

HELP <keyword> shows a page of help on that keyword.  The keywords include
all the commands, spells, and skills listed in the game.  Type HELP INDEX
for an alphabetical listing of all keywords.

See also: KEYWORDS, COMMANDS, SOCIALS
~

0 WEAR~
Syntax: wear <object>
Syntax: wear all

WEAR equips a piece of armor or clothing at its appropriate body slot.
You may not be able to wear an item if its alignment does not match yours,
it is too heavy for you, or you are not experienced enough.  WEAR ALL
attempts to equip every suitable item in your inventory at once.

See also: WIELD, HOLD, REMOVE, EQUIPMENT
~

0 WIELD~
Syntax: wield <object>

WIELD equips a weapon in your main hand.  Characters with the SECONDARY
skill can equip a second weapon in the off hand using the SECONDARY
command.

See also: WEAR, HOLD, REMOVE, SECONDARY, DUAL WIELD
~

0 HOLD~
Syntax: hold <object>

HOLD equips a light source, wand, or staff in the held-item slot.  Only
items designated for holding can be used this way.

See also: WEAR, WIELD, ZAP, BRANDISH, REMOVE
~

0 REMOVE~
Syntax: remove <object>
Syntax: remove all

REMOVE takes an equipped item and puts it back into your inventory.
REMOVE ALL strips all your gear except for cursed items, which cannot
be removed until the curse is lifted.

See also: WEAR, WIELD, HOLD, EQUIPMENT
~

0 JOIN~
Syntax: join <guildname>

The JOIN command is used to join a guild.  It works only when you are in
Melancholy's treehouse.  The possible guildnames are cleric, mage, thief,
and warrior.  Joining a guild costs 500 coins, which Melancholy will
take automatically when you use the join command.

See also: GUILDS, GAIN, GROUPS
~

0 KILL~
Syntax: kill <name of mob>

The KILL command starts a fight with a mobile (non-player character).  You
need to type kill only once per fight.  Attacking player characters
requires MURDER instead.

See also: MURDER, HIT, FLEE, WIMPY
~

0 PKILL~
Syntax: pkill <password>

PKILL is a permanent, irreversible setting.  Between level 15 and 30, you
may choose to participate in player killing by typing PKILL followed by
your password.  After level 30 the option is no longer available.  Once
set, pkill status cannot be revoked under any circumstances.

You can identify other pkillers only by walking up to them and using
CONSIDER.  Non-pkillers may only engage in player combat inside the arena.

See also: MURDER, ARENA, KILL
~

0 NOFOLLOW  NOFOL~

Syntax: nofollow
        nofol

NOFOLLOW toggles the follow flag, either on or off.

If you have pets you need to get rid of, or people you don't want following
you (i.e. members of a castle with whom you are at war), you can use this
command to prevent them from following you.

In order to be grouped, you must be able to accept followers.

The default for NOFOL is OFF (meaning you can accept followers), and you can
check the status of the NOFOL flag with the AUTO command.

See also: GROUP, FOLLOW, AUTO
~

0 NOLOOT~

Syntax: noloot

NOLOOT toggles whether or not anyone can mess with your corpse.
There are valid reasons why you might want someone to mess with your corpse:
to retrieve it or to get certain items from it.  If your corpse is set NOLOOT
then it cannot be picked up by, nor can objects be removed by, anyone else.

The default for NOLOOT is on (i.e. your corpse cannot be looted).  You can
check the status of this flag with the AUTO command.

See also: AUTO, NOSUMMON, NOFOLLOW
~

0 NOSUMMON~

NOSUMMON toggles whether or not you can be summoned, gated to, or cancelled
by other players.  Read help on SUMMON, GATE and CANCEL for more information
on these spells.  In general, you will want to turn NOSUMMON off only to
permit trusted wizard friends to assist you in transportation and in removing
harmful spells.

The default for NOSUMMON is on (you cannot be summoned, gated to or cancelled).
The status of this flag can be checked with the AUTO command.

See also: AUTO, NOLOOT, NOFOLLOW
~

0 NOTE NOTES~
The following options are available:

reading notes:
note list       : show all notes (unread notes are marked with an N)
note unread     : show only the ones you have not read yet
note read       : either by number, or read next to read unread notes
note search     : find notes mentioning a word, in the sender, the
		  subject or the body
note catchup    : mark everything read without opening it
note remove     : removes a note that you wrote or are the target of

answering notes:
note reply      : start a note back to whoever wrote number <n>, with
		  the subject set to Re: theirs
note forward    : send number <n> on to somebody else, with the original
		  text carried across and marked as theirs

writing new notes:
note to         : sets the to line for a new note (name(s), immortal, all,
		  or the castle(s) name)
note subject    : sets the subject for a new note
note +          : adds a line to a new note (i.e. note + hi guys!)
note -          : deletes the last line you typed (no argument required)
note clear      : erase a note in progress
note show       : shows the note you are working on
note post       : posts a new note. This MUST be done to post a note.

A number is always the note's place in your full list, even when you
are looking at a filtered one, so a number you read off a search is the
number to give to note read.

When you log in, you will be informed if there are unread notes waiting. Type
note read (or just note) to scroll through them one at a time.

You must be at least level 2 to use the note system.

See also: BUG, IDEA, TYPO, CHANNELS
~

0 ORDER~
Syntax: order <character> <command>
Syntax: order all <command>

ORDER orders one or all of your charmed followers (including pets) to
perform any command.  The command may have arguments.  You are responsible
for the actions of your followers, and others who attack your followers
will incur the same penalty as if they attacked you directly.

Most charmed creatures lose their aggressive nature (while charmed).  However,
they will become aggressive again when the charm wears off.  For that reason,
charmed aggressive mobs should not be brought outside the areas they are
normally found in.

See also: GROUP, FOLLOW, CHARM PERSON
~

0 OUTFIT~
Syntax: outfit

The outfit command, usable by levels 5 and below, equips your character with
a new set of sub issue gear (banner, weapon, helmet, shield, and vest),
courtesy of the Mayor's warehouses.  Only empty equipment slots are affected.

See also: EQUIPMENT, WEAR, SUBISSUE
~

0 PASSWORD~
Syntax: password <old-password> <new-password>

PASSWORD changes your character's password.  The first argument must be
your old password.  The second argument is your new password.

The PASSWORD command is protected against being snooped or logged.

See also: DELETE, QUIT, SAVE
~

0 PRACTICE~
Syntax: PRACTICE
Syntax: PRACTICE <skill|spell>

PRACTICE without an argument tells you your current ability level in all
the skills and spells available to you.  You can check this anywhere.

PRACTICE with an argument practice that skill or spell.  Your learning
percentage varies from 1% (unlearned) to a maximum of 75%.  You will continue
to improve in a skill or spell by using it as long as you are not at 1%.
You must be at a guild master to practice.

The higher your intelligence, the more you will learn at each practice
session.  The higher your wisdom, the more practice sessions you will gain
each time you gain a level.  Unused sessions are saved until you do use them.

See also: TRAIN, GAIN, SKILLS, SPELLS
~

0 SAVE~
Syntax: save

Immediately saves your character and carried inventory to disk.  The game
also auto-saves every 15 minutes, so manual saves are rarely critical.
Typing save can cause a brief command delay, so avoid spamming it.
You must be at least level 3 to save.

Note: some objects (keys, items more than two levels above yours) are not
saved and will vanish on logout.  QUIT performs an automatic save, so
you do not need to save before quitting.

See also: QUIT, PASSWORD
~

0 RENT~
Syntax: (not applicable)

There is no RENT in this game.  Simply SAVE and QUIT when you want to
leave; your character and equipment are stored automatically.  You will
return to the same room next time you log in.

See also: QUIT, SAVE
~

0 RECALL /~
Syntax: RECALL
        RECALL SET
        RECALL DEFAULT
        RECALL WHERE

RECALL prays to the gods for miraculous transportation from where you are
back to your recall point.  '/' is a synonym for RECALL.

Every character begins with their recall point at the Temple of Devota in
Dresden.  You may move it:

  RECALL SET       makes the room you are standing in your recall point.
  RECALL DEFAULT   puts it back to the Temple.
  RECALL WHERE     tells you where it is, and whether you may move it yet.

Choose somewhere you go often - a hunting ground you are working through,
a questmaster, the shop you live out of.  Anywhere the gods have forsaken
is refused, and so are jails and death traps.  A busy room is fine: who
else happens to be standing there makes no difference.

You may move your recall point once every 30 minutes, and not while you
are fighting or while the blood is still up from a fight.  RECALL DEFAULT
is exempt from all of that: you can send your recall back to the Temple at
any moment, so a choice you regret can never strand you.

WORD OF RECALL is a different journey, and worth knowing about.  It
always goes to the Temple whatever you have set, so between the two you
have two places to reach in a hurry.  It also works while you are cursed,
where RECALL does not - a curse silences your own prayer but has no hold
over somebody else's magic.  A scroll or potion of recall carries the same
spell and behaves the same way.  The Recall Ring, and being pulled to
safety while link-dead, also go to the Temple.

If you RECALL during combat, you will lose experience (more than for fleeing),
and you will have a chance of failing (again, more than for fleeing).  This
chance is based on your recall skill, although a 100% recall does not
insure success.

RECALL costs half of your movement points.

RECALL doesn't work in certain god-forsaken rooms.  Characters afflicted by a
curse may not recall at all.

See also: WORD OF RECALL, PORTAL, MOVEMENT, CHANGES
~

0 REMORT~
Syntax: REMORT <password> <class> <guild> <race>

REMORT allows a character at their current maximum level to start over as a
reborn adventurer of the same name.  Each remort raises your permanent maximum
level by one, increases your starting and per-level stats, and grants extra
starting resources.  You may remort up to five times.

REQUIREMENT
  You must reach level 54 + (number of remorts already done) to remort.
  Example: first remort requires level 54; second requires level 55; etc.

WHAT YOU KEEP vs. WHAT YOU LOSE
  Keep: character name, gold (held and banked), quest points, remort bonuses,
        titles earned, and every item you own -- worn gear included, though
        most of it will out-level you until you climb back up.
  Lose: all levels (restart at level 3), all spells and skills, all group
        knowledge, current class/guild/race (replaced by your new choices).
        Stats are reset to 13 in all categories.

BENEFITS PER REMORT
  The table below shows cumulative values AT THE START of each new life.
  "Base HP/Mana/Move" is your starting maximum before any level gains.
  "Per Level Bonus" is added to every normal level-up gain.

  Remort   Req.Lvl   Max Lvl   Base HP/Mana/Move   Pracs   Trains   Per-Lvl
  ------   -------   -------   ----------------    -----   ------   -------
    1st       54        55           200 each          17      10       +1
    2nd       55        56           400 each          19      12       +2
    3rd       56        57           600 each          21      14       +3
    4th       57        58           800 each          23      16       +4
    5th       58        59          1000 each          25      18       +5

  Starting at remort 1 you are immune to hunger and thirst forever.
  Starting at remort 2 you gain psionic powers, and every remort after that
  deepens them: one power from each of the 4 disciplines at the 2nd remort,
  two from each at the 3rd, three from each at the 4th.  They STACK.  A
  remort wipes your skills, but the powers you have been given are
  remembered and handed back, so a later life never costs you one you
  already had.  The full pool contains 17: ego whip, mindblast, nightmare,
  torment, clairvoyance, mindbar, psionic armor, psychic shield,
  telekinesis, astral walk, shift, confuse, project, transfusion, mind
  leech, enervate, and pyrotechnics.  At the final (5th) remort you are
  given every one of them.
  Starting at remort 3 you carry twice as many items and twice the weight,
  you may idle twice as long before the void takes you, and your recall
  always succeeds (no skill check, no random-room mishap).  Recall from
  combat still costs experience.
  At remort 4 you are taught SHADOWMELD at 50%.  It is a real skill from
  there: practise it, improve it with use, and no later remort takes it
  away.  See HELP SHADOWMELD.
  On the 5th (final) remort you carry unlimited items and weight and may
  choose any class and guild freely (the usual restriction of picking only
  classes you have never been does not apply).

HERO QUEST COST
  Buying level 51 (hero) via AQUEST BUY HERO costs:
    7000 quest points for a character who has never remorted.
    5000 quest points for any character who has remorted at least once.

QUEST SHOP ITEMS
  These items can be purchased from the quest master (AQUEST BUY <item>):

  POTION OF POWER (450 qp)  - buy: AQUEST BUY EMPOWER  or  AQUEST BUY POWER
    Bestows Sanctuary, Haste (+DEX), and Flight for the duration of the
    cast.  A single vial of extraordinary divine energy.

  POTION OF THE GIANT (450 qp)  - buy: AQUEST BUY GIANT
    Grants Giant Strength, Stone Skin fortification, and the ability to
    Pass Door.  Brewed from ancient mountain earth and raw elemental force.

  SCROLL OF FARSLAY (600 qp)  - buy: AQUEST BUY FARSLAY  or  AQUEST BUY SCROLL
    A black scroll inscribed with the ancient rite of Farslay. Targets one
    named character anywhere in the world. One use only, with grave risks.

  POTION OF SANCTUARY (150 qp)  - buy: AQUEST BUY SANCTUARY
    A more modest blessing that wraps the drinker in a protective aura.

  PRACTICES (500 qp)  - buy: AQUEST BUY PRAC
    Awards 1-3 extra practice sessions for training skills.

CLASS, GUILD, AND RACE OPTIONS
  Every life must be a new one, so that levelling it is a different game
  from the last -- and the CLASS is what decides that.  You may not live
  twice as the same class.  Your current class counts as used the moment
  you remort away from it, and the game remembers every class you have
  ever been.

  The classes are mage, cleric, thief, warrior, monk and necro, so a
  character who remorts all the way through will have played nearly all
  of them.

  The GUILD is free.  Join any of them, as often as you like, or none.
  Monks and necromancers must still use 'none', because those classes
  have no guild.

  If a class is refused, the game lists every one still open to you.

  On your final (5th) remort even the class history is set aside and you
  may pick anything.
  Classes: mage, cleric, thief, warrior, monk, necro
  Guilds:  mage, cleric, thief, warrior, none
           (Monks and Necros must use 'none' as their guild.)
  Races:   human, elf, dwarf, hobbit, saurian
           Monks are restricted to: dwarf, human.
           Necromancers are restricted to: elf, human.

NOTES
  Finish or abort any active automatic quest before remorting.
  If you supply a class with no guild (monk or necro), type 'none' for guild.
  All affects, immunities, resistances, and flags are cleared on remort.
  Your pk_state is reset; the title is set to match your new class.
  Remort history is saved; HELP SCORE shows your remort count.

See also: HERO, HEROLEVELS, GAIN, GROUPS

Each remort carries its own gift, and they build on one another:

    first   (at level 54)   hunger and thirst no longer touch you
    second  (at level 55)   psionic powers, one from each discipline
    third   (at level 56)   twice the carrying room, twice as long before
                            the idle void takes you, a recall that never
                            fails, and a second power from each discipline
    fourth  (at level 57)   the SHADOWMELD skill at 50%, and a third power
                            from each discipline
    fifth   (at level 58)   every psionic power, no carrying limit at all,
                            a free choice of class and guild, and the
                            climb to 59

Every remort also raises the flat hit, mana and movement you start a
life with, and the practices and trains you begin it holding.
~

0 AFFECT~
Syntax: affect

Shows all magical and psionic effects currently active on your character.
For each effect, the entry shows the spell or skill name, the modifier it
applies, the stat or attribute affected, and how many ticks remain.  A
duration of "permanent" means the effect does not expire.

Some effects change no statistic at all - a psionic ward such as MINDBAR
is a named protection and a duration and nothing else.  Those show the
name and how long is left.

See also: SCORE, IDENTIFY, ATTRIBUTE
~

0 ATTRIBUTE ATTRIBUTES~
Syntax: attribute

Displays a quick summary of your five core attributes: Strength,
Intelligence, Wisdom, Dexterity, and Constitution.  Values are shown as
base(current) to separate your trained score from bonuses applied by
equipment or active spells.

See also: SCORE, AFFECT, TRAIN
~

0 REPORT~
Syntax: report

Announces your current hit points, mana, and movement to yourself and to
every player in the same room.  Useful in group combat so healers and
allies can gauge how much support you need.  Does not reveal your
maximum values, only your current condition.

See also: SCORE, GROUP, HEAL
~

0 SCORE~
Syntax: score

Displays your full character sheet: name, level, class, race, all
attributes, hit points, mana, movement, armor class, hitroll, damroll,
experience, gold, remort count, and more.  Attribute scores are shown as
trained(current) so you can see exactly how much bonus gear and spells
are contributing.  Only you can see your own SCORE output.

See also: ATTRIBUTE, AFFECT, WORTH, REPORT
~

0 WORTH~
Syntax: worth

Shows a brief financial and experience summary: your current gold on hand,
bank balance, total experience earned, and how much experience remains
before your next level.  A quick alternative to the full SCORE display
when you only need to check progress or wealth.

See also: SCORE, BANK, BALANCE
~

0 SLEEP~
Syntax: sleep

SLEEP puts your character into a deep slumber.  While asleep you
regenerate hit points, mana, and movement at the fastest possible rate,
but you are more vulnerable to attack and will not hear most events in
the room.  Use WAKE or STAND to get up.

For information on the sleep spell, see SLEEPSPELL.

See also: REST, STAND, WAKE, WIMPY
~

0 REST STAND WAKE SIT~
Syntax: rest
Syntax: stand
Syntax: wake
Syntax: sit

These commands change your position.  When you REST or SIT, you regenerate
hit points, mana, and movement faster than while standing, while remaining
aware of your surroundings.

STAND or WAKE returns you to a standing position.  You can also use WAKE
to rouse a sleeping character.

See also: SLEEP, FLEE, WIMPY
~

0 RUN~
Syntax: run <direction> [distance]

RUN moves repeatedly in one direction.  Distance is optional; it defaults to
30 and is capped at 30.  Movement can stop early at a wall, closed exit, trap,
combat, or any other condition that prevents normal movement.  All ten normal
directions and their abbreviations are accepted.

See also: SPEEDWALK, RECALL, AUTOEXIT, EXITS
~

0 SPEEDWALK~
Syntax: speedwalk <route>

SPEEDWALK follows compact lowercase direction tokens.  Put an optional count
before each direction; each count defaults to 1 and is capped at 30.

Examples: speedwalk 3n2e1s
          speedwalk 2ne4w1d

Valid tokens are n, e, s, w, u, d, ne, nw, se, and sw.  Movement still uses
normal exits and costs, and stops if combat begins or you cannot continue.

See also: RUN, EXITS, AUTOEXIT
~

0 SACRIFICE~
Syntax: sacrifice <object>

SACRIFICE offers an object to the gods, who may reward you.
The nature of the reward depends upon the type of object.

See also: JUNK, DONATE, AUTOSAC
~

0 SCAN~
Syntax: scan

The SCAN command tells you what mobs are in surrounding rooms.  Only visible
mobs will be shown, unless you are affected by the appropriate detection
spells.  You cannot scan through a closed door.

See also: SEARCH, TRACK, EXITS
~

0 SCROLL~
Syntax: scroll
Syntax: scroll <number>

This command changes the number of lines the mud sends you in a page (the
default is 24 lines).  Change this to a higher number for larger screen
sizes, or to 0 to disable paging.

See also: BRIEF, COMPACT, PROMPT
~

0 SCROLL OF FARSLAY FARSLAY~
The Scroll of Farslay is purchasable from the quest master for 600 quest
points (AQUEST BUY FARSLAY).

Syntax: recite farslay <character>
        recite scroll <character>

The rite seeks one named character anywhere in the world. After a successful
recitation, Farslayer has an even chance to kill the target or turn back and
kill the reader. Either outcome leaves a mortal reader stunned at one hit,
mana, and movement point; permanently reduces maximum hit points, mana, and
movement; and permanently reduces one attribute. If the target is struck,
the reader becomes wanted. The scroll is consumed even if recitation fails,
but a missing target or omitted target does not consume it.

Farslay cannot finish Ganon; the Silver Arrow is still required.

See also: AQUEST, RECITE, POTION OF POWER, POTION OF THE GIANT
~

0 POTION OF POWER~
The Potion of Power is purchasable from the quest master for 450 quest
points (AQUEST BUY EMPOWER or AQUEST BUY POWER).

Drinking it grants three powerful protective effects simultaneously:
  Sanctuary  - envelope the body in a divine shield (halves damage taken).
  Haste      - greatly increases attack speed and Dexterity.
  Fly        - lifts the drinker off the ground.

All effects last for the standard spell duration at level 60.

See also: AQUEST, POTION OF THE GIANT, SCROLL OF FARSLAY
~

0 POTION OF THE GIANT~
The Potion of the Giant is purchasable from the quest master for 450 quest
points (AQUEST BUY GIANT).

Drinking it applies three combat enhancement effects simultaneously:
  Giant Strength - dramatically boosts Strength and damage output.
  Stone Skin     - hardens the skin, significantly improving armor class.
  Pass Door      - allows movement through closed (non-locked) doors.

All effects last for the standard spell duration at level 60.

See also: AQUEST, POTION OF POWER, SCROLL OF FARSLAY
~

0 SKILLS SPELLS~
Syntax: skills
Syntax: spells

The skills and spells commands are used to display your character's list
of available skills (or spells, as the case may be).  They are listed in
order of level, with mana cost (for spells) or percentage (for skills)
listed where applicable.

See also: GAIN, GROUPS, PRACTICE
~

0 SPLIT~
Syntax: split <amount>

SPLIT splits some gold between you and all the members of your
group who are in the same room as you.  It's customary to SPLIT
the gold after a kill.  However, this is most conveniently done using
the AUTOSPLIT option.

See also: AUTOSPLIT, GROUP, GIVE
~

0 TIME~
Syntax: time

Displays the current in-game time of day, the date since the mud was last
started, and the real-world local time on the host server.  Game time
progresses faster than real time; day and night cycles affect some spells,
monster behavior, and room lighting.

See also: WEATHER, TICK
~

0 WEATHER~
Syntax: weather

Shows the current in-game weather conditions: sky state (clear, cloudy,
raining, storming, etc.), wind direction, and temperature.  Weather can
affect movement, some spells, and area events.  Certain seasons also
change which weather patterns are possible.

See also: TIME, TICK
~

0 TITLE~
Syntax: title <string>
        title default
        title

Sets your title to a given string.  TITLE is available from level 3;
before that the game answers Huh?.  A title is cut at 45 characters.

The game supplies your class's title when your character is created, and
moves it along each time you advance a level.  Once you set a title of
your own it stays: levelling and remorting leave it alone.

TITLE DEFAULT gives you back your class's title for your level, and from
then on it changes as you level again.  TITLE on its own shows your
current title and whether it is yours or your class's.

See also: DESCRIPTION, SCORE, WHO
~

0 TRAIN~
Syntax: train <str int wis dex con hp mana>

TRAIN increases one of your attributes.  When you start the game, your
character has standard attributes based on your class, and several
initial training sessions.  You can increase your attributes by
using these sessions at a trainer.  Trainers can be found in mud school,
wandering town or in guilds.

It takes one training session to improve an attribute, or to increase
mana or hp by 10.  You receive one training session per level.  Training
sessions are also used to buy new skills and practices may be converted to
trains to help you in your studies.  See help on GAIN for more information.

The best attributes to train first are WIS and CON.  WIS gives you more
practices when you gain a level and  CON gives you more hit points.
In the long run, your character will be most powerful if you train
both WIS and CON to their maximum (which depends on your race and class)
before training anything else.

See also: PRACTICE, GAIN, SCORE, ATTRIBUTE
~

0 VISIBLE~
Syntax: visible

VISIBLE cancels your hiding and sneaking, as well as any invisibility,
making you visible again.

See also: HIDE, SNEAK, STEALTH
~

0 WHERE~
Syntax: where
Syntax: where <character>

WHERE without an argument tells you the location of visible players in the same
area as you are.

WHERE with an argument tells you the location of one character with that name
within your area, including monsters.

See also: WHO, SCAN, AREAS
~

0 WHO WHOIS~
Syntax: who
Syntax: who <level-range>
Syntax: who <class or race>
Syntax: who <class or race> <level-range>
Syntax: whois <character>

WHO shows the people currently in the game.  Some people may choose to
remain unseen and won't appear when you use WHO.

WHOIS provides information about a specific player, including their class
and level.  That information may be useful in determining whether to respond
to a request to group or in ascertaining that the person speaking to you is
an immortal and should, therefore, really not be ignored.

WHO without any arguments shows all of the (visible) people currently playing.

With arguments, WHO can show players of certain classes or levels.
For example:

who 10          lists all players of level 10 or above
who 15 20       lists all players between level 15 and 20
who cleric      lists all clerics playing
who immortal    lists all immortals playing
who cleric 1 5  lists all clerics between levels 1 and 5
who elf cleric  lists all elven clerics
who <castle>    lists all members of a specified castle

Classes and races may be abbreviated.

See also: WHERE, FINGER, SCORE
~

0 WIMPY~
Syntax: wimpy
Syntax: wimpy <number>

WIMPY sets your wimpy value.  When your character takes damage that reduces
your hit points below your wimpy value, you will automatically attempt to flee.
You will only flee if your character is not in a wait state -- i.e. has not
been using combat commands like cast, trip and bash, and has not been
tripped or bash by an enemy.

WIMPY with no argument sets your wimpy value to 20% of your maximum hit points.

Some monsters are wimpy.

See also: FLEE, RECALL, SCORE
~

0 !~
Syntax: !

! repeats the last command you typed.

See also: COMMANDS
~

0 NORTH SOUTH EAST WEST UP DOWN NORTHEAST NORTHWEST SOUTHEAST SOUTHWEST~
Syntax: north
Syntax: south
Syntax: east
Syntax: west
Syntax: up
Syntax: down
Syntax: northeast
Syntax: ne
Syntax: northwest
Syntax: nw
Syntax: southeast
Syntax: se
Syntax: southwest
Syntax: sw

Use these commands to walk in a particular direction.  The ordinary directions
are usually abbreviated as N, S, E, W, U and D.   The diagonal directions
may be abbreviated as NE, NW, SE and SW.  If you are using a mud client to
access the game, you may need to alter some of the files to allow for the
abbreviated directions.

See also: EXITS, OPEN, MOVEMENT
~

0 BUG IDEA TYPO~
Syntax: bug <message>
Syntax: idea <message>
Syntax: typo <message>

These commands are essentially an artifact of mud history.  They still
exist and record a lot of bugs, ideas and typos to the implementors, but
it is preferable to use the bug and idea note forums.  See help NOTE for
more information.

See also: NOTE
~

0 GOSSIP .~
Syntax: gossip <message>
Syntax: gossip
Syntax: . <message>

GOSSIP broadcasts a message to all players in the world who have the channel
enabled.  The period (.) is a shortcut for GOSSIP.  This is the most
frequently used public channel on ToC.  Typing GOSSIP without a message
toggles the channel off; type it again to restore it.

You must be at least level 2 to use the gossip channel.

See also: SHOUT, YELL, CHANNELS, DEAF, QUIET, TELL
~

0 SHOUT~
Syntax: shout <message>

SHOUT sends a message to all awake players in the world.  To curb excessive
shouting, SHOUT imposes a three-second delay on the shouter.  Players must
be at least level 3 to shout.  Use DEAF to block incoming shouts, or QUIET
to silence all public channels at once.

See also: GOSSIP, YELL, DEAF, CHANNELS
~

0 YELL~
Syntax: yell <message>

YELL broadcasts a message to all awake players within your current area
only.  It is narrower than SHOUT (world-wide) and more broadly heard
than SAY (room only).

See also: SHOUT, GOSSIP, SAY, CHANNELS
~

0 INFO~
Syntax: info

INFO is a broadcast channel that displays automatic notifications when a
player attains a new level.  Typing INFO alone toggles the channel off or
on; you cannot send messages on the info channel directly.

See also: LEVELING, CHANNELS
~

0 LEVELING~
Syntax: leveling <message>
Syntax: leveling

The LEVELING channel is for congratulatory messages to players who just
leveled up.  INFO displays the automatic level notification; LEVELING is
where other players respond with congratulations.  Typing LEVELING alone
toggles the channel.

See also: INFO, GOSSIP, CHANNELS
~

0 MUSIC~
Syntax: music <message>
Syntax: music

The MUSIC channel is used for singing and sharing music-related content
with all players.  Send a message or type MUSIC alone to toggle it off or
on.

See also: GOSSIP, CHANNELS
~

0 DEAF~
Syntax: deaf

Toggles the shout channel on or off.  When deaf is active you will not
receive SHOUT messages from other players.  Type DEAF again to restore
your ability to hear shouts.  The gossip and other channels are unaffected;
use QUIET to silence everything at once.

See also: QUIET, CHANNELS, SHOUT
~

0 QUIET~
Syntax: quiet

Toggles all public channels off simultaneously, so you hear only EMOTES
and SAY in your current room.  Useful for concentrating during difficult
combat or roleplay scenes.  Type QUIET again to restore all channels.
Individual channel states are preserved; they return to how they were.

See also: DEAF, CHANNELS, NOISY
~

0 TELL~
Syntax: tell <character> <message>

TELL sends a private message to one awake player anywhere in the world.
Only the target sees your message.  Use REPLY to respond to the last tell
you received.

See also: REPLY, SAY, GTELL, CHANNELS
~

0 REPLY~
Syntax: reply <message>

REPLY sends a message to the last player who sent you a TELL.  REPLY
works even if you cannot see the player and without revealing their
identity.  This is handy for talking to invisible or switched immortal
players.

See also: TELL, SAY, GTELL
~

10 CGOS CASTLECHAT CC -~
Syntax: cgos <message>
Syntax: cc <message>

CGOS allows you to send a message to members of all castles.

CC is used to send  message only to members of your castle.  The dash, '-'
is a synonym for CC.

Type a channel name without a message to turn that channel off.

See also: CHANNELS, GOSSIP, TELL
~

51 HERO =~
Syntax: hero <message>

A channel is provided for heroes to communicate with one another.  This is
similar to the other public channels, like gossip, etc. The equals sign,
'=' is a synonym for hero.

Immortals may also use the hero channel.

See also: GOSSIP, CHANNELS, HEROQUEST
~

0 CHANNELS~
Syntax: channels

The CHANNELS command is used to tell you the status (on or off) of all of
your communications channels, e.g. gossip, shouts, info, etc..

To toggle the on/off status of a channel, type the name of that channel.

WHAT IS RECORDED
Everything said on a channel is kept two ways: in the shared history that
HISTORY reads back, and in a staff log.  You only ever see the channels you
are allowed to hear - a channel you have switched off, or one above your
rank, is not in your history and is not sent to your client.

SAY is recorded for staff and shown to everyone in the room, but it is not
in the shared history: see HISTORY for why.

Tells are private.  They are never in the shared history and never in the
staff log; each character keeps their own copy and no one else can read it.

See also: GOSSIP, SHOUT, TELL, SAY, HISTORY, DEAF, QUIET
~

