Areas added after original table:
102 tritia    111 ringfort     154 forsaken     155 sinistra    200 hcult
201 prison    205 emerald      260 scult        267 ashen       270 trcult

See also: VNUM, MEMORY
~


63 CLONE~
Syntax:
  clone obj <object>
  clone mob <mobile>
  clone <name>

The clone command duplicates an object or mobile.  The level of item or mobile
that may be cloned depends on your level.  The target object or mob must be in
the same room as you, or in your possession.  Strung and set information is
preserved by clone, as well as any spell effects and container contents or
mobile inventory, except for items which are too high for you to clone.
Strung extended descriptions on objects are not kept, however.

See also: LOAD, VNUM, STRING
~

69 ADVANCE~
Syntax: advance <character> <level>

ADVANCE sets a character's level to the specified value.  It is the
only way to create new immortal characters within the game.  ADVANCE
may also be used to demote characters.

See also: TRUST, WIZLIST
~

70 TRUST~
Syntax: trust <character> <level>

TRUST sets a character's trust to the specified level.  This is similar to
advancing the character for accessing immortal commands, but has no effect
on combat strength, et cetera.  You can give more trust to mortal (or
immortal) characters to give them access to more power without messing up
the character.  You can also restrict a player's trust to less than their
natural level.

A trust of 0 means to use the character's natural level again.

See also: ADVANCE, WIZLIST
~

67 ALLOW BAN~
Syntax: allow   <site>
        ban     <site>
        ban

BAN <site> prohibits players from that site from logging in.  BAN with
no arguments lists the banned sites.  ALLOW <site> removes a site from
the ban list.

The site ban test works by suffix comparison, so if you ban 'foo.edu',
all sites within 'foo.edu' are banned as well.

Site bans are not saved; every time the server is rebooted, the site
ban list is cleared.

See also: BAN, DNS
~

65 GATHER~
Syntax: GATHER <object name>
        gather <object name> <character>
        gather <object vnum>

***WARNING***
This command is very powerful.  Upmost discretion must be taken
in its use.

The command will gather all items in the game that match the object
fields name. Example:

gather sword

This will gather all items that have the word 'sword' in the
'name' field of the object.

This command should only be used to gather quest items that are
no longer in use, or objects that are suspected of being a bug
in the game.

The second form of the gather command, with a character's name as the
argument, will gather all of the objects that match the name from that
specific character.

The third version will gather all objects that have a corresponding
vnum.

See also: SPLIT, GIVE, GET
~

69 REBOOT SHUTDOWN~
Syntax: reboot
        shutdown

REBOOT shuts down and immediately restarts the game server.  When launched
under the normal startup script, the server pauses roughly fifteen seconds
for connections to drain, then launches a fresh copy.  All characters are
automatically saved before the reboot sequence begins.

  reboot  - safe restart; the startup script brings the server back up
            automatically after the delay

SHUTDOWN shuts the server down and signals the startup script NOT to
restart it.  Use shutdown only when the server needs to stay offline for
maintenance, patching, or emergency fixes.

  shutdown - permanent stop; requires manual intervention to bring the
             server back up

Both commands are implementor-level only.  Before using either, notify
the rest of the admin team so players are not caught off-guard.
A brief WIZINFO warning before shutdown is strongly recommended.

See also: WIZINFO, GECHO, WIZLOCK
~

66 WIZLOCK~
Syntax: wizlock

WIZLOCK locks the game against new logins.  All players below immortal rank
(level 62 and lower) are refused entry - except link-dead characters who are
returning to their existing sessions, who are allowed back in.

Wizlock should be used sparingly: typically only during a full reboot,
critical maintenance, or an exploit emergency.  When wizlocking, send a
GECHO or WIZINFO first so online players know what is happening.

Type WIZLOCK again to unlock the game.

See also: NEWLOCK, SHUTDOWN, REBOOT, DENY
~

62 NEWLOCK~
Syntax: newlock

NEWLOCK blocks new character creation.  Existing characters can still log
in normally; only the creation of brand-new characters is prevented.  This
is typically used when dealing with a ban-evading spammer who keeps making
new alts.

Type NEWLOCK again to re-enable character creation.

See also: WIZLOCK, DENY, BAN
~

67 DENY~
Syntax: deny <name>

DENY permanently locks a named character out of the game.  The player will
be unable to log in with that character; the game simply disconnects them
during the login sequence.  This is a hard ban on a character file.

DENY should be reserved for serious, repeated violations where a FREEZE or
timeout is insufficient.  Unlike FREEZE, a denied character cannot be
unblocked from within the game unless a staff member explicitly uses UNDENY.

See also: UNDENY, FREEZE, BAN, WIZLOCK
~

67 UNDENY~
Syntax: undeny <name>

UNDENY removes a previously applied DENY flag from a character, restoring
their ability to log in.  Only supply the character name - they do not need
to be online for UNDENY to take effect.

See also: DENY
~

67 DUMP~
Syntax: dump <name>

DUMP (formerly DISCONNECT) breaks a player's network link, returning them to
their telnet prompt or their client's connection screen.  The character
remains in the game briefly in a link-dead state and saves normally.

Use DUMP as a non-lethal first response to spammers or players who need to
be cleanly logged off.  If the player reconnects and continues misbehaving,
consider FREEZE, DENY, or escalate to BAN.

See also: FREEZE, DENY, BAN, SNOOP
~

64 FREEZE~
Syntax: freeze <name>

FREEZE <character> is a toggle which prevents a character from issuing any
commands at all. It can be removed from within the game, so freezing a
troublemaker is usually a better option than the deny command.

See also: DENY, PETRIFY, STASIS, MUTE
~

63 FORCE~
Syntax: force <character> <command>
Syntax: force all <command>

FORCE forces one character to execute a command, except of course delete.

Below level 67 force can only be used on mobiles, and force all is
unavailable.  You also cannot 'force orc murder hugo', for example.

FORCE 'all' forces all player characters to execute a command.
This is typically used for 'force all save'.

See also: TRANSFER, AT, SOCKETS
~

68 MAXLOAD~
Syntax: maxload set <vnum> <amount>
        maxload list
        maxload pla <vnum>

MAXLOAD sets, lists the amount of objects the game should load depending on
vnum. Maxload set & Maxload pla are level 70 commands, while Maxload list
can be done at 68.

In case of maxload set you have options for amount:
-1        -> remove the maxload
0..number -> add the maxload

Remember to check, because if the maxload is lower as the sum of in_game
and with the players, the item will not load anymore, until enough items
got extracted so that the sum of the items with the players and in the game
is LESS as the maxload value specified. Which can take a while....

See also: MEMORY, VNUM
~

63 LASTLOG~
Syntax: lastlog
        lastlog <count>
        lastlog <count> <name>
        lastlog <name>

LASTLOG lists the most recent logins, newest first, with the date and time,
the character, whether the session was a new character, an ordinary connect,
or a reconnect, how long the session lasted, and the host the player came
from.

A session has no length until it ends, so the Played column shows a dash for
a player who is still connected, and for the login half of a finished
session.  The closing row carries the time.  Sessions that ended in a hard
crash were never closed and keep the dash.

The Event column says how the session ended as well as how it began:
quit is a deliberate exit, linkdead a dropped connection, and shutdown a
server restart.

SOCKETS only sees a descriptor while it is still attached, so it cannot tell
you who was here an hour ago.  LASTLOG reads a journal the server appends at
every login, so the history survives both disconnects and server restarts.

Without arguments the last 20 logins are shown.  Supply a count to widen or
narrow that, and a name to restrict the list to one character.  The server
keeps the most recent 200 entries.

Records contain player IP addresses.  Treat the output as private operational
data and do not paste it into public channels.

See also: SOCKETS, FINGER, WHOIS
~

63 SOCKETS~
Syntax: sockets
        sockets <name>
        sockets ip <ip address>

SOCKETS reports all of the visible users connected to the server, including
users in the process of logging in.  The two numbers inside brackets are the
channel number and the 'connected' state.  The 'connected' states are listed in
merc.h:

PLAYING                      0
GET_NAME                     1
GET_OLD_PASSWORD             2
CONFIRM_NEW_NAME             3
GET_NEW_PASSWORD             4
CONFIRM_NEW_PASSWORD         5
GET_NEW_RACE                 6
GET_NEW_SEX                  7
GET_NEW_CLASS                8
GET_ALIGNMENT                9
DEFAULT_CHOICE              10
GEN_GROUPS                  11
PICK_WEAPON                 12
READ_IMOTD                  13
READ_MOTD                   14
BREAK_CONNECT               15
RETRY_PASSWORD              16
COPYOVER_RECOVER            17

The optional name argument shows the site of a player, or optional can be used
to identify switched gods (i.e. socket orc shows the name of anyone switched
into an orc).

You can also use the IP argument to list only the players from a certain site.
Example:  socket ip nowhere.com

See also: TRANSFER, STAT, MEMORY

~

65 LOAD~
Syntax: load mob <vnum>
        load obj <vnum> <level>

The load command is used to load new objects or mobiles (use clone to
duplicate strung items and mobs).  The vnums can be found with the vnum
command, or by stat'ing an existing mob or object.

Load puts objects in inventory if they can be carried.  Otherwise they are
put in the room.  Mobiles are always put into the same room as the god. Old
format objects must be given a level argument to determine their power, new
format objects have a preset level that cannot be changed without set.

See also: CLONE, VNUM, STAT
~

67 SLOOKUP~
This command will show the slot number, and skill number used by the
system. It is useful for finding out numbers to use when 'setting'
a potion or scroll, and various other items.

See also: LOAD, BREW, SCRIBE
~

66 SET~
Syntax: set char  <player> <field> <value>
        set mob   <mobile> <field> <value>
        set obj   <object> <field> <value>
        set room  <number> <field> <value>
        set skill <char>   <name>  <rating>
        set skill <char>   all     <rating>

SET changes a live value on a character, object or room. The change takes
effect immediately and, for a player, is saved with them. Type any of the
forms above without a field to see what that form accepts.

SET CHAR / SET MOB
  Stats       str int wis dex con
  Identity    sex class race level align guild
  Resources   hp mana end move maxmove practice train exp
  Combat      hitroll damroll armor wimpy hunt annoy
  Currency    platinum gold silver copper bank qp
  Conditions  thirst hunger drunk timer
  Estates     castle castlehead mountable were
  Flags       pkiller excon wanted freeze deny
  Records     dcount pkills

  armor sets all four armour classes at once; setting one and not the
  others is almost never what is meant.

  Carry capacity has no field of its own: it is computed from strength,
  dexterity and level, so raise those instead. Every player already carries
  half again what that formula alone would allow.

  pkiller  <on|off>   toggle PKiller status
  excon    <on|off>   toggle ex-convict (con-death) flag
  wanted   <on|off>   toggle WANTED flag
  freeze   <on|off>   freeze or unfreeze a player (saved)
  deny     <on|off>   grant or revoke login access (saved)

SET OBJ
  Numbers     level weight cost timer type condition material

  COST is in copper.  A gold piece is 10000, a silver 100.  So

    set obj bread cost 1          one copper
    set obj sword cost 50000      five gold
  Values      value0 value1 value2 value3 value4  (v0 to v4)
  Flags       extra wear
  Text        name short long

  value0 to value4 mean different things for each item type; STAT OBJ
  shows what they currently hold. Setting a portal or a manipulation item
  requires level 70.

  Flag fields (extra, wear, and SET ROOM's flags) take the same letters
  the area files and STAT use. Give them together, and lead with + to add
  to what is already set or - to take away:

    set obj sword extra AG        replace: glow and magic, nothing else
    set obj sword extra +H        add nodrop, keep the rest
    set obj sword extra -H        remove nodrop, keep the rest
    set room 3700 flags +R        make this room newbies-only
    set room 3700 flags -R        stop it being newbies-only

  Without + or - the whole field is replaced, so a bare list clears every
  flag you did not name. A plain number still works if you have one.

  EXTRA flags:
    A glow            B hum             C dark            D lock
    E evil            F invis           G magic           H nodrop
    I bless           J anti-good       K anti-evil       L anti-neutral
    M noremove        N inventory       O nopurge         P rot on death
    Q vanish on death R metal           S bounce          T teleport
    U no identify     V no locate       W race restricted X adds affect
    Y embalmed

  WEAR flags -- where it can go. A is required for anything a player is
  meant to pick up at all:
    A take            B finger          C neck            D body
    E head            F legs            G feet            H hands
    I arms            J shield          K about body      L waist
    M wrist           N wield           O hold            P two handed

  TYPE numbers:
     1 light      2 scroll     3 wand       4 staff      5 weapon
     8 treasure   9 armor     10 potion    11 clothing  12 furniture
    13 trash     15 container 17 drink can 18 key       19 food
    20 money     22 boat      23 npc corpse 24 pc corpse 25 fountain
    26 pill      27 protect   28 map       29 scuba gear 30 portal
    31 manipulation           32 castle relic           33 saddle
    34 herb      35 spell component        36 soul container
    37 action

SET ROOM
  Numbers     flags sector
  Text        name description

  A room you made yourself with GOTO is yours to change at any staff rank.
  A room that came out of an area file may only be changed by an
  implementor, because it is somebody else's work and RSAVE can now make
  the change permanent.  See BUILDING.

  FLAGS -- same letters, same + and - rules as above:
    A dark            B jail            C no mobs         D indoors
    E river           F teleport        G cult entrance   H affected by
    I death trap      J private         K safe            L solitary
    M pet shop        N no recall       O implementor only P gods only
    Q heroes only     R newbies only    S law             T hp regen
    U mana regen      V arena           W castle join     X silent
    Y pathfinder mark

  SECTOR numbers:
     0 inside     1 city       2 field      3 forest     4 hills
     5 mountain   6 swimmable water         7 unswimmable water
     8 underwater 9 air       10 desert    11 underground

SET SKILL
  set skill <char> <skill or spell> <percent>
  set skill <char> all <percent>            (players only)

Text fields take the rest of the line, spaces included, and replace what
was there.

Changes to mobiles and objects are to the one in front of you and last
until it is reset or the game reboots. Changes to rooms are the same until
you RSAVE, which writes that area's rooms to its file and makes them
permanent. See BUILDING.

See also: BUILDING, RSAVE, RLINK, STRING, STAT, MSET, OSET, RSET
~

64 STRING~
Syntax: string obj <field> <string>
        string mob <field> <string>

The string command changes the various strings associated with an object or
mobile.  A full list of fields can be viewed by typing string obj or string
mob.  Sorry, multi-line strings are not yet supported.

For the action_to_room and action_to_char strings.  Use $p for the object
name and $n for the player name if you want those in the message.

DO NOT USE ANY OTHER VARIABLES, DOING SO WILL CAUSE THE MUD TO CRASH.

See also: STAT, LOAD, CLONE
~

63 NOCHANNEL~
Syntax: nochannels <character>

NOCHANNELS is the most broad communication restriction for a misbehaving
player.  It prevents the target from using ANY global channel: gossip,
auction, info, music, Q/A, etc.  The target can still say, tell, and
emote normally.  Toggle again to restore channel privileges.

Typically used for persistent channel spammers or players using channels
for language violations.  For a complete communication lockdown, use MUTE
instead, which also strips tell, shout, say, and emote simultaneously.

See also: MUTE, NOSHOUT, NOEMOTE, NONOTE
~

66 PARDON~
Syntax: pardon <character> <flag>
  Flags: wanted excon pkiller warned freeze deny all

PARDON removes a punishment or status flag from a player.  The flag names
are:

  wanted   -- clears the WANTED flag
  excon    -- clears the EXCON (ex-convict / con-death) flag
  pkiller  -- clears active PKiller status
  warned   -- clears the WARNED flag
  freeze   -- removes the FREEZE (total lockout) flag
  deny     -- restores login access denied by the DENY flag
  all      -- clears WANTED, EXCON, PKILLER, and WARNED in one step

The old keywords "killer" and "thief" are accepted as aliases for "wanted"
and "excon" respectively.  Don't pardon a player unless you are sure they
deserve it.  Characters are saved immediately after any flag change.

See also: SET, FREEZE, DENY
~

66 PURGE~
Syntax: purge
        purge <character>

Purge is used to clean up the world.  Purge with no arguments removes all the
NPC's and objects in the current room.  Purge with an argument purges one
character from anywhere in the world.

Purge will not get rid of PC's, or objects or mobiles with a NOPURGE flag
set (i.e. the pit, the fountain, shopkeepers).  Mobiles may be
purged if they are called directly by name.

See also: SLAY, RESTORE, LOAD
~


66 RESTORE~
Syntax: restore <character>
        restore room
        restore all

Restore restores full hit points, mana points, and movement points to the
target character.  It also heals poison, plague, and blindness.

Restore room (or restore with no argument) performs a restore on every player
in the room, restore all does the same for all connected players. Restore all
is only usable by creators and implementors.  Restore should be used sparingly
or not at all.

See also: PURGE, SLAY
~

61 HERBIE~
Syntax: herbie <player>

Summons Herbie the Angel directly to an online player's location.  Herbie
arrives in person, kneels beside the player, channels a golden healing
light, then departs.  The player and everyone in the room sees the full
sequence of acts.

Herbie cures: poison, plague, blindness, sleep, and curse.
Herbie restores: hit points, mana, movement, hunger, and thirst.

Cannot be used on players of equal or higher level.  Cannot target mobs.

See also: RESTORE, EMPOWER
~

66 SPELLUP SPELLPURGE HERMIE~
Syntax: spellup
Syntax: spellpurge

Spellup places Hermie the Angel -- Herbie's girlfriend -- in your current
room.  She stays there, taking requests, until somebody removes her.

Players talk to her with SAY.  Saying her name, or 'menu', 'list', 'hello'
or 'help', makes her read out her list; after that they say either the
number or the name of what they want and she casts it on them.  Saying
'all' gets them every entry in one go.

She ignores anything else said in the room, so she can be parked somewhere
busy without burying every conversation in her menu.

She offers armor, bless, shield, stone skin, sanctuary, haste, fly, pass
door, protection evil, fire shield, frost shield, divine protection, giant
strength, frenzy, major globe, infravision, detect invis, detect hidden,
detect magic, detect good, detect evil, refresh, heal and restore mana
individually, plus empower and titanic as bundles.

Every spell she hands out lasts 30 ticks, whoever asks for it, and every
one is written to the game log.

She is a permanent fixture, not a visitor.  Remove the one in front of you
with PURGE, or remove every copy of her in the world with SPELLPURGE.

See also: HERBIE, EMPOWER, TITANIC, PURGE
~

66 SLAY~
Syntax: slay <character>

Slay kills a character in cold blood, no saving throw.  Best not to use this
command on players if you enjoy being a god.

See also: PURGE, RESTORE, KILL
~

63 TRANSFER TELEPORT~
Syntax: transfer <character>
        transfer all
        transfer <character> <location>
        transfer all         <location>

Transfer transfers the target character, or ALL player characters,
to your current location (default) or to a specified location. Trans all
should almost never be used, as 100 players popping into one room can have
horrible effects on slow links. Teleport is a synonym for transfer.

See also: GOTO, AT, FORCE
~

63 AT~
Syntax: at <location> <command>

At executes the given command (which may have arguments) at the given
location.  The location may be specified as a vnum, as the name of
a mobile, or as the name of an object.

At works by temporarily moving you to that location, executing the
command, and then moving you back (if the command didn't change your
location).

See also: GOTO, TRANSFER, FORCE
~

64 ECHO~
Syntax: echo <message>

ECHO sends a raw message to all characters in your current room.  No name
or attribution is shown - the text appears verbatim on every nearby screen.
Useful for producing custom room flavor text or staging events.  Immortals
of higher trust will see a faint staff tag prepended to the message.

See also: GECHO, PECHO
~

64 GECHO~
Syntax: gecho <message>

GECHO broadcasts a message to every connected player on the entire MUD,
regardless of location.  No attribution is appended.  Use sparingly -
unnecessary global echoes are disruptive and are logged.

See also: ECHO, PECHO, ANNOUNCE
~

64 PECHO~
Syntax: pecho <player> <message>

PECHO sends a raw private message directly to a single player's screen.
No name or source is shown.  The player sees only the text you type.
Useful for delivering event narration or hints without breaking immersion.

See also: ECHO, GECHO, TELL
~

62 GOTO~
Syntax: goto <location>

Goto takes you to a location.  The location may be specified as a vnum,
as the name of a mobile, or as the name of an object.

You may not Goto a room if it is PRIVATE and has two (or more) characters
already present, or if it is SOLITARY and has one (or more) characters
already present. Some other rooms are barred to players below a certain
god level.

See also: TRANSFER, AT, STAT
~

63 EXPLODE~
Syntax: explode container <container name>
        explode room

This command will scatter mobs in rooms, or items in containers in your
inventory all over the MUD. Its main use is to simplify placing quest
oriented stuff into the game. Item 26 is a generic item with a no_locate
flag on it that can be used as a quest item.

See also: PURGE, LOAD, VNUM
~

62 HOLYLIGHT~
Syntax: holylight

HOLYLIGHT is a toggle.  When active, you can see virtually everything in
the game regardless of visibility conditions: invisible characters, mobs,
and objects; dark rooms; hidden players; and camouflaged entities.

When HOLYLIGHT is off, you see what mortal players see - useful for testing
how a room or mob appears to players without elevated vision.

Holylight can be toggled on and off at any time.  It does not affect
whether other players can see you.

See also: WIZINVIS, INVIS, DETECT
~

62 WIZINVIS~
Syntax: wizinvis [level]

WIZINVIS sets your visibility level.  Any player whose level is below your
wizinvis level will be completely unable to see you - you won't appear in
the room, won't show in WHO, and your actions produce no messages to them.

  wizinvis      - sets invisibility to your own level (maximum stealth)
  wizinvis 70   - total invisibility to everyone except other 70s
  wizinvis 0    - removes wizinvis; you become fully visible again
  wizinvis 62   - visible to other immortals but hidden from all mortals

Players who CAN see you (at or above your wizi level) see your name flagged
as (Wizi) in the room and WHO list.

See also: HOLYLIGHT, INVIS, POOFIN, POOFOUT
~

65 MEMORY~
Syntax: memory

Memory reports the size of the mud.

The 'Mobs' and 'Objs' lines report the number of types of mobiles and objects,
not the number of instances currently in the game.

The 'Strings' line shows memory used by area-file strings.  These strings have
a maximum limit, which is also shown.

The 'Perms' line shows permanently allocated memory blocks.  There is no limit
on the number and size of these blocks.

See also: SOCKETS, STAT
~

65 VNUM~
Syntax: vnum <name>
        vnum <obj or mob> <name>
        vnum skill <name>

Vnum finds all objects and mobiles with a particular name, and prints out
the results.  Use vnum obj or vnum mob to limit the search.  This vnum is
very useful for loading. Vnum skill returns the skill number (for set) and
the slot number (for making new zones) of a skill name.

See also: LOAD, STAT, AREAVNUMS
~

63 STAT~
Syntax: stat <name>
        stat mob  <name>
        stat obj  <name>
        stat room <number>

The stat command gives you detailed information on an object, mobile, or room
within the game.  Without a class argument, the command will return an object
(if found), a mobile (if no object exists, but a mobile does), or a room
(if a number is  given).  Stat room with no argument stats the room you are
standing in.  Stat can be used to find room vnums for goto.

See also: GOTO, TRANSFER, VNUM
~

62 CLOAK~
Syntax: cloak <level>

Cloak will make you invisible to anyone not in the same room with you.  Anyone
is higher level than the level you set your cloak at can see you anywhere though.

See also: WIZHELP, IMMORTAL
~

69 BACKUP~
Syntax: backup
        backup now

This command will perform a backup on all of the pfiles.  Typing backup by itself
will show when the next scheduled backup will take place.

See also: MEMORY, SOCKETS

~

69 PSTAT~
Syntax: pstat

Pstat shows you various statistics of all the players connected to the mud.

See also: SOCKETS, MEMORY
~

62 MWHERE~
Syntax: mwhere <keyword>

MWHERE searches for all loaded instances of any mobile whose name field
matches the given keyword and displays their room name and vnum.
The search is case-insensitive and matches any part of the mob name.

Example:
  mwhere guard
  City Guard          [  2345]
  North Gate Guard    [ 12001]

See also: OWHERE, RWHERE, GWHERE, MSTAT
~

62 OWHERE~
Syntax: owhere <keyword>

OWHERE locates all instances of an object whose name field matches the
given keyword anywhere in the game world - carried, worn, in containers,
or on mob corpses.  Displays object name, holder/location, and room vnum.

See also: MWHERE, RWHERE, GWHERE, VNUM
~

62 RWHERE~
Syntax: rwhere <string>

RWHERE searches the room name of every loaded room for a partial match
and returns the room name and vnum.  Case-insensitive.  Useful for
locating a room when you only know part of its name.

Example:
  rwhere hermit
  Hermit's Hut          [  331]
  Hermit's Lair         [ 1589]
  The Hermit's Corner   [ 6373]

See also: MWHERE, OWHERE, GWHERE, GOTO
~

62 GWHERE~
Syntax: gwhere [keyword]

GWHERE with no argument lists every player currently connected, their
room name, and their room vnum.  With a keyword argument it filters to
mobiles matching that name, similar to MWHERE.

See also: MWHERE, OWHERE, RWHERE, WIZCHECK
~

62 QFLAG~
Syntax: qflag <player>

This command sets a (Quest) flag on players that will keep them from losing
experience or equipment during quests.  Remember to remove the quest flag from
the player after the quest is over.  The flag will automatically be removed when
the player quits otherwise.

See also: WIZHELP, IMMORTAL

~

64 PEACE~
Syntax: peace

Peace causes all characters in a room to stop fighting. It also strips the
AGGRESSIVE bit from mobiles.

See also: SLAY, RESTORE, FORCE
~

62 POOFIN~
Syntax: poofin <message>

POOFIN sets the message other players see when you use GOTO to arrive in
their room.  The default is '$n appears in a swirling mist.'  Your
custom message must include your name somewhere (the game inserts $n for
you if you include the literal text of your name, or simply write it
in naturally).  Only players who can see you will see the message.

Type 'poofin' with no argument to reset to the default message.

Example:  poofin Galdor rides in on a bolt of lightning.

See also: POOFOUT, GOTO, INVIS
~

62 POOFOUT~
Syntax: poofout <message>

POOFOUT sets the message other players see when you leave a room via GOTO.
The default is '$n leaves in a swirling mist.'  Same rules as POOFIN -
the message should read naturally with your name included, and only visible
characters will see it.

Type 'poofout' with no argument to reset to the default message.

Example:  poofout Galdor vanishes with a thunderclap.

See also: POOFIN, GOTO, INVIS
~

66 LOG~
Syntax: log <character>
        log all

LOG toggles command logging for the specified player.  When enabled, every
command the player types is written to the server log file.  This is a
persistent per-character toggle that survives reboots.

  log <name>  - toggle logging for that specific player
  log all     - toggle server-wide logging of ALL players (session only;
                does not persist across reboots)

Note: PASSWORD commands are never logged regardless of this flag.
All immortal commands are always logged regardless of this flag.
Requires creator level or higher.  Inform an implementor before use.

See also: SNOOP, PSTAT
~

67 SNOOP~
Syntax: snoop <character>
        snoop <your own name>

SNOOP lets you monitor a live copy of all input and output for the target
character.  You see exactly what they type and exactly what their screen
shows, in real time.  You may snoop multiple characters simultaneously.

Snoop yourself (or your own name) to cancel ALL active snoops at once.

Privacy guidelines:
  - Only use snoop if you have a legitimate reason (cheating investigation,
    bug reproduction, or explicit admin need).
  - Ask for permission when the purpose is debugging rather than enforcement.
  - Do not use snoop purely out of curiosity.  Snoops are logged and you
    may be questioned about their use.

See also: LOG, SWITCH, PSTAT
~

64 SWITCH~
Syntax: switch <mobile>

SWITCH transfers your consciousness into the target mobile, giving you
direct control of it.  You type commands as if you were that mob - its
stats, skills, and position all apply.  Your immortal body is left standing
in place while you are switched.

Restrictions:
  - You can only switch into mobs, not player characters.
  - You cannot switch into a mob that already has a descriptor (i.e. one
    another immortal is already controlling).
  - You cannot switch into link-dead PCs.

Use RETURN to exit the mob and return to your immortal body.
If the mob is killed while you are switched, you are ejected safely.

See also: RETURN, FORCE, AT
~

64 RETURN~
Syntax: return

RETURN exits a mobile body and returns your immortal character to normal
control.  Use this after SWITCH to come back to yourself.  If you are
not currently switched into a mobile, RETURN has no effect.

See also: SWITCH
~

62 IMMTALK :~
Syntax: immtalk <message>
        immtalk
        : <message>

IMMTALK (alias :) sends a message to all immortals who have the immortal
channel active.  Messages are prefixed with your name and level so other
staff can identify the sender.

Using IMMTALK with no argument toggles your own immortal channel on or off.
When off, you will neither send nor receive immtalk messages.

See also: GODTALK (@), HERO (=), WIZINFO, BEEP, CHANNELS
~

62 WIZHELP~
Syntax: wizhelp

Wizhelp provides a list of all the immortal commands available to you.

See also: COMMANDS, SUMMARY
~

69 COMPONENT~
Syntax: component

Component command forces the scattering of random components throughout
the world, rather than waiting for the MUD to do it randomly over time.
This was added to restore components after reboots or crashes that wipe
the current components from the world.

(do 'owhere component' to see the current components in the world.)

See also: LOAD, VNUM
~

65 EDIT~
Syntax: edit exit <direction> <field> <value>

EDIT modifies live room exit data without requiring a full area reload.
The only currently supported field is 'exit', which lets you adjust exit
flags, door states, or key vnums on the fly for testing or event purposes.

Use STAT ROOM to inspect current exit values before editing.  Changes made
with EDIT persist only until the next area reset or reboot; they are not
written to the area file.

See also: STAT, GOTO, REPOP
~

62 SHOWHUNT~
Syntax: showhunt

SHOWHUNT reports how many mobile NPCs currently have an active hunt target
set.  Hunting mobs pursue their targets across rooms until the quarry is
killed, escapes too far, or the hunt expires.  High hunt counts can affect
server performance; this command helps staff identify if hunting behavior
is running out of control.

See also: HPARDON, MWHERE
~

62 HPARDON~
Syntax: hpardon <character>
        hpardon all

HPARDON cancels any active hunts targeting the specified character, causing
all mobs currently pursuing them to abandon the chase.  HPARDON ALL clears
every active hunt in the game simultaneously.

Use this when a hunt becomes stuck, when a player is being griefed by
relentless hunting mobs after a crash or bug, or to clean up testing.

See also: SHOWHUNT
~

67 RESTORECHAR~
Syntax: restorechar <player> <date>

Restores a character's pfile as of the specified date.  Date format is,
for example, Jun.1.  Restorechar is intended for use in restoring characters
with bad pfiles, not those who have deleted themselves.

See also: RESTORE, PURGE
~

67 SWEDISH~
Syntax: swedish <character>

Makes the victim talk like a swede.  Remember to remove it from the player
before you or they leave.

See also: WIZHELP
~

63 WIZINFO~
Syntax: wizinfo
Syntax: wizinfo <message>

WIZINFO with no argument toggles whether you receive the stream of automatic
staff notifications: logins, logouts, link-death events, level-ups, and other
significant game events flagged with LOG_ALWAYS.  Most immortals keep this
enabled.

WIZINFO with a message broadcasts that message to all immortals who have
WIZINFO enabled, similar to an immortal-only announcement channel.  Use it
to coordinate with staff, warn about reboots, or share important information.

See also: IMMTALK, GODTALK, ANNOUNCE
~

68 WHINE~
Syntax: whine <character>

Flags target character as a "WHINER", this prevents them from noting to
all or immortal, and causes cityguards to taunt them incessantly. This
command is NOT to be abused!

See also: WIZHELP, PUNISHED
~

68 NONOTE~
Syntax: nonote <character>

This command completely removes target character's noting privileges.
This is to be used only as a last resort for players that have
consistently abused their note privilege.

See also: NOTE, WIZHELP
~

0 ' SAY~
Syntax: '<message>
Syntax: say <message>

The apostrophe (') is a shortcut for the SAY command.  Typing:
   'Hello everyone!
is identical to:
   say Hello everyone!

SAY speaks aloud to everyone in your current room.  Only people in the same
room can hear you.  Use TELL for private messages to individuals, or GOSSIP
for world-wide chat.

See also: TELL, GOSSIP, YELL
~

0 ; GTELL~
Syntax: ;<message>
Syntax: gtell <message>

The semicolon (;) is a shortcut for the GTELL command.  Typing:
   ;Careful, there's a trap ahead.
is identical to:
   gtell Careful, there's a trap ahead.

GTELL sends a message privately to all members of your current group,
regardless of where they are in the world.  Use GROUP to see who is in your
party.  Note that you must have no space between the semicolon and your
message.

See also: TELL, GOSSIP, GROUP
~

0 'ASTRAL WALK' ASTRAL~
Syntax: astral <target>

ASTRAL WALK is a psionic travel ability that teleports you to another
character anywhere in the world.  The target must be visible to you and
not in a protected room (private, safe, immortal-only, etc.).  The spell
costs 70 mana and requires training in the astral_walk psionic skill.

It can take you out of a no-recall room, but never into one; a jail holds
you either way.

If successful, you (and your pet if nearby) shimmer and appear next to
the target.  If you fail your concentration check, you lose a small amount
of mana without teleporting.  The target receives no warning of your arrival.

See also: PROJECT, SHIFT
~

0 'EGO WHIP' EGOWHIP~
Syntax: ego whip <target>

EGO WHIP is a psionic attack that assaults the victim's mind, dealing
mental damage and potentially reducing one of their primary stats (STR,
INT, WIS, or DEX) for a period of time.  The stat penalty increases with
your level.  Costs 20 mana per use.

If the target already has the ego whip debuff, additional uses deal damage
without adding another stat reduction.  Useful against physically strong
foes since it bypasses armor entirely.  Requires training in the ego_whip
psionic skill.

See also: MINDBLAST
~

0 ENERVATE~
Syntax: enervate <target>

ENERVATE is a psionic assault that directly tears the target's life force
and physical stamina away, siphoning them back into the caster.  It belongs
to the Control psionic discipline alongside clairvoyance, confuse, and mind
leech.

Mechanics:
  - Deals mental damage to HP equal to level to level*2.
  - Drains endurance (move) equal to level to level*2, floored at 0.
  - Caster absorbs half the HP damage as healing.
  - Caster absorbs half the endurance drained as restored movement.
  - Mana cost: 35.

Enervate is particularly punishing against physically active foes since it
simultaneously degrades their ability to fight, flee, and sustain themselves.
Requires training in the enervate psionic skill.

See also: MINDLEECH, EGO WHIP, TORMENT
~

60 IMOTD~
Syntax: imotd

Displays the Immortal Message of the Day, which contains important notices
and updates specifically for immortal staff members.  This message is shown
automatically when an immortal logs in.

See also: MOTD, NEWS, JOBS
~

64 IPORTAL~
Syntax: iportal <target> [timer] [description]

IPORTAL creates an immortal portal in your current room leading to the
specified destination.  The target can be either a room vnum or the name
of a mob/player currently online.

Options:
  timer        - Number of ticks before the portal disappears.  Use 0 for
                 permanent.  Default is 0.
  description  - Custom description for the portal.  If omitted, the portal
                 appears as "(Shimmering) <default portal text>".

Examples:
  iportal 3001               - Permanent portal to room 3001
  iportal 3001 10            - Portal to room 3001 lasting 10 ticks
  iportal Brak 0 A swirling vortex of energy crackles here.

The portal carries 10000 charges and is not locked to you, so anyone who
finds it can use it.  Treat it as a door you are leaving open in whatever
room you drop it in.

To remove one: SMASH clears every object in the room and leaves the mobs
alone, or PURGE <portal> removes just that one.

See also: SMASH, PURGE, GATE, GOTO, TRANSFER, RECALL
~

69 @ GODTALK~
Syntax: @<message>

The @ symbol is a shortcut for the GODTALK channel, available only to gods
(level 69+).  This is a private channel for the highest immortals.

Typing:
   @Anyone around for a reboot?
is identical to:
   godtalk Anyone around for a reboot?

See also: IMMTALK (:), HERO (=)
~

70 DUMP_EXITS~
Syntax: dump_exits

DUMP_EXITS writes a complete dump of all room exits in the game to the
file "exits.dmp" in the area directory.  This is an IMP-only diagnostic
command used for debugging area connectivity or verifying exit data.

The dump includes source room vnum, destination vnum, and exit direction
for every exit defined in the loaded areas.

See also: AREAVNUMS, STAT, VNUM
~

64 ANNOUNCE~
Syntax: announce <message>

ANNOUNCE broadcasts a highlighted global message to every player online.
The message is framed with a colored banner so it stands out from regular
game text.  Use it for server-wide news, events, or important notices.

See also: WIZINFO, GECHO
~

63 REPOP~
Syntax: repop

REPOP forces an immediate reset of the area you are currently standing in.
It is equivalent to the area aging out naturally, respawning all mobs and
objects according to the area's reset data.  The area age is randomized
after the reset so the normal timer continues from a low value.

Useful for testing area resets or quickly restocking a cleared zone.

See also: LOAD, VNUM
~

64 STASIS~
Syntax: stasis <player>

STASIS is a toggle that locks a player in their current room.  While in
stasis the player can still communicate (say, tell, chat, etc.) but cannot
move, flee, or recall.  Toggling stasis again releases them.  The flag is
saved across logins.

See also: FREEZE, JAIL
~

65 SMASH~
Syntax: smash

SMASH removes all purgeable objects from the current room, leaving mobs
intact.  Objects with the NOPURGE flag (pits, fountains, etc.) are
unaffected.  A count of removed items is echoed back to the immortal.

See also: PURGE
~

62 RENAME~
Syntax: rename <player> <new_name>

RENAME changes an online player's character name and renames their save
file on disk accordingly.  The new name must be 2-12 alphabetic characters.
The player is notified of the change.  Immortals of equal or higher trust
cannot be renamed.

Use with caution - the old player file is deleted and replaced with a new
one under the new name immediately.

See also: STAT, PURGE
~

63 MUTE~
Syntax: mute <player>

MUTE silences a player completely - they cannot use say, tell, shout,
channels, or emote until the immortal toggles the mute off.  All
communication flags are set simultaneously.  Toggling mute again on a
muted player restores all speech privileges.  The flag is saved across
logins.

See also: NOCHANNELS, NOTELL, NOSHOUT, NOEMOTE
~

64 DRAG~
Syntax: drag <player>

DRAG instantly pulls an online player from wherever they are to your
current room, as if hauled by an unseen force.  Any active combat the
victim is engaged in is stopped first.  The dragged player sees a
notification and an automatic room description.

See also: TRANSFER
~

64 DUEL~
Syntax: duel <player1> <player2>

DUEL forces two online players into combat against each other.  Player2
is transported to player1's room; both are set PK-enabled and their
fighting is started automatically.  A global notification is sent to both
participants and to you.

Use responsibly - this will force real combat between the two players.

See also: SLAY, PEACE
~

63 IWEATHER~
Syntax: iweather <sunny|cloudy|rain|storm>

IWEATHER immediately forces a global weather change.  All outdoor rooms will
reflect the new conditions, and atmospheric transition messages are sent to
all players in outdoor areas.

  iweather sunny  - clear skies; barometric pressure rises to 1000+ mmhg
  iweather cloudy - overcast skies; pressure sits at a middling 750 mmhg
  iweather rain   - light-to-heavy rain; pressure drops below 700 mmhg
  iweather storm  - full lightning storm; pressure plummets below 500 mmhg

Storm and rain conditions affect certain spells and skills (e.g. call
lightning gains bonus damage during storms).  Sunny conditions speed up
healing ticks for outdoor characters.

The player-level WEATHER command (no arguments) simply reads the current
conditions and is separate from this immortal command.

See also: REPOP, LIGHTS
~

63 LIGHTS~
Syntax: lights

LIGHTS toggles the ROOM_DARK flag on your current room by reaching over
and flipping the proverbial light switch.  *CLICK*  When dark, mobs and
players without infravision or a light source cannot see clearly.
Flip it again to restore the room's lighting.  Let there be light!

See also: SEAL
~

63 SEAL~
Syntax: seal <direction>

SEAL toggles the WIZLOCKED flag on the specified exit of your current room.
A sealed exit cannot be passed by any player, regardless of level, until
unsealed.  Valid directions: north, east, south, west, up, down, ne, nw,
se, sw.

See also: LIGHTS
~

63 FINGER~
Syntax: finger <player>

FINGER retrieves information about a player.  If the player is online, their
live stats (level, race, class, title, HP/mana/move, room, playing time) are
shown.  If offline, the player file is scanned for level, race, class, last
login, and total playing time.

See also: ACTIVITY, PSTAT, WHOIS
~

63 ACTIVITY~
Syntax: activity <player>

ACTIVITY shows session statistics for a player.

If the player is online, live data for the current session is displayed:
  - Session login time and duration
  - Current level and experience (with session gains)
  - Mob kills, PK kills, deaths, and quests completed this session

If the player is offline, the last recorded session snapshot is shown with
the same fields from the moment they logged out.

Session data is tracked automatically from login to logout and written to
the player file on quit.

See also: FINGER, TRAIL, PSTAT
~

63 PRESTORE~
Syntax: prestore <player>
        prestore <player> <number>

PRESTORE manages versioned snapshots of a player's save file.

With just a player name, PRESTORE lists all available restore points in
order from newest to oldest, showing the date and time of each snapshot:

  prestore Conan
    [1] Fri Apr 04 2026 15:30:22   <- most recent
    [2] Fri Apr 04 2026 12:10:05
    [3] Thu Apr 03 2026 22:45:11

To restore a player to one of the listed points, specify the number:

  prestore Conan 2

Snapshots are taken automatically every time a player's file is saved.
Up to 30 versions are kept per player; older ones are pruned automatically.

If the target player is currently online, they are saved and disconnected
before the restore takes effect.  They must reconnect to use the restored
character.

See also: ACTIVITY, FINGER, PSTAT
~

65 TRAIL~
Syntax: trail <player>

TRAIL shows the last few rooms visited by the specified player, taken from
their session ring-buffer.  The buffer holds the TRAIL_LEN most recent
rooms and is updated every time the player moves.  Entries are shown
oldest-first with room vnum and name.  The buffer is cleared on login.

See also: FINGER, PSTAT
~

63 RESETPWD~
Syntax: resetpwd <player> <newpassword>

RESETPWD resets the password of an OFFLINE player to the specified new
password.  This is the "forgot password" rescue tool for when a player
cannot log in and contacts an immortal for help.

  resetpwd Bob K7p4N2xQ

Requirements and restrictions:
  - The target player must be OFFLINE.  If they are logged in, ask them
    to use the PASSWORD command themselves.
  - The new password must be at least 5 characters and must not contain
    the tilde character.
  - You cannot reset the password of a character above your own level.
  - Below level 70 you cannot reset a character at your own level either.
    Implementors are the exception: a level 70 may reset any other level 70,
    because there is no higher rank to ask when an implementor is locked out.
    That means every implementor can take over every other implementor's
    account, which is why each reset is announced and logged.
  - The reset event is logged and broadcast on WIZINFO, but the password
    argument is suppressed from command logs and snoop output.

The command rewrites only the hashed password field in the player save
file, leaving all other character data untouched.  ToC currently uses
traditional DES hashes, so only the first eight password bytes are effective.

See also: FINGER, TRAIL
~

60 NEWBIE~
Syntax: newbie
        newbie <player>

NEWBIE builds a starter kit and hands it over.  With no argument the pack
appears in your own hands; with a player name it is given to them, with a
message to the recipient and to everyone in the room.

The pack is a renamed container holding a mud school diploma, red potions,
food and berries, potions of sanctuary and extra healing, a water jug, and
an assortment of starter jewellery.  Its contents are defined in the server
rather than in an area file, so they are the same every time regardless of
what is loaded in the world.

If an item has been renumbered out of the world the command still hands over
the pack and warns you how many pieces were missing, rather than failing.

The gift is logged and announced on WIZINFO.

See also: LOAD, RESTORE, TRANSFER
~

63 PETRIFY~
Syntax: petrify <player> [duration]

PETRIFY applies a stone affect to a player, completely blocking all commands
for the duration (in ticks).  Default duration is 50 ticks.  Petrified
players cannot act at all.  Toggling petrify on an already-petrified player
removes the effect immediately.  The effect is saved across logins.

See also: FREEZE, STASIS
~

62 EMPOWER~
Syntax: empower <player>
        empower <player> <ticks>
        empower <player> perm
        empower <player> toggle

EMPOWER grants a player a full suite of divine buffs: sanctuary, haste,
fly, pass door, protection, regeneration, divine shielding, and large
boosts to all five stats plus hitroll, damroll, and armor class.

  empower bob           -- permanent (duration -1); default when no arg given
  empower bob 200       -- applies for 200 ticks; expires naturally
  empower bob perm      -- explicit permanent
  empower bob toggle    -- permanent; if target is already empowered,
                           removes all empower effects immediately instead

If the target is already empowered, a second empower call (any argument)
strips the effects and acts as a toggle-off regardless of duration given.

All effects use the 'empower' skill type and are removed atomically.

See also: TITANIC, RESTORE
~

62 TITANIC~
Syntax: titanic <player>
        titanic <player> <ticks>
        titanic <player> perm
        titanic <player> toggle

TITANIC grants a player a 500% bonus to maximum HP, mana, and move
(capped at +30000 per resource to prevent overflow), then heals them to
full immediately.  Also grants +50% bonus experience for the duration.

  titanic bob          -- permanent (duration -1); default when no arg given
  titanic bob 300      -- 300-tick duration
  titanic bob perm     -- explicit permanent
  titanic bob toggle   -- permanent; if already titanic, strips and
                          removes the boost instead

If the target is already under titanic, calling again strips it
regardless of arguments.  Health, mana, and move are clamped back to
new (reduced) max on removal.

Boosts are applied as affects under the 'titanic' skill type.

See also: EMPOWER, RESTORE
~

65 SUMMONEVENT~
Syntax: summonevent
        summonevent status

SUMMONEVENT manually spawns (or despawns) the current seasonal event boss
in Dresden's Oak Tree / Christmas Tree Square (room 2401).

  summonevent          -- if no boss is alive, spawn it; if alive, despawn it
  summonevent status   -- show active season and boss state without acting

The boss spawns automatically once every 48 hours during active seasonal
events (Hallows End: Oct 18-Nov 1; Winter Veil: Dec 16-Jan 6).  It
broadcasts a global shout on arrival, periodically yells taunts, and
auto-despawns after two real hours if not killed.

Event mobs:
  the Headless Horseman (vnum 29910) -- Hallows End boss, undead, level 52
  Father Winter          (vnum 29911) -- Winter Veil boss, human, level 50

Rare drops on kill:
  the Headless Horseman's Reaper (29920) -- two-handed vorpal sword
  the Horseman's cursed helm (29921)     -- powerful head armor
  Frostbite (29922)                      -- frost warhammer
  the Winter Veil mantle (29923)         -- powerful cloak armor
  a pumpkin-lantern charm (29924)        -- neck trinket (mana/move)
  a snowflake pendant (29925)            -- neck trinket (mana/hp)
  a piece of shadowed horseman's cake (29926) -- VERY RARE: +1 train on eat
  a slice of Winter Veil yule log (29927)     -- VERY RARE: +1 train on eat

This command requires Level 5 immortal or higher.

See also: TITANIC, EMPOWER, PETRIFY
~

65 FLING~
Syntax: fling <player>
        fling all

FLING hurls the target player (or all players) into the sky and drops
them in a completely random room somewhere in the world.  The target sees
an appropriately dramatic message and lands with a room description.

See also: TRANSFER, DRAG
~

69 GKICK~
Syntax: gkick

GKICK immediately disconnects all non-immortal players from the game.
Use for emergencies or immediately before scheduled reboots.

See also: REBOOT, SHUTDOWN, WIZLOCK
~

60 IMOTD~
Syntax: imotd

IMOTD displays the immortal message-of-the-day.

See also: MOTD
~

62 INVIS~
Syntax: invis [level]

INVIS makes you invisible to all players below the specified trust level
(default: your own level).  Use VISIBLE to reappear.

See also: WIZINVIS, HOLYLIGHT
~

68 ITRANS~
Syntax: itrans <player> [room]
        itrans all [room]

ITRANS performs a silent transfer - the target is moved without seeing
the usual transfer arrival or departure messages.  Room may be a vnum or
a player name for their current location.  Omit room to transfer to your
own position.

See also: TRANSFER, DRAG
~

62 JAIL~
Syntax: jail <player>

JAIL teleports the specified player to the jail room.

See also: TRANSFER, STASIS
~

64 KSOCK~
Syntax: ksock <descriptor>

KSOCK forcibly closes a network connection by descriptor number.
Use SOCKETS to view current descriptor numbers.

See also: SOCKETS, DUMP
~

68 MAXLOADS~
Syntax: maxloads list
        maxloads set <vnum> <count>
        maxloads pla <vnum>

MAXLOADS manages per-object maximum load counts.  'list' shows all
configured limits.  'set' assigns a max count for an object vnum.
'pla' shows current player-carried counts for a vnum.

See also: LOAD
~

67 NEWCORPSE~
Syntax: newcorpse <player>
        newcorpse <player> <corpse_number>

NEWCORPSE reconstructs a lost corpse for the specified player from their
saved corpse file.  Useful for restoring items lost to crashes or bugs.

See also: RESTORE
~

63 NOCHANNELS~
Syntax: nochannels <player>

NOCHANNELS prevents a player from using any global communication channel
(gossip, music, info, etc.).  Toggle again to restore access.

See also: MUTE, NOSHOUT, NOEMOTE
~

64 NOEMOTE~
Syntax: noemote <player>

NOEMOTE prevents a player from using the emote command.
Toggle again to restore.

See also: MUTE, NOCHANNELS
~

64 NOSHOUT~
Syntax: noshout <player>

NOSHOUT prevents a player from using the shout command.
Toggle again to restore.

See also: MUTE, NOCHANNELS
~

65 NOTELL~
Syntax: notell <player>

NOTELL stops one player sending tells, and typing it again lets them send
again.  Use it when somebody is pestering one person; MUTE takes away every
channel at once and is the heavier answer.

The player is told either way, and the change is announced on WIZINFO.

See also: MUTE, TELL, SILENCE
~

65 NOTITLE~
Syntax: notitle <player>

NOTITLE prevents a player from changing their own title.
Toggle again to allow title changes.

See also: SET
~

66 SLA~
Syntax: sla <player>

SLA is a silent version of SLAY.  The target is removed with no message
shown to the room.

See also: SLAY
~

62 WARN~
Syntax: warn <player> <message>

WARN sends a formal warning to the specified player and logs the action
to the system log.

See also: FREEZE, STASIS
~

67 WHINER~
Syntax: whiner <player>

WHINER toggles the WHINER communication flag on a player.  Flagged
players have their channel messages marked, identifying them as chronic
complainers.

See also: NOCHANNELS
~

62 WIZCHECK~
Syntax: wizcheck
        wizcheck <player>

WIZCHECK with no argument lists all immortals currently online with their
invisibility level and room vnum.  With a player name, shows extended
status info for that character.

See also: WIZLIST
~

0 $~

